window.HeartClearShadowsEnglish=["SHADOWS OF TRUTH\nA Memoir of Mind vs Body\nMathihul Akmal Mohamed Jabir\nCopyright © 2026 Mathihul Akmal Mohamed Jabir\nAll rights reserved.\nThis is a work of memoir. It reflects the author's\nrecollections of experiences over time. Some names and\nidentifying details have been changed to protect the\nprivacy of individuals.\nNo part of this book may be reproduced without written\npermission from the author, except brief quotations in\nreviews.\nISBN:","First Edition — 2026","CONTENTS\n1. The Boy Who Was Already Watching 5\n2. The Boat, the Tsunami, and the Boy Who Watched 12\n3. The Messenger of Ampara 21\n4. The Man on the Bicycle 29\n5. The Questions I Did Not Know I Was Carrying 40\n6. The Surah on the Flight 47\n7. The Fire in the Name 59\n8. The Things I Carried in Silence 71\n9. The Stillness My Father Never Named 78\n10. You Cannot Divide Her Day by Eight 89\n11. Colombo — The Pentium III and the Mat 97\n12. 02\n13. Beragala — Rosie and Freddie 105\n14. Jailani — The Midnight Mountain 113\n15. Oluvil — The Forgotten Plate 119\n16. Akkan 125\n17. Qatar Was the First Leaving 131\n18. Dubai Fed My Body 140\n19. The Bunk Bed and the Waterfall 150\n20. The Horse and the Rider 156\n21. Are You Akmal? 166\n22. The First Thing Prison Stripped Away 176\n23. Cell 13 184\n24. The Sound of Time 195\n25. The People Who Kept Me Human 204\n26. The First Book 211\n27. The Broken Pen and Three Papers 219\n28. The Awakening Songs 224\n29. The Eleven Rules 231\n30. Mike — Physics, English, and the Notebook That Outlived Him\n240\n31. The Sacred Mosque Inside the Head 248\n32. Forty Steps Across the Street 266\n33. The Door Opens Like a Womb 276\n34. Patience and the Rebuild 282\n12. Aachchi 102 Archie - The Hindu Aachchi","35. The First Room After Prison 291\n36. The Signal and the Television 301\n37. Kun — When the Word Becomes Real 308\n38. Breath and the Bluetooth 315\n39. What the Mind Sees 321\n40. The Three Minds and the Beggar 328\n41. Light Is Dark 336\n42. The Fire Is Cooking You Now 343\n43. One Day They Will Call You Human 352\n44. The Sound of Kun 357\n45. Man and Woman — The Form Love Takes 365\n46. Destiny Starts with Nothing 370\n47. One Side Is Enough 376\n48. Earn the Gratitude 380\n49. The Stone on the Road 385\n50. Before the Bloom 393\n51. The Teacher in the Small Body 397\n52. How to Grow a Child 402\n53. The Practice 410\n54. The Witnesses 419\n55. The Two Books 427\n56. The Speed of Stillness 433\n57. Why I Have Told You All of This 438\n58. A Witness to My Whole Time on Earth 447\n59. On the Making of This Book 454\n60. Explained in Detail 462\nPhotographs 484","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER\n1\nThe Boy Who Was Already Watching\nBefore I learned how to sit still, I knew how to\nmove.\nFor most of my early life, movement was my proof\nof being alive. Wake up. Work. Travel. Lift. Carry.\nEarn. Help. Go somewhere. Chase something. Come\nhome tired. Sleep. Begin again. The body sat at the\ncentre of everything I did. If you had asked me then\nwhether I was wasting my life, I would have said no —\nI was trying, I was not lazy, I was doing what my\nfamily and my country expected of an eldest son. But\nthere was another truth hidden under the first one. I was\nfeeding the body far more than I was feeding the mind.\nA person can be very busy and still be deeply asleep\ninside. That was me. Always moving, rarely watching.\nAnd yet, even then, some quiet part of me was\nalready watching. I just did not know its name. I would\nnot give it a name for another twenty-five years. The\nname would arrive in a small room in another country,","in a place I could not yet have imagined as a boy\nwalking the dust roads of Akkaraipattu. But the watcher\nwas there from the beginning. He was the one taking\nthe photograph of my childhood while the rest of me\nwas busy living it.\n* * *\nMy grandmother was one of my first teachers,\nthough she never sat me down to teach. She taught me\nby walking.\nI still remember the road we took together. The main\nstreet. The left turn past the old petrol station. The cows\nthat moved freely along the lane before the streets were\never paved and tidied. The small sharp thorns from\ncertain trees that pricked my legs as we walked to my\naunt's house. She would stop and remove them, one by\none, with the kind of patience that only certain people\ncarry without being taught it. That sort of care settles\ninto a child's body long before he has words for it. It\nteaches you something underneath language: that\ngentleness is not weakness, and that the people who\ntreat small things carefully are usually the same people\nwho treat human beings carefully later on.","The road smelled of dust, of warm tar in the\nafternoon, of cow dung dried by sun, of frying oil from\nthe corner shop. My grandmother walked through all of\nit as if patience itself had legs.\nOne day, walking beside her, I picked up a small\nstone and crept behind a cow. Some childish pride, I\nsuppose. I tried to throw the stone. The cow kicked\nback, hard, fast — and I felt the shame land in my chest\nbefore anyone said a word. No lecture was needed. My\nbody had already understood what my mind had not yet\nlearned to say: do not provoke what is innocent. The\nlesson went straight in, the way the deepest lessons\nalways do — without explanation. The cow walked on.\nThe grandmother walked on. The boy walked on,\nslightly different than before.\n* * *\nThere was a mango tree that stood between the\nhouses near my grandmother's place. It was wide and\ngenerous, its branches spreading toward the roof as\nthough it had grown on purpose for children to climb.\nMy brother and I climbed it onto the roof and played up\nthere as if danger were something that happened only to","other people. The roof was hot under our feet. The tiles\nwere old and dusty. The view from up there showed the\nsmall village below in a way that made it look like a\npostcard a stranger might send.\nOne afternoon my grandmother was sitting below\nthe tree, cutting betel nut with a sharp metal cutter. My\nbrother and I were being rough with each other, the way\nbrothers are when their energy is bigger than their\nwisdom. In one careless second, I threw the cutter. It\nstruck him near the left side of his face, and the blood\ncame at once. My mother ran out, frightened and angry\nin the same breath, and I ran — and hid for hours.\nI made my body small. I made my breath quiet. I\nmade myself smaller than the small space I was hiding\nin, as if becoming smaller could undo what my hand\nhad done. It is a strange thing, what a child learns while\nhiding. You learn that a single second can split a whole\nday into a before and an after. You learn that some\nthings, once done, cannot be taken back, no matter how\ncompletely you can disappear behind a wall.\nI did not know it then, but the boy who was hiding in\nthat small space was practising something. Decades","later, in a cell on another island, my body would\nremember how to make itself small inside a room it\ncould not leave. The hiding had begun long before the\nprison.\n* * *\nBehind our house there was a guava tree that leaned\ntoward the roof. One day my brother slipped from the\nedge of the roof and fell. At that exact moment, my\nmother happened to be walking out from the kitchen,\nand he fell directly onto her back.\nI was still up on the roof, frozen, shouting down at\nthem. I can still see her there, bent low under the force\nof it, carrying the whole weight of his fall with her own\nbody. People can say many things about a mother, and\nnot all of them kind. But beneath all of it, she is still\nyour mother. In that one moment I saw something I\ncould not explain — instinct, sacrifice, protection,\nperhaps even grace. I was not the one who got hurt. I\nwas the one who watched.\nAnd I have come to learn that sometimes it is the\nwitness who carries the longest memory of all.","* * *\nI want to add one small thing here, because the rest\nof this book depends on you understanding it.\nThose small early moments — the thorn removed,\nthe cow that kicked, the cutter that flew, the mother\nwho caught the fall — none of them seemed important\nwhile I was living them. They were just childhood. Just\nthe small accidents and lessons of being a boy in a small\nTamil-Muslim village on the eastern coast of Sri Lanka.\nI did not understand, in any of those moments, that I\nwas being shaped.\nIt is only now, more than twenty years later, sitting\nat a desk on a small island in the Mediterranean, that I\ncan see what was happening. Every one of those\nmoments was teaching the watcher in me how to watch.\nThe mango tree was teaching me about consequence.\nThe grandmother was teaching me about patience. The\ncow was teaching me about provocation. The cutter was\nteaching me about the unrecoverable second. My\nmother's back was teaching me about sacrifice.\nThe watcher was learning, but he had not yet been\nforced to sit still.","That would come later. Much later. In a place I had\nnot yet imagined, on an island whose name I had not yet\nheard, inside walls that were already being built for a\nman who did not yet know he would arrive at them. The\nmango tree did not know it was preparing me. The\ngrandmother did not know it. My mother did not know\nit. I did not know it. But the watcher knew. The watcher\nalways knew.\nThe mango tree where the watcher first learned to\nclimb.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER\n2\nThe Boat, the Tsunami, and the Boy\nWho Watched\nThere are two beaches I want to write about in this\nchapter. They happened in different years, in different\nways, with different consequences. But in my memory\nthey sit beside each other, because both of them taught\nme something about how the body responds when the\nworld suddenly becomes larger than the body can hold.\n* * *\nThe first beach. I was in eighth grade. A Friday\nafternoon, after school and after Jumma prayer, when\nthe air over the eastern coast carries that particular\nfreedom only Fridays have in a small Muslim town.\nTwo friends and I went to the beach. One of them had\nfamily land near the water. We packed food, took our\nbicycles — mine was a green mountain bike, the kind a\nboy is proud to be seen on — and rode out together.","There was nowhere proper to lean the bikes when we\ngot there, so we parked all three of them in a triangle,\neach holding the others upright. We sat beneath a small\nwooden sailing boat that someone had pulled up onto\nthe sand. The water had eaten the soil away around it,\nso the ground dropped sharply behind us. From the\nroad, you could not be seen at all. We were inside a\nsmall private world of three boys, one boat, and the\nopen sea.\nMy friends wanted to try bidi. My grandfather\nMohammed Ismail — the man everyone called Farooq\n— manufactured them. The small hand-rolled cigarettes\nthat smelled of tobacco and home and his fingers. I had\ngrown up around them without ever touching one. But\ncuriosity in a group moves faster than any father's\nexample. I stole a small amount from home. I packed\nrice husks loose into my right pocket as filler. We tried\nto light the bidi. It would not catch. The wind off the\nwater kept blowing out every flame we struck.\nI climbed under the boat to get out of the wind. I\nstruck a match. It lit. I saw the smoke. Then I threw the\nmatchstick away, casually, the way a boy does when he\nbelieves a flame has gone out simply because he has","stopped looking at it. I came back out and sat facing the\nocean. The wind came off the water and pushed into our\nfaces, which meant anything behind us — smell, sound,\nheat — disappeared into the air above the boat, out of\nour reach.\nWe did not see what was happening behind our own\nbacks.\n* * *\nAfter some time, I turned around.\nThe boat was on fire. Not a small fire. A full, rising\nfire, with black smoke climbing in a thick mushroom\nshape into the sky. Before the fear arrived, I remember\nthinking, almost calmly, that it looked nuclear. It is\nstrange what a child's mind reaches for in the first\nsecond of disaster. Not panic. A comparison. As if the\nbrain needs to file the image somewhere before it can\nbegin to be afraid.\nRunning toward the road, I saw a man in a white\nshirt and a white cap. He lived behind the boys' school,\na relative of Jasil Kaka's shop. He had not spoken since\nbirth. He was a man without words. And he was\nscreaming. The man who had never spoken in his life","was screaming. I told my friends: look back. Run. But\nbeach sand does not let you run the way fear needs you\nto run. The feet sink. The body wants speed and the\nsand refuses it. People came from everywhere, from\nevery direction, the way people always come when\nsmoke goes up.\nMy friend spoke first — the quickest thinker of the\nthree of us. He said we had just arrived. He said we had\nseen strangers walking nearby. He said we did not know\nwhat had happened. He said we had run toward the fire\nlike everyone else. They said: check your pockets. I had\nalready thrown the matchbox away. My hands had\nacted before my mind had decided. The pockets were\nempty.\nThen an old man stepped forward. White shirt.\nWhite cap. The calm of a certain kind of village elder\n— not the calm of gentleness, but the calm of authority.\nHe looked at me, and only at me.\nYou are Farooq Bedi's grandson, aren't you?\nYes.\nGo home, he said. I will come.","And then, to the others around us: leave these\nchildren.\nI ran home, lay down on the bed fully dressed, and\nwaited for the sound of the gate. My friend's mother\ncame first, crying, whispering at the door to my mother.\nMy mother came to me with that whisper mothers use\nwhen they are trying to protect and discipline you at the\nsame time. The voice that says I know, and the world\nmust not know. I lay still. I felt guilty in the way guilt\nsits in the chest of a child even when the body is not\nmoving.\nThe old man never came. To this day, the people\nthere still believe I did not do it. But I did. I am writing\nit here now because I cannot leave a burning boat out of\nmy own story.\n* * *\nThe second beach. December 26, 2004. I was\nmoving from eighth grade into ninth.\nThat Sunday morning I woke at seven and decided to\nmake paratha. One kilogram of wheat flour, mixed and\nrested, left to wait at my grandmother's house. The plan\nin my head was clear and simple — science class first,","then home, then paratha. A boy's day, made entirely of\nfood and study, with no idea that the sea had its own\nplans.\nOur principal was Haron sir — the kind of man who\ncould make a whole corridor go silent just by appearing\nat the far end of it. He had begun, around that time,\ngiving evening science classes to our batch outside\nschool hours. The first time he walked into the room as\nour class teacher, I remember bracing for the strictness.\nBut he was different there. Funny. Calm. He said\nsomething that stayed with me: school is school, outside\nschool is outside school. I saw, for the first time in my\nlife, that strictness and warmth are not opposites. They\ncan live inside the same man.\nI moved from the back of the class to the first bench\nafter that day. I wanted to be near a person who carried\nthat combination, because something in me had always\nsuspected it was possible and had not yet seen it\nconfirmed.\nThat morning we arrived early. We were sitting in\nour places, ready. Then the manager came running into\nthe room — not walking, running, the way a man runs","when something is wrong that he has no words for yet.\nClass is cancelled. Go home. Do not go anywhere\nelse. The water is coming. The sea is coming.\n* * *\nWe were delighted. Free Sunday morning, no class,\nthe whole day in front of us. Freedom.\nI walked out toward home. Something — I cannot\nexplain it, even now — made me turn around and look\nback. People were running. Many people. Some in\nnighties, some in whatever they had woken up wearing,\nsome without proper clothes at all, screaming, moving\naway from the water. I laughed. The young mind, still\ninside its own joke, said: how can the sea be coming?\nI went home. I told my mother the sea was coming. I\nmade my paratha. The water came up to the main street\nand stopped. It did not reach our house. Inside, my\naunts, my grandmother, my grandfather all ate the bread\nI had made before I knew anyone would need it.\nI climbed the big mango tree to see for myself what\nthe sea had done. My grandfather Mohammed Ismail\nshouted at me to come down at once. He was always","watching. We left soon after for Kudiyiruppu — higher\nground, away from the water. The rains came heavy for\nsix days. The radio stayed on. The mud stayed. The\nstories began to arrive from the coastal villages, one\nafter another, each one worse than the last.\nWe stayed alive. That is the part I did not understand\nthen and barely understand now. Tens of thousands did\nnot. The same sea that took whole villages stopped at\nour main street. There is no theology I can make from\nthat. I will not pretend otherwise. I only know that I\nmade paratha for my family on a morning when the\nworld was ending for other families, and the bread was\neaten, and we lived.\n* * *\nThe boy who burned the boat and the boy who made\nthe paratha are the same boy.\nHe carried both events inside him without knowing\nwhat to do with either of them. The watcher had now\nseen two large things — one that he had caused with his\nown hand, and one that had passed over him without his\npermission. Both had taught him the same lesson, in\ndifferent forms: that a single small action, or a single","second of being in the wrong place, can change\neverything. That control is mostly an illusion. That\nwhat saves you and what destroys you may both arrive\non the same kind of morning.\nTwenty years later, on another beach, in another\ncountry, a wave of a different kind would arrive in my\nlife. It would not be made of water. It would not kill\nanyone. But it would be just as sudden, and it would\ntake just as much from me. And the boy who had\nwatched the burning boat and watched the sea swallow\nhis coast would be the man inside the body when that\nthird wave arrived.\nBut that is a later chapter. For now, we are still on\nthe beach. The boat is still burning. The sea has only\njust receded.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER\n3\nThe Messenger of Ampara\nIn 1999, the air at the Ampara bus depot was thick\nwith diesel and the weight of a departure that would last\ntwenty-six years.\nBack then, a father leaving for the Gulf was a rare\nand staggering sacrifice. My father, Mohamed Jabir,\nwas heading toward a life split between Saudi Arabia\nand Qatar, eventually spending his final decade at the\nQatari airport. I was eight years old, born in 1991. For\nmost of my childhood until that morning, I had been the\none left behind — the boy at the gate, the boy waving,\nthe boy looking at a man's back disappearing into a bus.\nBut that morning, before he boarded, he turned\naround. He looked at me. And he handed me ninety\nrupees.\nGive this to your mother, he said.\nIt was not a gift for a child. It was a mission for a\nsuccessor. He did not say it that way. He just said the","words. But I felt the change land in my hands along\nwith the notes. I carried those ninety rupees home like a\nsacred trust. When I handed them to my mother, her\neyes were already moving with the cold mathematics of\nsurvival. The milk powder costs this much. The\nkerosene costs this much. The rice. The school. The\nmedicine if anyone fell sick.\nNinety rupees became more than currency in that\nmoment. They became the price of our nourishment,\nweighed against everything else that nourishment had to\nbe set beside.\nI became something older than eight that day. I do\nnot say that for effect. It is simply true. The boy who\nhad carried the money grew, in one afternoon, into the\nboy who understood what money was — not coins to\nspend on ice cream, but lines on a worried list inside his\nmother's head.\n* * *\nFrom then on, I started walking to the Old Market\nalone.\nSri Lanka was at war in those years. The path\nrequired care. There were checkpoints, soldiers, the","kind of nervous adult faces that children learn to read\nvery early. I was eight, and I was walking alone through\na town that was not yet at peace, carrying a small\namount of money and the entire weight of a mission my\nfather had handed me at a bus depot.\nTo survive the walk, I used a ghost.\nMy father had taken me on his motorbike many\ntimes before he left. I would sit behind him, gripping\nhis waist, my chin near his back, watching the road over\nhis shoulder. The familiar smell of his shirt. The\nwarmth of him. The sense of being held by speed. So\nnow, when I had to walk alone, I closed my eyes for\none second at the gate of the house, and I visualised his\nback. I imagined I was still balanced behind him on that\nmotorbike. I imagined he was still in front of me,\nblocking the wind, blocking the road.\nAnd then I opened my eyes and followed that\nimagined back through the dust and the heat and the\ncareful streets to the market and home again.\nI did not have the luxury of missing him with my\nheart. I had to honour him with my discipline. Missing\nhim would have made me sit down and cry. Discipline","made me walk. So I walked. The body did the errand.\nThe mind held the ghost. And somewhere in those small\ndaily walks, between the bus depot and the market and\nhome, the watcher in me was already being trained —\nnot as a philosopher, but as a small boy who had\nlearned that you can carry someone with you even when\nthey are not there.\nLong after my father came home from the Gulf for\nthe last time, I would still know how to do this. I would\nstill know how to walk through hard places by closing\nmy eyes and finding a back to follow. The lesson did\nnot stay only with my father. The lesson became a\nmethod. The lesson became, eventually, a way of\nsurviving rooms much smaller than the streets of\nAmpara.\n* * *\nMy grandfather named me Akmal.\nAkmal — in its Arabic root, it carries the sense of\ncompleteness, of being whole, of reaching the fullness\nof a thing. He gave me that name because that was the\nprayer he had for me. That I would become complete.\nThat I would not be a half-thing, a fragment, an","unfinished man. He did not say all of that out loud.\nGrandfathers in our culture rarely explain the weight of\nthe gifts they give. But the name was the prayer.\nAnd then a clerical error on my passport, somewhere\nalong the way, decided the world would call me Jabir\ninstead. A rhythm slip in the printing. So I carry both\nnames now. At home, in the streets of my village, in the\nmouths of my mother and my brother and my sisters\nand my old teachers — Akmal. In the documents that\nmove me across borders — Jabir.\nTwo names. Two registers. The inside and the\noutside of one man.\nI have not corrected the document. Maybe one day I\nwill. Maybe I will not. I have made my peace with\ncarrying both. Akmal is the name my grandfather\nprayed for me. Jabir is the name a printer mistyped.\nBoth are with me. The deeper one was given by love.\nThe other one was given by accident. I keep them both\nbecause life turned out to be made of both — what was\nprayed for, and what was mistyped.\nYears later, in a cell on another island, an officer\nwould knock at a door and call out the name on my","passport. Jabir, tomorrow, eleven o'clock. The world\nwould still be calling me by the printer's mistake on the\nday I most needed my grandfather's prayer. I would\nunderstand, in that small moment, that the name on the\npaper does not save you. Only the name your soul has\nbeen given to live up to does.\nI am my father's reflection. Same skin tone. Same\nbirth month. The same large patience that can\nsometimes become a heaviness if it is not watched. The\nsame body, almost the same hands. When I look in the\nmirror in the morning, I see a younger version of the\nman who handed me ninety rupees at a bus depot in\n1999.\n* * *\nMy father worked twenty-six years away so that we\ncould grow. Twenty-six years is not a phrase. It is the\nlength of a life.\nHe left in 1999 and the years he spent abroad —\nSaudi, then Qatar, the airport, the maintenance, the long\nshifts, the small accommodations, the food eaten alone,\nthe calls home — added up to that. Twenty-six. From\nthe year I was eight until the year I am writing this. He","paid for our growing with the slow expenditure of his\nown years on the other side of an ocean.\nMy mother carried the house for the same twenty-six\nyears. From 2000 to 2026, she raised eight children —\nsix girls, my younger brother, and me — building a life\nfrom very little, without complaint that I ever heard her\nspeak out loud. She fed us. She dressed us. She taught\nus. She married off my sisters one by one. She worried\nfor us. She prayed for us. And she held the whole\nstructure together while her husband was on the other\nside of the Indian Ocean keeping the structure\nfinancially alive.\nI did not understand that weight when I was eight,\nwalking to the market. I thought I was being a man\nbecause my father had given me a job. I did not see\nwhat my mother was doing every day around me,\nbecause she was doing it so steadily that it looked, to a\nchild, like the natural background of life.\nI understand it now.\nThe messenger of Ampara was a small boy with\nninety rupees. The real carrier of our family for\ntwenty-six years was a quiet woman in a house, doing","what no one ever named out loud as work. There is a\nchapter much later in this book that I owe her in\nparticular. I am not ready to write it yet, even now, even\nat this point in the book. But I will. When the time is\nright, the chapter will come. For now, here at the\nbeginning, all I can do is name her, and bow my head,\nand continue walking the road my father started me on\nwith the small handful of notes.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER\n4\nThe Man on the Bicycle\nMy grandfather Mohammed Ismail — Farooq to\nthose who knew him — lived until he was eighty-two.\nHe did not just die. He lived until his last breath and\nthen finished. There is a difference between a man who\nfades and a man who completes himself, and he was the\nsecond kind. The candle did not go out in pieces. It\nburned all the way down, and then it was done.\nEvery morning he woke before the day had any\nopinions about him. Before the village was awake.\nBefore the muezzin called. He took his bicycle — the\nsame old frame, the same handlebars — and he rode\nfifteen kilometres from Akkaraipattu to the other side,\ndistributing the cigarettes and bidi he manufactured\nhimself, collecting what was owed to him, moving\nthrough his route with a consistency that never required\na machine, or a manager, or an alarm. The bicycle was\nhis office. His legs were his engine. His memory was\nhis accounting system.","He came home around ten or eleven in the morning.\nHe slept at eight-thirty in the evening, no later. He ate at\nseven-thirty. He controlled the house the way old men\ndo — not cruelly, but with a love that looked like\nstrictness because that was the only language his\ngeneration had for authority. He smoked his own\ncigarettes. Of course he did. He manufactured them,\nand he tasted them himself to check the quality. That\nwas his life's work, the way another man might check\nthe salt in a soup he had cooked.\nHe did not trust banks. He did not trust doctors. He\ndid not trust the new machines. He kept his money in a\nsafe in the house and dealt only in cash. He did not go\nto the hospital because, until eighty-two, he was\nhealthy. The body had been built by fifteen kilometres a\nday for decades, and it did what bodies do when they\nare used well: it lasted.\n* * *\nI used to sleep at my grandmother's house, beside\nher, on a mat or on the bed depending on the night. My\ngrandfather always left the house before I woke.\nMosque first — the early prayer — then his bicycle","route. But when he came back, he brought something\nwith him from the shop.\nPaalappam. The soft hopper with coconut cream\npoured on top and crystal sugar sprinkled over it. Still\nslightly warm from the pan it had been cooked in.\nI was excited to wake up because of that paalappam.\nNot because of school, not because of duty, not because\nof religion — because of that bread, that cream, that\nsugar. That is what he gave me. He did not give me a\nlecture on discipline. He gave me a reason to open my\neyes before the day began. That is how you grow a\nchild. You do not tell them why to live. You give them\nsomething worth waking up for, and let the rest follow.\n* * *\nThere is a memory I carry from the deepest part of\nsleep. Three in the morning. I am not fully awake, not\nfully gone. The room is dark. The house is quiet. And I\nhear his door open. The hinge has a small particular\nsound that belonged to him. Then the cough — his\nparticular cough, the cough that came with the first\ncigarette of the early morning.","I counted his footsteps in my half-sleep. I knew the\nnumber of steps from his door to the toilet. I heard him\ngo. I heard the strike of the match. I heard him smoke. I\nheard another cough. And then I heard the footsteps\nreturn to his room.\nThat cough told me he was okay.\nA boy in deep sleep, monitoring his grandfather's\nbreathing through the sounds of a coughing man in the\ndark. I did not know I was doing it. The body did it on\nits own. That is love before it has a name. It is one\norganism listening for another organism, before\nlanguage, before duty, before religion. Just the ear,\nattached to the chest, attached to a small boy who\nneeded his grandfather to be alive.\nYears later, in another country, in a small room I\ncould not leave, I would learn that I had been trained to\nlisten at night by an old man who did not know he was\ntraining me. The ear that had once tracked a\ngrandfather's footsteps in the dark would learn to track\nkeys, bells, doors, and the call to the phone. The teacher\nhad been the grandfather. The classroom had been the\ndark. The graduation would not come until much later,","in a place neither of us could yet imagine.\nDuring festival time, when the fireworks began in\nthe streets, my grandfather would come running toward\nus — not to celebrate, but to stop us. He could not bear\nthe sound. Sharp, sudden noises hurt him in some way\nthe rest of us could not see. And now, when certain\nsounds disturb me — a slammed door, an unexpected\nbang, a particular sharpness — I recognise him. I carry\nhis sensitivity without ever having chosen it. The\nbloodline does not only pass down what we want. It\npasses down what was true.\n* * *\nWhen I was old enough to start going to the mosque\nwith him, I noticed something. He always sat at the\nback. As far from the front as a man could sit and still\nbe inside the building.\nPeople might say he sat there to leave early, to slip\nout before the prayer ended, to avoid the press of the\ncrowd. But I know the real reason. I know it now in a\nway I could not have known it then.\nIt was guilt. Not his own — the guilt society had\npressed into him over years. He sold cigarettes. That","was their judgment. So he carried their judgment with\nhim into the house of God and sat at the furthest point\nfrom the front, as though he had already decided, on\ntheir behalf, that he did not belong in the row with the\nmore respectable men.\nHe never stopped going. That is what matters. He\njust went quietly, to the back, and prayed alone. The\nvillage's opinion did not stop him from God. It only\nstopped him from the front row.\nThat is the cruelty of judgment when it dresses itself\nin religion. It does not actually drive a man away from\nhis faith. It just makes him pray smaller. It pushes him\nto the back of the room where his faith becomes a\nprivate, lonely thing instead of a shared one. He prayed\nin the same building, with the same words, to the same\nGod, with the same forehead touching the same kind of\nground. And he did it from the back, because the village\nhad not given him permission to sit anywhere else.\nI have thought about that back row many times.\nThere is more honesty in a man who keeps coming to a\nbuilding that does not fully welcome him than in many\nof the men in the front.","* * *\nHis shirts had two pockets. One on the outside,\nvisible to everyone, where he kept small things —\nreceipts, a folded handkerchief. And one on the inside,\nhidden, sewn into the lining, where he kept his money.\nFolded notes — twenties, thirties, fifties. The inside\npocket was his bank.\nI knew about that pocket. Every boy in that house\neventually learned about that pocket.\nMe and my friends wanted small money. Ice cream\nfrom the corner shop. Snacks at school. Things\neight-year-olds want with the urgency only\neight-year-olds can produce. So I stole. Not once.\nMultiple times. Never more than fifty rupees. I would\nslide my hand into the inside pocket when his shirt was\nhanging, take a folded note, and run. I bought ice\ncream. I was generous with what was not mine to give\n— sharing it with my friends, pretending to be the boy\nwith the money, enjoying for a few minutes a status I\nhad not earned.\nOne day he noticed. He counted his money and the\ncount was wrong. He went to my grandmother and","fought with her, accused her of taking it. My mother\nheard the argument and understood immediately.\nWhich pocket. Which house. Which boy. She knew,\nbecause mothers always know, even when grandfathers\ndo not. She came to me and she beat me. She did not\nneed to ask. She told me what I had done as though she\nhad watched me do it.\nBut my grandfather never found out it was me. He\nforgave my grandmother without ever knowing who the\nreal thief was. He let it go. And I carried the guilt\nquietly inside, the way I carried the matchbox at the\nbeach. Hidden. Unconfessed.\nThe boy with the inside pocket of his own — the\npocket of his chest — was already collecting small\nthefts that no one else would ever know about. Maybe\nthis book is the confession. Maybe writing it down in\nfront of strangers is the only way to finally place those\nsmall notes back into a hand that can no longer receive\nthem. Real generosity, I learned later, comes from what\nis yours. Everything else is just the practice of wanting\nto give before you have learned how. I wanted to be\ngenerous with my friends. I had not earned the means.\nSo I took it. And the man who had earned it, who had","ridden fifteen kilometres a day for decades to fold those\nnotes into his inside pocket, never knew it was me.\n* * *\nHe died in 2016 while I was in Qatar.\nThat was the first time I cried as a grown man. I was\nin the kitchen of the small accommodation, on a video\ncall when I learned he was gone, and the body did what\nthe body does when a witness inside it loses one of its\nfirst reasons to wake up. The paalappam stopped that\nday. The cough at three in the morning stopped that\nday. The man on the bicycle finished his route.\nWherever he is now — and I believe he is\nsomewhere — I want him to know that the small notes\nhave been confessed. The boy who took them grew up.\nAnd the grandson he never quite suspected became the\nfirst man in his line to leave a book.\nI will write more about that bloodline soon. There is\na man at the top of that line — my great-grandfather,\nShehu Muhammad — who carried something I am only\nnow beginning to understand. He had a name the village\ngave him that I did not take seriously as a boy. Neruppu\nMasthan. The fire saint. I thought it was an old story. I","thought it was the kind of thing villages call elders to\nflatter them. Then I went to prison, and I sat very still\nfor many days, and I began to understand that the name\nwas not a flattery. The name was a description. And the\ndescription was something I was carrying in my own\nblood without knowing it.\nBut that chapter is for later. For now, the bicycle\nstops. The route is finished. The man on the bicycle has\ngone home for the last time. And the boy he loved, in\nanother country, kneels in a kitchen and cries for the\nfirst time as a grown man.","Grandfather Muhammad Ismail (Farooq Bedi) — the\nman on the bicycle.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER\n5\nThe Questions I Did Not Know I Was\nCarrying\nI was always a person who had questions inside me.\nNot always out loud — most of the time I kept them to\nmyself, turning them over privately until something\nclose to an answer rose up to meet them.\nIf someone told me not to do a thing, I would ask,\nquietly inside, why? If I believed something, I wanted\nto understand it, not simply carry it. Belief without\nunderstanding always felt to me like wearing a coat that\ndid not fit. You could keep it on, you could even tell\nyourself it was warm, but a part of you always knew it\nwas borrowed.\nI grew up in Sri Lanka, in a Tamil-speaking Muslim\nfamily, in a country where Hindus and Muslims lived\nclose enough to each other that the smell of one\nneighbour's cooking could travel into another\nneighbour's house without permission. The temples and\nthe mosques shared the same streets. The festivals","shared the same nights. That mixture shaped my mind\nearly. I was never able to see religion as a single, sealed\nroom. I saw it the way I saw the road outside my\ngrandmother's house — a place where many feet were\nwalking, in many directions, toward many things that,\nin the end, often turned out to be the same thing.\n* * *\nOne question stayed with me for a long time, longer\nthan I realised.\nHow can people understand the Qur'an if they only\nrecite it in Arabic without grasping its meaning? I was\nnot rebelling against the language. Arabic is the door —\nthe original sound, the original rhythm, the way the\nwords first entered the world. I respected the door. But\na door, however beautiful, is not the room. The room is\nthe meaning. And for me, the key to the room was\nTamil and English, because those were the languages\nmy mind actually lived inside. I wanted to walk through\nthe door, not stand admiring it from the outside while\npretending I had been in.\nI noticed, even as a child, that people around me\ncould finish the entire Qur'an by reciting it without ever","pausing to ask what a single sentence meant. They had\nmemorised the surface and called it faith. I did not think\nthey were bad people. They were doing what they had\nbeen taught. But something in me knew, even then, that\nI could not survive on that. I needed the meaning. I\nneeded to feel the words enter me with their weight and\ntheir argument intact, not just with their sound.\nWhen I was small, in our Madrasa, I do not\nremember the teacher's name clearly enough to write it\nas fact. I remember him as an Ustad, a Maulvi, a short\nman with a long beard, a Hifzul teacher who knew the\nverses well and could correct any small mistake in our\nrecitation with the patience of a man who had spent\nyears listening to children mispronounce the same lines.\nHe taught us how to say the words. He did not teach us,\nin any deep way, what the words meant. That was not\nonly his limitation. It was the system. The system was\nmemorisation first. Understanding, if it came at all,\ncame later, and usually only to the small handful of\nboys who went further.\nI did not yet go further. I was a boy. I memorised.\nBut the question — what does this actually mean? —\nnever left me. It just got pushed underneath the other","questions of a boy's life, and waited.\n* * *\nTwo grandfathers shaped this question in me,\nwithout ever knowing they were doing it.\nMy mother's father — Mohammed Ismail, the man\neveryone called Farooq — sold cigarettes and bidi for\nhis living, and so he sat at the back of the mosque out of\nguilt. Not God's guilt. Society's guilt. The world had\ndecided that a man who made his livelihood from\ntobacco belonged at the back row, far from the prayer\nmat, far from the eyes of the more respectable men. He\nnever argued with that judgment. He simply accepted\nhis seat at the back, and he kept going, every day, for\nyears, because his faith did not need a front row to\nsurvive. He believed in God privately, completely,\nwithout needing the village to confirm it for him.\nMy father's father was different in another way. His\nname was Muhammad Ibrahim. He read the Qur'an\nevery single day. He wore white, clean, disciplined\nclothes. He had the dark mark of prayer pressed into his\nforehead from years of placing his head on the ground.\nHe was respected as a teacher of Islam. From the","outside, he was the image of a religious man.\nAnd yet, inside the family memory, there was\nhardness. According to what I observed over many\nyears, he did not truly forgive or come properly toward\nhis own son — my father, Jabir.\nI am not writing this to make him a villain. He was\nmy elder. He was part of my blood. He carried\nknowledge, discipline, and form. But the form did not\nalways become softness inside the house.\nThat contradiction set itself inside me like a stone in\na shoe. The real contrast was not Ismail good, Ibrahim\nbad. The real contrast was that society judged Ismail\noutwardly, but he carried private sincerity; and society\nrespected Ibrahim outwardly, but inside the family there\nwas hardness.\nGod placed two paths in front of me. One path\nshowed me faith without display. One path showed me\ndisplay without enough softness.\nLong before I had the words for what I was asking,\nmy own bloodline was teaching me that form and fire\nare not the same thing. You can have all of one and not\nenough of the other. And the world will keep clapping","for the form.\n* * *\nSo I carried this quietly. I prayed sometimes, missed\nprayers sometimes, fasted in Ramadan, avoided the\nthings I had been told to avoid, and tried, in my own\nway, to be a decent man.\nBut I was still feeding the body far more than the\nmind. I went to the gym. I learned my work. I earned\nmy salary. I built friendships and chased relationships\nand lifted weights and watched my muscles grow, and\ncalled all of that progress. And in a way it was — but\nonly in one direction. The other direction, the inner one,\nwas being neglected.\nI lived inside contradiction without resolving it.\nBelief and doubt. Discipline and restlessness.\nGenerosity and confusion. A boy who watched\neverything and still understood almost nothing about\nhimself.\nThe version of me before the four walls was not\nempty, and he was not fake. He was real. He cared. He\ntried. But he was unfinished. And the questions I did\nnot know I was carrying — Why do religious men carry","ego? Why do people judged by society sometimes carry\nprivate mercy? What is the meaning underneath the\nwords I had been reciting since I was a small boy in\nfront of the Ustad in the Madrasa? — those questions\nwere waiting, patient as stones, for a place quiet enough\nto be heard.\nThat place would not be the mosque. It would not be\nthe gym. It would not be the cafeteria of a theme park in\nAbu Dhabi. It would not be the offices of any of the\ncompanies I worked for. It would be a small room on an\nisland whose name I did not yet know how to\npronounce, with a door that locked from the outside,\nand a window narrow enough that the wind only came\nthrough at certain angles. But I did not know that yet.\nThe questions did. The questions were already on their\nway to the room. I was the one who had to be carried\nthere.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER\n6\nThe Surah on the Flight\nBefore I understood what Surah Al-Baqarah meant, I\ncarried it in my body. It entered me the hard way, the\nway most sacred things enter a child — through cold\nwater on the face, through standing in the dark in a\nblanket, through the sleepy resistance of a boy who\nwould rather be anywhere else.\nThere was a Maulvi behind my home in those years,\nand that is where the recitation happened. Long before\ndawn. The line of children would form outside the toilet\nblock, half-asleep, waiting our turn for ablution, leaning\nagainst the wall, sometimes sleeping standing up. The\nwater that touched our faces was not warm. It did not\nask whether we were ready. It just arrived, and the body\nwoke up before the mind could complain.\n* * *\nMy father had already left for the Gulf by then. The\nyear was 1999. I was eight years old. The boy who had","been handed ninety rupees at the Ampara bus depot was\nnow growing up in a house where the father's chair was\nempty most months of the year. So it was not my father\nwho walked me to the Madrasa in those early dawn\nhours.\nIt was my grandfather Mohammed Ismail — Farooq\n— the cigarette-seller, the man who sat at the back of\nthe mosque out of guilt, the man whose shirts had two\npockets. He woke before dawn and walked me through\nthe darkness to the place where the words of God were\nbeing passed into small mouths. I did not appreciate it\nthen. I was small, barely able to keep my eyes open. I\nwas resentful of the hour, resentful of the cold, resentful\nof the long sit. But he brought me anyway. He did not\nlecture me about why. He simply showed up at my\nshoulder in the dark and walked, and so I walked too.\nYears later, when I was older — somewhere around\nthe eighth standard, when I had outgrown the early\nMadrasa — I was taken again into a different kind of\nlearning. By then my father had been gone for several\nyears. He did not attend that Madrasa. He did not know\nthat world directly. My father, Jabir, was born and\nraised in Ninthavur / Nindavur, and later moved about","fifteen kilometres to Akkaraipattu with almost nothing,\nnot even land, because of his love for my mother. I was\nborn and raised in Akkaraipattu. The Madrasa world\nbelonged to my childhood there, mostly connected to\nmy mother's side and to the people who were present\nwhile my father was abroad.\nOne of the first meaningful Islam teachers in my life\nwas Musammil Alim. He was my friend's father, and in\nthose years he became a guidance figure for me in a\nway I did not understand at the time. He was not an\narrogant man. He was humble, with a small white\nbeard, a net-covered cap, and white Muslim clothing —\noften a full-sleeve shirt and a sarong. Later, because he\ntaught in school at Kudiyiruppu, society and school\npressure pushed him toward trousers. But in my\nmemory he still stands in that simple white clothing,\ncarrying a quiet dignity.\nMusammil Alim taught Arabic through Tamil\nguidance and life meaning. He taught the early letters\nand sounds — Alif, Lam, Meem; Alif, Ba, Ta. He did\nnot only teach sound. Through him, Arabic started\ntouching Tamil, behaviour, guidance, and life. The\nletters were not floating in the air. They were being","placed into a boy's mouth, a boy's ear, a boy's body, and\nslowly into a boy's character.\nSometimes, when I wanted to escape the last ten\nminutes of reading or recitation, he would send me\noutside. There was a big mango tree near the house. He\nwould tell me to stand under it, collect the fallen leaves,\ncount ten leaves, and then go home.\nAs a child, I was happy. Ten leaves were easier than\nten lines. Ten leaves did not require the tongue to\nstruggle with Arabic sounds. But even then, a small ego\nlived inside me. Why should I clean his house? Why\nshould I pick up leaves for him? I did not say it, but the\nfeeling was there.\nOnly later did I understand that the lesson was not\nonly in the page. The Qur'an was training the tongue.\nThe mango tree was training the ego. To bend, to pick\nup what had fallen, to count slowly, to do a small task\nwithout being praised — that too was education.\nToday some parents might see such a thing\ndifferently. They might think it is humiliation or abuse,\nand sometimes parents are right to be careful because\nteachers can be wrong too. A child must be protected","from cruelty. But in this memory, from this man, I did\nnot receive cruelty. I received simplicity, discipline, and\nhumility.\nI was not always obedient. While reciting Arabic\nwithout understanding, I sometimes secretly turned\npages or skipped ahead when the teacher looked away,\npretending I had completed Surahs or tasks I had not\ntruly completed. That was the child in me — clever,\nafraid, resistant, and hungry to finish without really\nreceiving. I am not proud of it, but it is true. Even then,\nthe body wanted to escape what the mind had not yet\nunderstood.\nThere is another memory from that time. One\nafternoon, around 2:30 pm, I heard a thin, fearful sound\nfrom the boys' school side. I did not clearly see what\nhappened, but I heard crying, surrender, anger, and the\nsound of a belt. A father was forcing his son to attend\nMadrasa to learn Alif and Qur'an recitation in Arabic. I\nwill protect him here as S.\nS, if you ever read this, even if you forgot it, I\nremember. I heard your pain. Your father's method was\nwrong. Fear is not guidance. A belt cannot place","understanding into a child's heart. But maybe buried\ninside that wrong method was his broken wish that you\nwould learn. If you are ready one day, do not carry\nhatred forever. Release what can be released, and keep\nonly the lesson that helps you live.\nLater I heard another story about Musammil Alim.\nWhile he was teaching at school, a student from another\nclassroom threw a shoe and it landed near him, or\nbehind him. I remember feeling pain when I heard it.\nHe was a teacher. He was a human being. Whatever his\nlimits, he did not deserve that humiliation.\nA teacher can carry wisdom and limitation. A father\ncan carry love and violence. A religious man can carry\nego. A cigarette-seller can carry sincerity. A human\nbeing cannot be judged only by one outer label. This,\ntoo, was part of what the Madrasa years were teaching\nme before I had language for it.\nThis is one of the truths of my early life that I want\nto write clearly. My father paid for our growing from\nfar away. But the men who walked me into the rooms\nwhere I would learn the things I now write about —\nthose were the grandfathers and the teachers. The","bloodline does its work through whoever is present.\nAbsence is not failure. Presence is not virtue. The work\ngets done by whoever is in the room when the door\nopens.\n* * *\nThe reading moved from Surah Al-Fatiha into Surah\nAl-Baqarah. For Hifzul — for memorisation — the\npoint was not to understand. The point was to receive.\nThe sounds entered through the ear and were placed,\nline by line, into a part of memory that does not require\ncomprehension. I was a small storage vessel for verses I\ncould not yet read with my mind. The Maulvi did not\nstop to explain. He did not have time, and I did not have\nthe years.\nBut there was one moment, every morning, when\nsomething shifted. When the Adhan called for Fajr, the\nrecitation paused, and we stood for prayer. After the\nlong sit, after the swaying body and the foreign words,\nthe prayer felt like a rest. Standing in the row, hands\nfolded, eyes lowered, the breath finally slowed. In that\nmoment, prayer was not an obligation I was rushing\nthrough. It was a stillness after the movement. I did not","have the language for it then, but my body knew the\ndifference. The recitation worked the muscles of the\ntongue. The prayer worked something deeper that I\nwould not learn to name for another twenty years.\n* * *\nLater, when we were a little older, my brother and I\nfound a different kind of freedom.\nMy father, by then, had gifted me a 50cc scooter —\nsmall, light, mine. My mother would wake us before\ndawn and send us to the Madrasa in Kudiyiruppu. We\nwould dress in our Madrasa clothes, take the scooter,\nand ride.\nBut we did not always go to the Madrasa. We had\nfound a place — the Kudiyiruppu bridge. We would\nstop there, in the early light, and change out of our\nMadrasa clothes into normal clothes that we had hidden\ninside the bike. And then we would ride. Round and\nround those roads, free, two brothers and a small engine\nand the morning sky, until eight o'clock — the hour the\nMadrasa would have ended — and then we would go\nhome, as proof that we had attended.","I am not proud of the deception now. But I am\nhonest about it. We did not always go. And yet,\nsomehow, half of Surah Al-Baqarah had already\nentered me by then, and it stayed. The sound was inside\nmy body. The meaning was still waiting to find me.\n* * *\nThirty years passed. Thirty years of leaving, of\nworking, of feeding the body, of becoming a son and a\nbrother and a technician and a man chasing love across\ncountries.\nAnd then I was on a flight — the last one of my life\nthat would land in Malta as a free man, though I did not\nknow that yet — and the words came back to me in a\nway I had not expected.\nA flight is a strange kind of stillness. The body is\nmoving at incredible speed and yet it cannot move.\nThere is nowhere to walk, nothing to fix, no errand to\nrun. The seat holds you. The sky holds you. You are\nforced, for a few hours, into a posture that the body\nalmost never accepts on the ground. Stillness.\nSo for the first time in my life, I played Surah\nAl-Baqarah with the English translation in my ear. Not","to recite. Not for reward. Not to fulfil an obligation. To\nunderstand. To finally meet, as a grown man, the verses\nthat an old cigarette-seller had walked me through the\ndark to receive when I was a boy too small to know\nwhat he was giving me.\nI cannot pretend it all came at once. It did not. But\nsomething opened. The words I had carried in my\nmouth for thirty years began to land, slowly, in my\nchest. The cow. The orphan. The patience. The hellfire.\nThe throne. The verses that warn and the verses that\ncomfort. The God who never sleeps. The believers and\nthe Jews and the Christians and any who believed in\nGod and the Last Day and did good — all of them with\ntheir reward. I had been carrying this all along. I had\nsimply never been still enough to hear it.\n* * *\nWhat was I praying for on that plane? I want to be\nhonest, because honesty is the only thing this book has\nto offer.\nI was chasing love. That is the only true answer. I\nwas not asking God to protect me from what was\ncoming, because I did not know anything was coming. I","was the same horse without a rider, running fast toward\na woman, a country, a future, a feeling. The mind was\nopen, the heart was open, but the watcher in me had not\nyet taken hold of the reins.\nAnd so I landed in Malta with Surah Al-Baqarah\nnewly understood in my chest and my body still\nrunning in the wrong direction.\nThe cage was already waiting for me. I just could not\nsee it yet.\nWhat I know now, what the years since have taught\nme, is this: the only place quiet enough for me to finally\nfinish hearing what Surah Al-Baqarah had been saying\nall along was a cell I had not yet entered. The flight had\ngiven me a taste of the stillness. The prison would give\nme the rest of it.\nBut I am moving ahead too fast. Before the cell there\nwas Qatar. Before Qatar there were many roads. The\nflight on which I played Surah Al-Baqarah was not the\nfirst time I left Sri Lanka. It was the last time, before the\ndoor closed. Let me go back to the beginning of the\nleaving — to a young man with a passport in his hand\nand a father waiting for him in a foreign airport — and","tell you how the body that would one day need that\nsurah was first carried out of its village.\nThe boy who carried the verses before he understood\nthem.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER · BLOODLINE\n7\nThe Fire in the Name\nI carry four names. Mathihul. Akmal. Mohamed.\nJabir.\nMost people call me Akmal. The passport that\nbrought me to Malta calls me Jabir, because of a small\nclerical error I have written about already, and which I\nhave never quite been able to undo. But the full name\n— all four parts of it — is not just a string of sounds. It\nis a map. It is four generations pressed into one\nsentence.\nI did not understand that for most of my life. I\nthought a name was a thing people called you at dinner,\na thing printed on a school report. I did not know that\nmy name was also a story that began long before I was\nborn, and would continue long after I am gone.\nLet me tell you where the name comes from. Let me\ntell you about the fire.\n* * *","My great-grandfather's name was Shehu\nMuhammad. He lived in Kankeyanodai, in the\nKattankudy area of Batticaloa, on the eastern coast of\nSri Lanka. He was a Sufi — a Muslim mystic — of the\nkind that does not fit easily into any category the world\ntries to put him in.\nHe did not run a formal school the way people\nimagine schools. He did not wear religion as a costume.\nHe lived inside his practice the way fire lives inside\nwood. You could not always see it from the outside. But\nyou could feel the heat coming off him from across a\nroom. People who sat near him said the air itself\nchanged. Children grew quiet around him. Grown men\nlowered their voices without being asked.\nThere are people in this world who carry a kind of\nweight that has nothing to do with their size or their\nwealth or their words, and he was one of them.\nThe people of his area called him Neruppu Masthan.\nNeruppu, in Tamil, means fire. Masthan, in the Sufi\nsense, means a saint who has been pulled so completely\ninto the presence of God that the ordinary rules of life\nno longer apply to him in ordinary ways.","Fire saint. That is what they called my\ngreat-grandfather. And the name was not poetry. The\nname came from something they had seen with their\nown eyes.\n* * *\nThe story my family kept is simple, the way real\nstories are always simple. They did not decorate it.\nThey did not add to it over the years to make it more\nimpressive. They told it the way you report the weather\n— calmly, as a thing that had happened, with no need\nfor embellishment.\nOne day, Shehu Muhammad gathered some dry\nleaves. He brought them together in front of him. He\nlooked at them. He breathed. And he spoke one word.\nHu.\nThe leaves caught fire. They burned to ash.\nThat is the whole story. Leaves, breath, one word,\nfire. No explanation was offered, and none was asked\nfor. The people who saw it did not need a theory to\nmake peace with it. They simply gave him a name and\ncarried the memory forward, parent to child, parent to","child, until it reached my father, and my father gave it\nto me.\nFor most of my life I held that story at a careful\ndistance. I respected it. I believed that my family\nbelieved it. But I did not know what to do with it. It sat\nsomewhere between a miracle and a poem, and I did not\nhave the language to bring it closer. How do you hold a\nstory like that? You are not there. You cannot test it.\nYou only have the faces of the people who told it to\nyou, and the fact that they were not the kind of people\nwho lied.\nThen I went to prison. And inside Cell 13, with\nnothing left but time and silence and a window and the\ndirection of the wind, I began to understand things I had\nnever understood before. Not because the prison taught\nme, but because the stillness did. And after I came out,\nwhen I read about what scientists had measured in\nTibetan monasteries, I stopped holding my\ngreat-grandfather's story at a distance. I let it come\nclose. Because what the scientists were measuring in a\nlaboratory was the same thing my family had been\ncarrying in their memory for seventy years.","* * *\nIn 1981, a doctor from Harvard Medical School\ntravelled to the Himalayas to study Tibetan monks who\npractised a meditation called g-tummo — the yoga of\ninner fire. These monks could do something that\nseemed impossible.\nSitting in the freezing cold, wrapped in wet sheets,\nusing only their breath and the focus of their minds,\nthey generated enough heat in their bodies to dry the\nsheets and send steam rising into the cold mountain air.\nNot through shivering. Not through movement.\nThrough breath, stillness, and the trained attention of a\nmind that had practised for years.\nThe doctor brought instruments with him. He\nmeasured. He confirmed it was real. The heat was real.\nLater studies measured rises in skin temperature that\nshould not have been possible through thought alone.\nAnd yet there it was, recorded, peer-reviewed,\nundeniable. The human body, through breath and\nconcentration, can produce real, measurable heat. The\nline between the inner world of the mind and the outer\nworld of the body is not as fixed as we have been taught","to believe.\nI am not a scientist. I want to be honest about that. I\nam not telling you that my great-grandfather was doing\nexactly what those monks were doing, measured to the\ndegree. I am telling you that when I read about those\nmonks, I recognised my own bloodline.\nShehu Muhammad was not in Tibet. He was on a\nsmall island in the Indian Ocean, inside a tradition of\nSufi breath practice that uses a single word as its\nengine: Hu. The unmanifested name of God. The\nessence before the attributes. He inhaled the name of\nGod and exhaled Hu, again and again, for years, until\nthe breath became a prayer and the prayer became\nsomething with heat in it.\nHe was not breaking the laws of nature. I believe he\nwas living inside laws of nature that most human beings\nnever reach — the laws that live on the far side of\nstillness, where the body and the mind stop behaving\nlike two separate things.\n* * *\nMy family says he taught Wahdatul Wujud. This is\nnot a small phrase. It is a precise teaching in Islamic","mysticism. It means the Unity of Existence — the\nunderstanding that in this entire cosmos there is only\none true existence, and that existence is God.\nEverything else that appears to exist — you, me, the\nleaves, the fire, the air between my fingers as I write\nthis — is a reflection, an expression, a face of that\nsingle Reality. This teaching is dangerous to those who\nhave not felt it, because to a literal mind it sounds like\nconfusion, like mixing God with creation. There were\nreligious authorities who declared people who taught it\nto be outside the faith. And yet my great-grandfather\ntaught it openly. Families came to him and took oaths\nof allegiance. He did not hide.\nThat takes more than courage. It takes certainty —\nthe certainty of a man who has experienced the thing\nhimself and is no longer guessing.\nAnd it is exactly what I felt in Cell 13, lying on a\nnarrow bed, watching the sky change through a small\nwindow for hours. At some point in that long stillness I\nstopped feeling like a separate person with a separate\nproblem. The boundary between me and the moment\ngrew thin. Not gone. Thin. I did not have a word for","what I was feeling. But Shehu Muhammad had the\nword. He had been living inside that thinness his whole\nlife. We were separated by seventy years and an ocean\nand a prison wall, and yet for a few hours, I think I\ntouched the edge of the same thing he lived in every\nday.\n* * *\nHis son was Muhammad Ibrahim.\nMuhammad Ibrahim's son was my father, Mohamed\nJabir.\nAnd Mohamed Jabir's son is me — Mathihul Akmal.\nMy grandfather Muhammad Ibrahim read the Qur'an\nevery single day. He had the dark mark of prayer on his\nforehead, the one that comes only from years of\npressing the head to the ground. He was known as a\nteacher of Islam. He had the outer form, complete and\ndisciplined. And according to my long\nwitness-observation, he did not truly forgive or come\nproperly toward his own son — my father, Jabir. I do\nnot present this as a court record. People can say, I\nforgive. But action over many years shows the heart.","Recently I asked my father about this. He was\nuncertain. He said maybe his father forgave him. He\nsaid he may ask my mother, because he had been away\nabroad for so many years. I told him that from my side I\nwas not speaking as the victim or the accuser. I was\nspeaking as the witness.\nIf forgiveness had really lived there, it would have\nwalked. It would have visited. It would have crossed the\ndistance. It would have come not for money, not for\nfestival formality, not for ten rupees placed in a child's\nhand, but for love. I remember visiting my father's side\nonce or twice, mostly speaking to my father's brother.\nChildren may receive small festival money, ten rupees\nmaybe, but children do not go for ten rupees. They go\nfor love.\nThe fire that Shehu Muhammad had carried — the\nliving, transferable fire — had become, in the line after\nhim, something more complicated. Muhammad Ibrahim\ncarried form, knowledge, discipline, and prayer. But\ninside the family memory there was also hardness. I am\nnot writing this to mock him. He was my elder. He was\npart of my blood. I am writing it because the\ncontradiction is where the question began.","I have thought about this for a long time, because it\nfrightens me. It tells me that what you inherit from a\nbloodline is not automatic. The fire does not simply\npass down through blood like an eye colour. Shehu\nMuhammad had it. Muhammad Ibrahim, his son,\ncarried the form. My father, Mohamed Jabir, carried the\noil — the lonely labour, the sacrifice, the years abroad,\nthe fuel that kept our house alive. And maybe I was\nborn later, not to judge them, but to ask why the lamp\nhad not yet fully burned inside us.\nIn my own understanding, Surah 41 speaks of lamps\nin the nearest heaven, and Surah 24 speaks of a lamp,\nglass, a blessed tree, and oil almost glowing. I am not\ngiving tafsir here. I am only telling you what the images\ndid inside me. Maybe Shehu Muhammad carried the\nfire. Maybe Ibrahim carried the form. Maybe my father\ncarried the oil. And maybe my work is to explain in\ndetail why the lamp still matters.\nThe name does not save you. Only the stillness does.\n* * *\nMy full name is Mathihul Akmal Mohamed Jabir.\nBut inside this story, the two names that pull against","each other are Akmal and Jabir. Akmal is the inner\nname, the called name, the name that carries\ncompleteness, perfection, wholeness. Jabir is the\ndocument name, the passport name, the outside-world\nname, the name the world often used.\nI carry both. I carry the fire and the forgetting. I\ncarry the saint and the man who could not soften\nenough toward his son. I carry the paalappam and the\ninside pocket and the cough at three in the morning. I\ncarry the bridge at Kudiyiruppu and half of Surah\nAl-Baqarah memorised in the dark before I understood\na word of it.\nAnd then I went to prison. And in that cell, in that\nparticular silence that only comes when everything else\nhas been taken from you, something in me began to\nmove in the direction of Shehu Muhammad. I am not\ntrying to become him. I am not trying to repeat miracles\nor make fire from leaves. That belonged to his time, his\nlanguage, his relationship with God.\nI am not trying to make fire from leaves. I am trying\nto explain in detail.","That is the work given to me now. To explain life,\nright and wrong, experience, awareness, and how\nmistakes can become teachers if a person wakes up\nfrom them. I am not encouraging wrong living. Do not\nlive in wrongness. But if you have passed through\nwrongness, do not waste it. Understand it. Wake up\nfrom it. Choose differently.\nShehu Muhammad left no book. Muhammad\nIbrahim left no book. Mohamed Jabir has not left a\nbook, though he is alive and there is still time. I am the\nfirst in this line to leave one. That is not pride. That is\nresponsibility. The fire was always in the bloodline. It\nonly needed someone still enough to feel it, and willing\nenough to write it down.\nKun fayakun. Be — and it is. The fire continues.\nAnd the next chapter of this book is, in some way, the\nchapter the fire saint never got to write. He gave me the\nname. I am giving him the page.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER\n8\nThe Things I Carried in Silence\nThis is a book of truth, and so I have to write the\nthings I hid even from myself for a long time. Not for\nsympathy. Not to accuse anyone.\nI write them because a shadow only has power while\nit stays hidden, and I am tired of carrying shadows that\nwere never mine to carry in the first place.\nThere were two times in my early life when grown\nmen tried to use me — a child, and then a young man\nlooking for work — for something a child should never\nbe near. I will not write these in detail, because the\ndetail is not the point, and the detail belongs only to me.\nBut I will write enough that any person reading this\nbook who has carried something similar in silence will\nknow, with their whole body, that they are not alone,\nand that it was never their fault.\n* * *","The first time, I was small. Around the fourth\nstandard, maybe a little later. The years run together\nnow in the way childhood years sometimes do.\nThere was a man behind the school who had a green\nparrot. A parrot is a magnet for children. We are drawn\nto bright things, to talking things, to anything that\nbreaks the ordinary day. That is exactly why he had it.\nThe bright thing was the bait. The children were what\nhe was really after.\nI remember the feeling more than the events. The\nfeeling of something being wrong before my mind had\nthe words for wrong. A child's body knows danger\nbefore a child's mind can name it — the same way I had\nlearned, years earlier, not to throw a stone behind an\ninnocent cow. Something in me pulled back. Something\nin me said no, even though I did not fully understand\nwhat I was saying no to. I got away.\nThat is the part I hold onto now. The watching boy,\nthe one who was always observing from the edge of\nthings — he saw enough to protect himself even then.\nHe did not have a vocabulary for what was happening,\nbut he had a body that knew, and he listened to it.","I never told anyone. I carried it the way I carried the\nmatchbox at the beach and the money from my\ngrandfather's inside pocket — silently, in the chest,\nwhere guilt and fear live in a child even when the child\ndid nothing wrong.\nThat is the cruelty of it. The child who is targeted\noften feels the shame that belongs entirely to the adult\nwho targeted him. The shame travels in the wrong\ndirection. The world has not yet figured out how to\nredirect it. So the child carries it forward, into other\nyears, into other rooms, into a body that grows up not\nunderstanding why some kinds of touch make it freeze\nand some kinds of trust feel impossible.\n* * *\nThe second time, I was older — a young man,\nwalking the streets of Colombo looking for work, the\nway young men from the villages do, with a bag and a\nhope and not much else.\nA man slowed his car beside me. He asked me for\ndirections, the way someone asks when they want to\nseem ordinary and safe. I started to help him, because\nhelping is what I had been taught to do. He used that.","He took me somewhere I had not meant to go.\nAnd I understood, in stages, what was actually\nhappening.\nAnd again, that same thing in me — the watcher, the\npart that sees — woke up. I understood. I refused. I left\nthat place. I do not remember every detail of how, but I\nremember the certainty of leaving, the clean feeling of\nhaving defended myself, the awareness that I had been\nawake enough to get out.\nI see that now as the second time my own\nobservation saved me. Maybe that is part of why I\nbecame the way I am — always watching, always\nreading the room, always sensing the thing under the\nthing. The boy who survived those moments learned,\nvery early, that the mind is what keeps the body safe.\n* * *\nI am telling these now, in this book, with my eyes\nopen, because I have decided that nothing in my life\nwill own me through silence anymore.\nIf you are reading this and you carried something\nsimilar — carry it differently now. It was not yours. The","shame belonged to them. The fear belonged to the\nmoment, not to your whole life. And the part of you that\ngot away, that said no, that left — that was your mind\nprotecting you. That was the watcher.\nFeed that part. It is the best of you. It kept you alive\nlong enough to read these words.\nAnd there is one more reason I am writing this\nchapter exactly here, between the bloodline and the\nleaving. The boy who survived those two grown men\ndid not yet know that the same instinct that saved him\nfrom them would, years later, be the same instinct that\nsurvived the cell. The watcher does not arrive when you\nneed him. The watcher is built over years by every\nsmall moment in which you noticed something before\nyou should have been able to notice it. The man behind\nthe school with the green parrot, in some strange way,\nwas one of the first teachers of the watcher who would,\ndecades later, sit calmly in a courtroom while strangers\nstared at him in handcuffs.\nI would not wish those teachers on any child. But\nsince they happened, I am at least going to tell the truth\nabout what was made from them. The man writing this","book is, in part, the boy who learned, very young, that\nhe had to keep his eyes open. Closed eyes were not safe\nfor him. The watcher was a survival tool before he\nbecame a spiritual one. The body knew. The body\nalways knew.","ACT ONE · THE MESSENGER\nTHE FATHER","9\nThe Stillness My Father Never Named\nFor most of my life, I knew my father as a back. The\nback I memorised at the Ampara bus station in 1999,\nclimbing into a bus, leaving for the Gulf. The back I\nrode behind on his motorcycle as a small boy, my cheek\nnear his shoulder, the wind broken by his body. The\nback that disappeared, for twenty-six years, into another\ncountry, so that we could grow.\nIt is only now, as a grown man who has himself\ncrossed oceans and lived alone in foreign rooms, that I\ncan finally turn him around and see his face. And what I\nsee, looking back, is a man who was half-awake in the\ndeepest sense — a man who had found God, truly, but\nfound Him only from one side.\n* * *\nMy father understood God. Not the way the village\nunderstood God, as a list of rules and a scoreboard in\nthe sky. He understood God in his body, through his\ndiscipline, through his patience, through twenty-six\nyears of stillness in a foreign mosque. He just did not","understand the other half — that God was also inside\nhim. He never read the Bible, so the door of the\nkingdom-within never opened for him in that language.\nHe had the Qur'an, and some verses held him, carried\nhim, steadied him across the decades. But he met God\nas something he turned toward, not yet as something he\nturned inward to find.\nThat is the half. He found God outside — in prayer,\nin the mosque, in the discipline of a working life. He\ndid not yet find God inside — in the stillness behind his\nown closed eyes, in the light, in the conscious mind that\nwas God's own seat within him. And yet, watching him,\nI now understand that he was living the practice his\nwhole life. He simply never called it a practice. He\nnever used a single one of the words I use. He just woke\nearly, cared for his body, walked away from conflict,\nand let it lead him back to prayer.\n* * *\nLet me tell you the small things, because the small\nthings are where I finally saw him.\nHe does not take medicine if he can avoid it. He does\nnot drink Coca-Cola — for any reason. Coconut water,","yes. Coconut, yes. The natural things. But Coca-Cola,\nnever. And here is what amazes me now: he reached\nthis without any deep knowledge of nutrition, without\nreading a single study, without any of the words I use.\nHe simply felt, in his body, that Coca-Cola was haram\n— forbidden — even though the religion only names\nalcohol among drinks. He extended the meaning of the\nword himself. Quietly. Privately. Correctly. Because his\nunderstanding of haram was simpler and deeper than\nthe village version: whatever hurts the body is haram.\nThat is all. One clean principle, instead of a long\ninherited list.\nHe eats what his body needs and leaves what it does\nnot. He never once sat me down and forced this on me.\nHe just lived it in front of me, every day, and I absorbed\nit the way a child absorbs a language — without a\nsingle formal lesson. That is how I learned. Not from\nhis words. From his body.\n* * *\nI remember small physical things about him that I\nonly understand now. He oils his head — to respect the\ntemperature of the crown, because the head overheats.","Many people do this. But he did it for one reason:\nbecause it needed to be done, the way he was raised to\ndo it. No performance. No explanation. Just the quiet\nmaintenance of the body, done faithfully, every day, for\na lifetime.\nWhen he eats, he sweats — a lot. And as he eats, he\nwipes his forehead with his left hand, or the back of his\nneck where the skin folds. He has always done this. He\nlikes to be cool while he eats, because his body accepts\nfood better that way. Even this — a small habit I\nwatched my whole childhood — was his body teaching\nhim what it needed, and him obeying it without ever\ncalling it a discipline.\n* * *\nHe still believes prayer gives him patience. And he\nhas a rule I only understand now: if he cannot be patient\nwith someone, he simply removes himself. He walks\naway from the place. And that removal leads him back\nto prayer. That is the eleven rules before I ever wrote\nthe eleven rules. That is the same teaching I reached in\na cell — do not feed the fire, remove yourself, return to\nstillness — except my father reached it through","twenty-six years of working alone in a foreign country,\nwith no family, no distraction, only work and the\nmosque.\nIn his deepest prayer, he went so completely into\nstillness that you could mistake him for a man who had\nstepped out of time. He was alone there. No family to\npull at him. No noise. Only work and the mosque and\nthe long quiet between them. And at one point, he\nstopped giving himself distraction at all. It elevated\nhim. It made him a more meaningful man — and, in his\nown quiet way, a better father than the father he had\nreceived.\n* * *\nBecause his own father — my grandfather,\nMohammed Ibrahim — did not bless his marriage. My\nmother's family was connected to the making and\nselling of bidi, and to my grandfather, that was haram,\nbeneath the family, a reason to refuse. So he refused, in\nhis way, to fully accept his own son for choosing love\nacross that line.\nLook at the irony sitting inside that. My grandfather\nused the word haram as a wall — haram as status,","haram as a reason to refuse love. And my father took\nthe very same word and healed it. The father\nweaponised haram. The son purified it into one clean\nprinciple: whatever hurts the body. The word that was\nused to reject his love became, in his hands, a quiet law\nof care. That is the bloodline waking up — one\ngeneration at a time, taking the broken thing and\nbending it back toward truth.\n* * *\nMy father chose his life as a young man. At\ntwenty-four, he chose love — his own marriage, his\nown path — instead of bending to his father's\ncommand. His father's authority did not get to decide\nhis life for him. I am thirty-four now, and I am making\nthe same kind of choice he made at twenty-four: to\nfollow my own conscience, my own understanding,\ninstead of bending to the commandment of any human\nauthority.\nBecause here is the law I have learned. If a man\naccepts a human being's commandment — even his\nown father's — in place of God's, he will be satisfied\nonly through suffering. When you obey a man in place","of God, you keep the peace on the outside and pay the\ncost inside, as suffering, for the rest of your life. And\nsuffering is not surrendering. The unawake man\nconfuses them. He thinks that because he is suffering\nunder someone's authority, he has surrendered to\nsomething holy. But he has not surrendered. He has\nonly submitted to a person out of fear of being let go.\nA man submits to a human authority because he\nfeels that person is holding him — keeping him safe,\nkeeping him from falling. And the deepest fear\nunderneath is: if I let go of this person's approval, who\nwill hold me? Will anyone? Will even God? The\nterrifying, freeing awakening is to be willing to feel that\nnobody is holding you — to let go of every hand, even\nthe comfortable image of God you were taught to lean\non — and to discover that what catches you is the real\nthing. My father did this at twenty-four. He let go of his\nfather's hand and chose love, and instead of falling, he\ngrew. I am doing it now. The bloodline is learning, one\ngeneration at a time, to let go of the wrong hands.\n* * *","These days, my father is compromising with his\nbody a little. His weight has shifted. His body carries\nsomething a bit unusual now — not because he stopped\ncaring, but because his routine was broken. Twenty-six\nyears of the Gulf gave him a fixed rhythm: the same\nwake time, the same work, the same mosque, the same\nsimple food. That rhythm was his medicine. And when\na man's lifelong routine breaks — through returning\nhome, through age, through the end of the work that\nstructured every day — the body feels the break before\nthe man does.\nBut here is what I love about him. He is doing his\nroutine again — just in another way. He has not given\nup. He is rebuilding the rhythm in a new shape, the way\nI rebuilt mine after the cell. The body still needs\nsomething — not the exact engineering job, not the\nexact Gulf schedule, but the same underlying thing: a\nrhythm, a discipline, a daily care. And he is finding it\nagain, in his older years, in his own quiet way.\n* * *\nMy father shows me something I want you to\nunderstand. You do not need the words to live the truth.","And you do not need a perfect, unbroken life to keep\nliving it.\nHe reached “whatever hurts the body is haram”\nwithout a single nutrition class. He reached “walk away\nfrom conflict and let it lead you to prayer” without a\nsingle book on anger. He reached “keep the body in a\nrhythm” through twenty-six years of solitude he never\nonce called a spiritual practice. And now, when his\nrhythm has broken with age, he is not collapsing — he\nis quietly rebuilding it.\nThe best teachers do not force. They live it, and let\nyou watch. My father never taught me a single lesson\non purpose. He taught me everything by being himself\nin front of me for the years he was home, and by\nleaving the rhythm of his discipline in my memory for\nthe years he was away. He had the practice. I have the\nwords. And when I put them together, I see that my\nfather has been my teacher all along — the unforced\nteacher, the one who never made me learn, who simply\nlived it in front of me until it became part of me.\nHe found God from one side. I am climbing the other\nside too. But it is the same God, and the same mountain,","and the same quiet at the top. And when I reach it, I\nknow whose back I will be following up the last of the\nslope.","ACT ONE · THE MESSENGER\nTHE MOTHER","10\nYou Cannot Divide Her Day by Eight\nDo not ask me what one ordinary day of my mother\nlooked like, divided neatly into hours. You cannot\ndivide her day by eight. Because she was never one\nwoman living one simple day. She was an entire\ninstitution, alone, every single day, for decades.\nShe was the cleaner for a household of eight. The\ngardener for eight. The housekeeping for eight, every\nday. The chef for eight. The kitchen staff for eight\nchildren, every day. The night watcher who lay awake\nover eight children. She was the accountant for more\nthan a decade, and the payroll too. She was the teacher\nof eight. The babysitter of eight. The tailor of eight,\nstitching their clothes. The tea maker of eight. The one\nwho arranged every room, who was the washing\nmachine before there was a washing machine, who\nprepared everything before anyone woke and cleaned\neverything after they slept.\nShe was the mother of eight children. And she was\nthe father of eight children too — because the father","was across the ocean for twenty-six years. She was the\ndoctor of eight. The physician of eight. One woman,\nholding every one of these roles, at the same time, every\nsingle day, for decades, alone.\nNo single human being should be able to be all of\nthis at once. And yet she was. That is not an ordinary\nday. That is a miracle performed so quietly, so\nconstantly, that everyone around her mistook it for\nnormal. She is higher than anything you can imagine.\nHow could I ever reduce her to a simple day, divided by\neight?\n* * *\nAnd there is something most people never realise.\nShe was also the walker of your thoughts. She did not\nonly feed your body and wash your clothes — she built\nyour mind. The way you think, the thoughts that move\nthrough you right now — more than half of them came\nfrom her, stitched in during the early years when she\ntaught you language, taught you the way of eating,\nsitting, sleeping, walking, before you could even\nremember being taught. She is inside your thoughts.\nShe is the source of most of how your mind moves.","In the family, I would say it like this: a quarter of\nyou comes from your mother, a quarter from your\nfather, and half you shape yourself. But that first quarter\nis the foundation the other three are built upon. Take\naway the mother's quarter, and there is no floor to stand\non.\n* * *\nLet me tell you one small thing, because the small\nthings hold more than the grand ones.\nWhen we were young, my brother and I both went to\nthe market. My brother knew exactly what he wanted,\nand he would tell her plainly: make this today, light, no\nspice. But she came to me differently. She came with\ntea — the morning tea I have loved my whole life, the\ntea I still fast with every day. She woke me gently,\nnever shouting, and asked softly: do you want the spicy\nchili curry, or the other one? And half-asleep, my own\nsubconscious answered for me — spicy, Ma. So she\nmade the spicy.\nAnd my brother complained: whatever I ask, you\ndon't make; whatever he says, you do. It was not really\na complaint. It was love — it was a child wanting his","mother's love expressed his own way, through his own\nchosen meal. She gave, and she also did not give. And\nin that small gap — who was woken gently with tea,\nwho had to ask plainly for his curry — the whole quiet\ndistance between two brothers was built, grain by grain.\nThe smallest things make the largest distances. To this\nday, when the family complains about her, I defend her,\nand my brother still carries a small edge — and neither\nof us fully realises it began with a cup of tea and a plate\nof curry.\n* * *\nShe woke at four, at five, sometimes at seven. She\ncomplained, but she accepted. She always made me eat,\nbut she never shouted at me to do it — though with my\nsister she would raise her voice, send her into the\nkitchen. Her love had categories, and to this day I do\nnot know who made them — the children, or her. With\nme, she never shouted. She never let me down.\nWhen I was very young, she was sick often. I would\nsee her belly swollen and not know what to ask. I only\nknew the fear: is she going to stay? Is something\nwrong? A small boy carrying, without words, the terror","that his mother might not live long. Later I understood\n— a baby was coming. And there was nerve trouble in\nher feet, from standing so long, from carrying the\nweight of eight people for so many years. Now I see the\nsame thing beginning faintly in my own skin, and my\ncolleagues notice something similar. The body she\ncarried is echoing now in mine.\n* * *\nThere is one thing I have never said to her face. So\nlet me say it here, and may it reach her.\nUmma. Listen to the word — it sounds like a kiss.\nThe lips come together to say it, the way they come\ntogether to kiss. The very name of the mother carries\nthe kiss inside its sound, because she is the first love,\nthe first lips that ever kissed you. The word itself is\nlove.\nUmma — I am your son. That is unchangeable.\nNothing in this world can alter it. So believe this: I will\nnever leave you behind, for any reason, until your\ndeath. I will be there. Stop fearing. Stop asking yourself\n— will he support me, will he help me, will he be with\nme, will he check on me, will he call, will he get angry,","will he avoid me because I could not rise from my bed\nall day? Don't worry about any of it. I am here, because\nyou are there. I will be.\nYou spoke to me in duty all your life, because no\none ever taught you to speak in love. The husband was\nfar, across the ocean, for twenty-six years — so it was\nthe first son who received the completeness of your\nlove, the love that had nowhere else to fully land. That\nis why this bond runs so deep. That is why I, of all your\nchildren, am the one who can finally speak the love\nback to you. The first son carries the first love. And\nnow I return it. Stop fearing. Rest. I am here.","Umma inside the kitchen, where survival became\ncare.\nUmma in the corridor, where a narrow light carried\nthe house.","Umma, carrying life across years.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER · FLASHBACK\n11\nColombo — The Pentium III and the\nMat\nBefore Qatar, before Dubai, before Malta, there was\nColombo.\nIn 2003, my father came home from the Gulf for one\nof his visits and brought with him a gift that changed\nthe direction of my life without my understanding it at\nthe time. A Pentium III computer. A grey tower, a thick\nmonitor, a keyboard that clicked like a small machine.\nTo a boy in our village in those years, having a personal\ncomputer at home was almost unheard of. The street\noutside still had cattle on it. The houses around us still\ncooked on wood fires. And in our small front room,\nbeside the prayer mat and the rice tin, sat a machine\nfrom another world.\nMy father did not say much when he gave it to me.\nHe did not lecture me about the future of technology.\nHe simply said: this is yours. Use it. Learn. He\nunderstood, in his own way, that the work he was doing","in the Gulf with his hands would not be the same work\nhis son would do in the years to come. His back, bent\nunder metal and machinery in the Saudi heat, was\npaying for a screen that would put a different kind of\nlife in front of me. He did not say any of this. The\nPentium said it for him.\nI taught myself on it for years. The slow internet of\nthose days, the early Windows, the dial-up sounds, the\nlong downloads, the dictionary I kept beside the\nkeyboard for the English words I did not yet know. I\nlearned the languages of computers the way a child\nlearns to speak — by repeating, by failing, by trying\nagain. By the time I left for Colombo to begin my\nHigher National Diploma in IT, the Pentium had taught\nme enough that I could speak with technicians at a level\nthat surprised people who looked at me and assumed,\nfrom my village clothes, that I knew nothing of\nmachines.\n* * *\nColombo was the first time I was alone in the world.\nI rented a small place with four other friends. We\nwere five young men from the eastern villages, all of us","in Colombo for the same reason — education, the\ndream of a job in the capital, the slow rising of the\nfamily through the eldest son. None of us had money.\nWe shared everything. The kitchen. The bathroom. The\nsingle fan.\nI slept on a woven mat on the floor. Not because we\ndid not have beds — though we did not have enough —\nbut because the mat was what I had grown up with, and\na mat does not need maintenance. You roll it out at\nnight. You roll it up in the morning. You sweep\nunderneath. The mat is honest. The body learns to sleep\non it. The back becomes straighter than it would have\non any mattress in those years.\nIn the mornings we ate at a small Muslim Tamil food\nshop near our place. The shop was run by Tamil\nMuslim neighbours — people who spoke Tamil like us,\nthe kind of Kattankudy or Qatar-returned people who\nknow how to feed young men far from home. It was not\na Saiva vegetarian shop. They served chicken, mutton,\nbiryani, rice, curry, tea, and other food that carried the\nsmell of home into the city. On the days we had no\nmoney, they let us eat on credit. Sometimes I borrowed\nfood and paid later. The credit was rarely written down.","The accounting was kept in human memory, by people\nwho knew the value of feeding young men whose\nmothers were far away. I think of them sometimes now\nand I owe them more than I can ever pay back. Not in\nmoney. They fed me. And in feeding me, they kept me\nhuman in a city that was trying to make me anonymous.\n* * *\nColombo was also the first time I really understood\nthat I had escaped my village.\nNot escaped in a bad way. I loved my village. I still\nlove my village. But in Colombo I was no longer the\neldest son of the man in the Gulf, the grandson of\nFarooq the cigarette-seller, the boy who carried ninety\nrupees from the bus depot. I was simply Akmal, a\nyoung man with a laptop bag and a HND in IT, walking\nthrough a city that did not know my family. The\nanonymity was, for the first time in my life, a kind of\nfreedom. I could become someone the village had not\npre-decided for me.\nI moved through Colombo on a motorbike and by\nbus, through neighbourhoods of every religion, every\nlanguage, every class. I crossed paths with monks in","saffron, with Christian priests in black robes, with\nHindu women lighting morning lamps, with Muslim\nmen coming out of the Friday prayer. The same streets\ncarried all of us. The same morning sun touched all of\nus. And the boy who had grown up in a Muslim Tamil\nvillage, who had thought the world was mostly people\nlike him, began to see that the world was much larger\nthan the village had told him.\nColombo did not make me less of a Muslim.\nColombo made me a wider one. And without that wider\none, Cell 13 ten years later would not have produced\nthe same man. The seeds of the cell were planted on the\nstreets of Colombo. The watcher who would eventually\nread the Bible beside the Qur'an without conflict was\nthe same watcher who had passed a Hindu temple at\nsunrise on the way to a HND class, and quietly\nunderstood that the people lighting lamps inside were\nnot the enemies of his religion. They were people. Like\nhim. Trying.\n* * *","12\nThere was a house in Colombo, painted green, where\nfor two years I lived as if I had been born there.\nThe green vegetarian shop was separate from the\nMuslim Tamil food shop near our room. It was about\ntwo bus stops away. That was where I met the Hindu\nwoman I called Archie. I called her that with the feeling\nof Aachchi inside it — elder woman, grandmotherly\nrespect, a word with softness in it.\nI do not remember exactly how I ended up at her\ndoor. Someone told someone, the way these things\nhappen in Colombo. I came with my bag and was\nshown a place to stay. She did not ask rent from me. I\ndid not pay rent. The stay was not a rental arrangement.\nIt was kindness, trust, and generosity. From that day,\nfor two years, that green house became home in a way\nno rented place had ever been before.\nArchie was an older Hindu woman, around\nsixty-three then. She had hands that had worked their\nwhole lives, a back that had bent over many cooking\nArchie - The Hindu Aachchi","pots, and eyes that watched young people the way only\nwomen who have raised many children can watch them.\nHer children had grown up and gone elsewhere. The\nhouse was big and quiet, and she allowed young men\nlike me to live inside its quiet without making us feel\nlike strangers.\nShe had a small prayer shelf in the front room. Hindu\npictures. Flowers. A lamp. The first time I saw it, I did\nnot know how to stand in that room. I was a Muslim\nboy from the east. My village had taught me many\nthings, but it had not taught me how to understand the\npeace in a Hindu woman's prayer shelf without\nbecoming afraid of it. Archie did not explain it. She\nsimply lived with it. The shelf was there. I was there.\nThe house did not break.\nI ate at her table for two years. I learned her dishes\nby watching her hands — dosa, vada, paruppu vada,\nulundu vada, rice, sambar, and other food whose names\nentered me through smell before they entered me\nthrough language. The way she chopped onions thin.\nThe way she tempered mustard seeds in oil before\nadding anything else. The way she rinsed rice until the\nwater was almost clear. It felt like a cooking class, but","really it was a life lesson without preaching. She did not\ntry to change my religion. She lived goodness in front\nof me.\nThat sentence will sound strange to people from\ncultures where shared plates are not normal. But in our\npart of the world, eating from the same plate is a\nparticular kind of trust. She would cook a vegetarian\nmeal in the simple Tamil style — rice, sambar, a\nvegetable, a chutney — and we would sit on the floor in\nfront of a single steel plate and eat with our right hands,\nfrom the same surface, the same rice, the same gravy.\nShe would put extra in front of me. She would notice if\nI had eaten less than usual and ask about it. She would\nscold me, in the way mothers scold, if I had skipped\nbreakfast for an early class.\nShe gave me a place without rent. She taught me\nthrough cooking. She showed me kindness beyond\nreligion. Colombo widened my understanding of\nhumanity, but Archie made that widening human. She\ndid not give me a speech about peace. She gave me\nfood, a floor, a room, a plate, and trust. That was\nenough. More than enough.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER · FLASHBACK\n13\nBeragala — Rosie and Freddie\nThere is a mountain in the Sri Lankan central hills\ncalled Beragala, and on a nine-acre piece of land near\nits slopes lived an Australian woman named Rosie and\na German man named Freddie, and for five years I\nvisited them as often as my work would allow, and they\nwere, in the way certain people become, like\ngrandparents to me.\nI had taken up long-distance motorbike journeys by\n2010. From my home in the east, I would travel three\nhundred kilometres, sometimes far more, across the\nisland, sleeping where I could, eating when I could. The\nmotorbike taught me something about endurance that\nthe gym could never teach. The gym is short bursts. A\nlong road is hours. The body becomes a slow patient\nmachine. The mind, given hours of travel with nothing\nelse to do, becomes its own quiet companion.\nI found Rosie and Freddie's farm by accident on one\nof those journeys. I had heard of an Australian woman","who had come back to Sri Lanka in 2009 after many\nyears abroad, and who had married a German man and\nbuilt a small farm in the hills. The story was unusual\nenough that I asked around. Someone gave me\ndirections. I rode up the mountain road, sweating, my\nbody tired from the long road, and I arrived at a gate\nthat had been built by someone who did not believe in\nfences. The land just opened. Nine acres of green, of\nfruit trees, of small paths winding through the slope.\nAnd in the middle of it, a wooden house with a\ncorrugated roof and two old people sitting on the front\nsteps drinking thin coffee.\n* * *\nRosie was small, sharp-eyed, with the dry Australian\nhumour of a woman who has lived several lives.\nFreddie was tall, German, slow-moving, the kind of\nman who took his time in everything he did. They were\nnot soft people. They had loved each other long enough\nto know exactly what was difficult about each other and\nto have stopped pretending otherwise. They argued in\nthe way long-married couples argue — sharp, fast, no\napology, then they would go back to the same coffee","five minutes later as if nothing had happened.\nThe first time Rosie offered me a cup of her coffee,\nshe had made it thin. Too thin. I do not know if she was\ntesting me or just running low on supplies. I was there\nwith two or three friends and two bikes. I drank a sip\nand did not know what to do with the rest. When Rosie\nwent inside, or toward the veranda, I secretly threw the\nthin coffee away outside on the nine-acre land. She did\nnot see it. She did not laugh. I felt guilty immediately.\nThe guilt stayed with me because kindness had been\noffered, even if the coffee was thin. Later, from that\nplace of guilt and affection, I understood Rosie better.\nHer coffee became part of the lesson.\nFreddie used to play a game on an old Nokia 3310,\nor a phone very much like it. Younger readers may not\nknow that phone. It had a tiny screen, hard buttons, and\nthe kind of body that seemed impossible to break. On it\nwas Snake, the simple game where the line of the snake\ngets longer as it eats and you cannot let it hit itself or\nthe wall. Freddie played this game obsessively,\nshouting in German when the snake died, slamming his\nhand on the table. The first time I saw him do it, I\nalmost laughed. A grown man, in his late sixties,","shouting at a snake. Then I understood. The snake was\nnot the point. The shouting was the point. He needed\nsomewhere to put the small daily frustrations of being\nan old German man on a Sri Lankan mountain. The\nsnake game was the chosen outlet. I have thought of\nFreddie's snake game many times since. It is a strange\nand human image of how every man needs a small\nritual to discharge the buzz of being alive.\nHe washed his clothes with discipline. Sometimes he\nused the washing machine. Sometimes he washed by\nhand. The important thing was not the method. The\nimportant thing was the order. He carried a bucket\nslowly. He rinsed. He arranged. He took his time.\nSometimes it took around half an hour just to hang the\nclothes outside on the line with clips. Rosie used to\nmock him for it. Why do you wash clothes that are\nalready clean? she would say. He would shrug and keep\nwashing. I did not understand then. Later, in prison, I\nunderstood the thing underneath it.\nNot because I was doing exactly what Freddie had\ndone. In prison, washing was different. There was a\nmachine. There were systems. Sometimes another\nperson could help. But I understood the thing","underneath it: order. Discipline. Routine. Patience.\nSmall repeated acts that keep a person steady when the\nworld around him is unstable.\n* * *\nI visited them maybe ten or fifteen times between\n2011 and 2016. Not always for long. Sometimes only\nan afternoon. Sometimes a weekend. Each visit, the\ngreen of the farm seemed deeper, the mountain air a\nlittle cleaner, the two old people a little older. They\nbecame, for me, what grandparents become for\ngrandchildren — not parents, not friends, something\ngentler than either. They asked about my work. They\ntold me about their bad backs. They laughed at things\nonly the very old laugh at.\nAnd I learned something from them that I do not\nthink I could have learned anywhere else. I learned that\ntwo people can love each other deeply and still fight\nevery day. That love does not require a smooth surface.\nIt requires a deep root. Their root was deep. Their\nsurface was full of argument, sharp coffee, German\nshouting at snake games, dry Australian humour at the\ndinner table. Underneath it all was a love so real that I","felt safer at their table than I had felt at most tables in\nmy own family. They were two souls, as I came to think\nof them, fighting each other with love. The fight was\nthe proof of the love. They had not run out of opinions\nabout each other. They had not gone numb. They were\nstill alive, still arguing, still drinking coffee together at\nthe end of every day.\nThrough Rosie and Freddie, I met foreign people and\nlearned world wisdom while I was still in Sri Lanka. I\ndid not need to spend huge money to go to Europe or\nAustralia to understand foreigners. Beragala gave me\nthat education first. Later, after visiting sixteen\ncountries and living in Malta, I understood something\nsimple: land is often similar; humans are what change.\nWhat I learned in Sri Lanka, I now experience\npractically in Malta.\n* * *\nRosie sold the farm in 2016 and went back to\nAustralia. I do not know what happened between her\nand Freddie at the end. I do not need to know. I only\nknow that something ended on that mountain that year,\nand a part of my own apprenticeship in being human","ended with it.\nIn Cell 13, on certain quiet afternoons, I thought of\nRosie and Freddie. I thought of the Snake game. I\nthought of the thin coffee I had secretly thrown away\nand the guilt it left in me. I thought of Freddie's bucket,\nnot as a bucket, but as order. I thought of two old\npeople fighting each other with love on a green\nnine-acre farm halfway up a Sri Lankan mountain. And\nthe watcher in me understood, quietly, that some of the\ndeepest schooling of my life had happened on that farm,\nwithout my knowing it was school.\nRosie. Freddie. Wherever you are now, separately or\ntogether, in this life or the next — thank you. You did\nnot have to take in a young Sri Lankan motorbike rider\nwho arrived tired at your gate. You did not have to feed\nme. You did not have to argue in front of me. You did\nnot have to let me see the texture of a long,\ncomplicated, real love. But you did. And you taught me,\nby being yourselves, that a marriage does not have to be\nquiet to be holy. It only has to be real. The fighting was\nreal. The love was real. The coffee, eventually, was\nreal. And the boy who sat at your table grew up and\nwent to a prison and came out and wrote a book, and in","this book you have, finally, the chapter that I owed you.\nThank you both.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER · FLASHBACK\n14\nJailani — The Midnight Mountain\nAfter my A-Levels, I made a decision that surprised\neveryone around me, including, in a way, myself. I\ndecided to travel alone, at night, to a mountain shrine\ncalled Jailani.\nJailani is in the central hills of Sri Lanka, a Sufi\npilgrimage site, named for the great saint Abdul Qadir\nal-Jilani. People go there to pray, to ask for healing, to\nhonour the saint, to be near a place that, over centuries,\nhas soaked up the quiet of many hands and many\nprayers. My father had taken our family there once,\nyears earlier, straight from the airport on one of his\nreturns from the Gulf. We had stayed only a short time.\nWhile climbing one of the rock paths, he had slipped\nand fallen — not seriously, but enough to be\nremembered. The image had stayed in my child mind.\nThe image had waited.\nNow, years later, after my A-Levels, with my father\nhome on vacation, I told him I wanted to go. Alone. He","looked at me. He did not argue. He said only: you are\nsafe. Go. That was his blessing. He did not give me\nmoney or instructions. He gave me the permission a son\nneeds from a father to do something the father himself\nwould not have done. Permission is sometimes the\nlargest gift a father gives. It was for me.\n* * *\nI took the bus. Akkaraipattu to Ampara, then\nconnecting buses up toward the central country. I rode\nthrough the afternoon. The bus carried me through\nvillages I had never seen, past rice fields and small\ntemples and pavement vendors selling fried snacks. I\nwatched everything. The watcher in me, even then at\neighteen, was already practising its work. By night I\nhad reached a town near the mountain.\nI did not have a place to stay. I did not have a\nrelative there. I did not have a hotel booking. I simply\ngot off the bus and walked toward where the mountain\nrose against the dark sky. There was a small shelter near\nthe path, a simple structure, and an old grandmother\nsitting outside it. She did not ask my name. She did not\nask my religion. She looked at me, saw a young man","alone, far from home, and brought me a warm drink. I\ndo not even remember what it was. Some kind of tea,\nsweetened with palm sugar maybe. She handed it to me\nwith the steady hands of a woman who had handed\nwarm drinks to many travellers.\nI slept that night in the shelter. Not well. The\nmountain wind moved through the wooden planks.\nInsects made their small night sounds. The mind was\nalive and quiet at the same time. I was alone in a way I\nhad never been alone before. No friends. No family. No\nschool. No phone, in those years, of any usefulness. Just\na young man on a bench under a mountain shelter, with\na warm drink finishing in his stomach, waiting for the\ndawn.\n* * *\nIn the morning, before I started up the path, an old\nman came to me with a small handmade catapult. A\nY-shaped piece of wood. A piece of bicycle rubber tube\ntied to its arms. A pocket of leather for the stones. He\nhanded it to me without long explanation. He said: for\nthe monkeys. They will come. They will try to take\nyour food. They are clever. With this, you scare them.","You do not hurt them. You scare them.\nAnd he was right. As I climbed the path that\nmorning, monkeys came down out of the trees. They\nwatched me. They followed me. One of them came\nclose enough to almost touch. I lifted the catapult,\npulled the rubber, and let a small stone fly past — not at\nhim, past him. He retreated. The other monkeys took\nnotice. The catapult was not a weapon. It was a\nlanguage. The old man had given me, without saying\nso, the way to converse with that mountain's other\ninhabitants.\nI climbed slowly. The path was steep. The body\nsweated. The breath got short. I sat down often to rest,\nto drink water, to look out at the view as it widened\nbeneath me. The world from up there was small and\ngreen and ancient. The villages below looked the way\nvillages must have looked for centuries. The wind\ncarried the sounds of birds, of distant temple bells, of a\nsingle faint truck horn far away.\n* * *\nI want to tell you why I went, because the why is the\npoint of this chapter.","I did not go for the saint. I will be honest about that.\nI was not, at eighteen, a Sufi seeker. I had heard about\nAbdul Qadir al-Jilani in passing, but I did not know\nmuch about him. I had not memorised his words. I had\nnot chosen him as a teacher.\nI went, instead, for a deeper and simpler reason. I\nwanted to understand why other people go. I wanted to\nknow what was in the act of pilgrimage that had drawn\nhuman beings to mountains for thousands of years. I\nwanted to feel, with my own body, what happens when\na person travels far from their ordinary life to a place\nthat is supposed to be sacred. I was studying the\nphenomenon, in a sense — not from books, but with my\nlegs.\nAnd what I learned, on that climb, on that night in\nthe shelter, on that morning with the catapult and the\nmonkeys, was this. The sacredness was not in the\nlocation. The sacredness was in the going. The fact of\nleaving the ordinary world, of travelling alone, of\naccepting a warm drink from a stranger, of sleeping\nunder a mountain wind, of climbing a path slowly, of\nreceiving a catapult from an old man, of facing\nmonkeys with a piece of bicycle rubber — all of that","was the actual pilgrimage. The shrine at the top was a\ndestination. But the destination was almost incidental.\nThe pilgrimage had already happened in the journey.\nAnd here is the other thing I understood, years later,\nwhen I sat down to think about why I had gone in the\nfirst place. The seed of the trip had been planted by my\nfather, ten or twelve years earlier, when he had brought\nus to that same mountain after returning from the Gulf\nand slipped on a rock. I had watched him slip. I had not\ncommented on it. It had gone into me silently. And\nthen, more than a decade later, when I was eighteen, the\nseed had broken open and produced the decision to go\nalone, at night, with no plan, to the same mountain.\nThat is how the conscious mind plants things in the\ndeeper mind. Things planted in the body, in childhood,\nsurface in their own time. Years pass. The seed waits.\nAnd then, one afternoon, the seed sends up its small\ngreen shoot, and you find yourself buying a bus ticket\nand saying to your father, I want to go. And the father,\nwho planted the seed without knowing he was planting\nit, gives you the blessing without knowing he is\ncompleting the circle. Kun fayakun, in slow motion\nacross twelve years.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER · FLASHBACK\n15\nOluvil — The Forgotten Plate\nThere was a school trip, when I was small, to a beach\ncalled Oluvil. I have told other stories from beaches in\nthis book — the burning boat, the tsunami morning —\nbut Oluvil belongs to a different kind of beach memory.\nOluvil is the memory of hiding.\nI do not remember the exact year. I was somewhere\nin primary school. My father had a strict household rule\nfor trips like this. He would say to all of us — me, my\nbrother, my four sisters at the time, all of us in a row\nunder one blanket on the floor of his room —\nwhichever of you sleeps first, I will take to the beach.\nHe was not joking. He used sleep as discipline. Sleep\nwas the qualifying test. The good child slept on time.\nThe good child therefore deserved the beach the next\nday.\nSo we lay under that one blanket, the six of us, all\ntrying to fake sleep, all of us secretly tense because we\nknew the others were also faking. Whoever's breathing","slowed first, my father would notice. Whoever's body\nrelaxed first, my father would mark in his memory. And\nthe next morning, the names would be announced. The\nones who had genuinely fallen asleep first would go to\nthe beach. The others would stay home.\nI went, on that trip. I had managed to sleep. The\nbody had relaxed early. The breath had slowed. I had\npassed my father's quiet test, and I had earned my place\nin the school van the next morning.\n* * *\nThe trip itself, I remember in fragments.\nThe bus to the beach. The smell of the salt in the air.\nThe other children laughing. Our class teacher — Haron\nsir, our principal, the same man who had given us those\nevening science classes I have written about elsewhere.\nHe was there that day too. I was afraid of him in the\nway schoolboys are afraid of a man whose stillness in a\ncorridor could silence a hundred boys. I saluted him\nwhenever he passed. I lowered my eyes when he spoke.\nHe was not cruel. He was just enormous in the way only\na school principal can be enormous to a small boy.","At lunch time, we were told to find our plates. Each\nof us had been given a small plate at the beginning of\nthe trip. Mine was somewhere in the sand, in the bag, in\nmy hands — and yet, when the moment came to find it\nand queue for food, my plate was nowhere. I could not\nfind it. I checked my bag. I checked the sand around\nwhere we had been sitting. I checked under the towel.\nNothing. The plate had vanished.\nAnd the body did, in that moment, what the body\ndoes when it is small and ashamed. It hid.\n* * *\nI did not tell anyone I had lost my plate. I did not\nraise my hand. I did not ask for a spare one. I did not\napproach Haron sir. The shame of admitting that I had\nfailed at the simple task of keeping a plate was, to my\nsmall mind, larger than the hunger of not eating lunch.\nSo I went behind a coconut tree. A small one, with a\ncurved trunk, casting a little shade. I stood behind it, out\nof sight of the food line, out of sight of the teachers, out\nof sight of my classmates. I told my body to be still. I\ntold my face to look casual, in case anyone saw me. I\nwaited.","When the lunch was over and the other children\nwere licking their fingers and washing their plates in the\nseawater, I came back into view. Someone asked where\nI had been. I said, quickly, that I had gone to wash my\nhands. The lie was small. The lie covered the shame.\nThe watcher in me, even at that age, already knew how\nto construct a story that would let the rest of the body\nsurvive.\nI went hungry that afternoon. The school van took us\nback. I ate nothing until I got home. My mother, when\nshe saw me, may or may not have noticed. I do not\nremember. She fed me when I asked for food. The trip\nwas over.\n* * *\nWhy am I writing this small thing in a book about\nawakening?\nBecause it is one of the earliest examples of a pattern\nthat would shape the next twenty-five years of my life.\nThe pattern of hiding rather than asking. The pattern of\npreferring to be hungry over the discomfort of\nadmitting a failure. The pattern of going behind the\ncoconut tree of every situation, of making my body","small rather than my voice loud.\nI would do this, in different forms, my whole life. I\nwould hide a stolen note from my grandfather instead of\nconfessing. I would hide a burning boat instead of\ntelling my mother. I would hide a relationship in pieces\ninstead of being honest with the woman I was in it with.\nI would hide my arrest from my brother instead of\ncalling him on the first day. The coconut tree changes.\nThe hiding does not.\nAnd the lesson my brother gave me in the prison —\nhe just wants to hear your voice — was the lesson I had\nspent thirty-four years refusing to learn. The Oluvil\nplate could have been replaced. Someone would have\ngiven me a plate. The food was there. The shame was\nsmall. But the boy could not yet bring himself to ask,\nand so the boy went hungry, and the man he became\nrepeated that lesson, in larger and larger versions, until\na cell in Malta finally interrupted the pattern.\nThis is why even small memories matter in a book\nlike this. The boy hiding behind a coconut tree on\nOluvil beach is the same man who, decades later, sat in\na cell because he had spent his whole life hiding rather","than asking. The patterns are laid down early. The\nlessons, when they come, only echo what was already\nthere. The watcher had been watching since the plate\nwent missing. The watcher had been recording. And the\nbook you are reading is the watcher, finally, speaking\nout loud what he had been silently noting for thirty-four\nyears.","ACT THREE — THE ROOFTOP\n16\nAkkan\nThere is a Tamil sound inside the word Akkan that I\nhave carried for years without fully understanding it.\nWhen I was very small, my mother used to call me\nAkkan.\nI do not write this as a fixed dictionary meaning. I\nwrite it as a son's interpretation of a mother's private\nword. My name begins with AK. In Tamil, kan or kann\ncan carry the feeling of eye. I had long, big, bold eyes\nas a child. Maybe she saw those eyes and joined the\nsound of my name with the feeling of the eye. Maybe\nAK became Akkan in her mouth. Maybe that is why the\nname stayed with me.\nBefore the world called me Jabir, before documents\nand courts and passports, my mother had already called\nme by a name that carried my eyes.\nI had forgotten this for years. The eldest-son weight I\nhave written about elsewhere — the ninety rupees, the","responsibility, the bus depot — had taken over my\nmemory of being her son. I had remembered the duty\nand forgotten the small word. The duty was real. The\nsmall word was also real, and the small word was older,\nand the small word was sweeter, and the small word\nwas the foundation that the duty had been built on.\n* * *\nI want to give you one small scene of her that I have\nnot given you yet in this book.\nI was small, maybe seven or eight. I had come home\nfrom school. The afternoon was hot. The house was\nquiet. My sisters were at her side, doing whatever they\nwere doing. I had taken off my school uniform and lay\ndown on the cool floor of the front room. The floor was\nmade of polished cement, the way old houses were\nmade then. In the afternoon, the cement was always\ncool. I lay on my back, looking up at the ceiling, with\nno particular thought in my head.\nMy mother came in. She did not say anything. She\nsat down on the floor beside me. She put her hand on\nmy forehead, the way mothers do to check for a fever,\nbut there was no fever. There was no reason for the","hand. She just put it there. She held it there for a few\nseconds. Then she got up and went back to whatever\nshe had been doing.\nThat is the whole scene. A mother's hand on a boy's\nforehead on a cool cement floor on a hot afternoon, for\na few seconds, with no fever, with no reason, with no\nwords. I do not know why I have remembered this. The\nboy did not understand it at the time. The man, writing\nit now, understands it. She was checking, in the only\nlanguage she had, that he was still hers. That he had not\nyet been taken away by the world. That for one more\nafternoon, he was small, on the floor, in her house,\nwhere she could place her hand on him.\nYears later, the world did take me away. Qatar.\nDubai. Malta. The cell. The accusation. The bail. The\nfirst room. The slow rebuild. The world took her boy a\nlong way from that cool cement floor. But the hand on\nthe forehead is still there in my memory, and on the\ndays I get overwhelmed by the rest of my life, I\nsometimes close my eyes and feel that hand again, and\nthe panic softens. A mother's hand is a kind of channel\ntoo. It does not stop working even when she is far away.\nEven when she is in another country. Even when she","does not know what to say to you on the phone because\nyou have gone somewhere with your life that she does\nnot understand.\n* * *\nShe has prayed for me since the day I was born.\nEvery Fajr, before the village wakes. Every\nMaghrib, when the call comes from the mosque. Every\nIsha, when she lies down to rest. I have not been present\nfor most of those prayers. I have been in other cities,\nother countries, other rooms, on other beds. But the\nprayers have followed me. The prayers have been the\nbluetooth I was not aware of for most of my life. The\nsignal that has been arriving from her, every day,\nwithout my asking. The reason, I now believe, that the\ncell did not break me. The reason the worst week of my\nlife did not become the last week. Somewhere, on the\nother side of the Indian Ocean, an old woman in a small\nhouse was lifting my name to God five times a day, and\nthe lifting did not stop when I forgot to lift my own.\nI want to write something here that I have not said\nout loud to her yet, because we are not a family that\nsays these things easily, and because I want her to find","it first in print, on a page she can hold, so that she can\nread it slowly, alone, the way certain truths should be\nreceived.\nMother. Amma. You carried this family for\ntwenty-six years while my father carried the money\nfrom far away. You raised eight children in a house that\nshould not have been able to hold us. You knew when I\nwas lying. You knew when I was hiding. You knew\nwhen I had stolen from your father's pocket. You knew\nwhen I had burned the boat. You knew every small bad\nthing I did, and you covered most of them with your\nsilence, and you punished the ones that could not be\ncovered, and you never once stopped feeding me. I have\nnot always been the kind of son you deserved. I left. I\nwent to other countries. I called less often than I should\nhave. I hid from you, the way the boy with the lost plate\nhid behind the coconut tree, the way I have hidden from\neveryone who loved me. I am sorry.\nAnd I want to say something my body has been\nwanting to say to you since I was small. I forgot to tell\nyou. I forgot to tell you when I was eight and you asked\nme how the trip to Oluvil was, and I said it was fine,\nand the truth was that I had lost my plate and gone","hungry behind a tree. I forgot to tell you when I was\nthirteen and the boat burned and you came in\nwhispering and beat me out of love. I forgot to tell you\nwhen I was twenty-four and the first arrest in my life\nfelt like it would never end. I forgot to tell you when I\nlanded in Malta in May and the airport closed around\nme and I did not have the courage to call your number\nfor days. I forgot to tell you every important thing\nbecause I thought the forgetting was protection. It was\nnot. It was a wound I gave you over and over, and you\nabsorbed each one, and you kept praying, and you kept\nfeeding, and you did not let any of it stop you.\nSo here, in this book, in front of strangers, I want to\nfinally tell you. I love you. I am sorry. The cool floor of\nthe front room is still in me. The hand on the forehead is\nstill in me. The word Akkan is still in me. And every\nword in this book, in some way, is also a word for you.\nThe book is the long letter I should have written you\nyears ago, and was too small, and too proud, and too\nhidden, to write. Here it is. Read it slowly. Take your\ntime. Your son finally has something to say.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER\n17\nQatar Was the First Leaving\nBefore Dubai, there was Qatar. That matters,\nbecause Dubai was not my first leaving.\nMy first leaving happened in 2016, when I walked\nout of Sri Lanka holding a passport with my own name\non it and entered the world as a man trying to become\nsomething. It was a magnificent thing in my life. For\nthe first time, I left holding proof of who I was. A boy\nwho had once walked to the Old Market with ninety\nrupees was now walking through an international\nairport with a small book in his hand that allowed him\nto cross borders.\nMy father — Mohamed Jabir — was already\ncarrying the weight of the Gulf before me. He had spent\nyears in Saudi Arabia, working away from us so we\ncould live. When I moved to Qatar, he panicked in the\nquiet way fathers panic — not by saying the fear\ndirectly, but by rearranging his own life around it. He\nleft his Saudi path and moved into airport maintenance","work in Qatar so he could be closer, so he could support\na son who had never been abroad before.\nHe was not a man afraid of work. But he was a father\nafraid of his son being alone in a foreign country. He\ndid not say any of this. He simply moved. That is how\nmy father loved. By rearranging the geography of his\nown life around the people he was trying to protect.\nI stayed first with a friend who received me, fed me,\nand gave me a place to exist. His accommodation was\nfar from Doha city, but I did not complain. I was young,\nabroad for the first time, and still learning how to see. I\nshould have been going out with my CV, knocking on\ndoors, submitting applications by hand. Instead, I\napplied online from the room and spent too much time\nobserving the world around me.\n* * *\nAnd I did observe. I remember the cats of Qatar.\nCats on the streets, near buildings, around couches,\nin the shade behind every corner. I would sit and watch\nthem, touch them, study them, as though even the\nanimals of this new country were teaching me that the\nworld was bigger than my own imagination had","allowed.\nSome days I took the bus to Doha city with my\nlaptop bag. The sun pressed down on the metal seats\nand the metal roads and the metal sides of the buses,\nand the waiting itself felt like a test. I had Hindi songs\ndownloaded in long playlists — five hours of music\nstitched together, one after another, without break. I\nwould move through the city with those songs in my\nears. Sometimes I stopped for chai. Often, that chai was\nmy food. It was warm, it was sweet, it filled the space\nwhere the proper meal should have been.\nI told myself I was not hungry. The body knew the\ntruth.\n* * *\nMy father was also struggling in his own way. His\nwork was changing. He came from a practical\nengineering life, the kind where the work happens with\nthe hands. But now, emails and computers and\nkeyboards were beginning to creep into the job.\nI remember his frustration. He told me his fingers\nwere too big for the keyboard, that he could not manage\nit, that the buttons were not made for hands like his. To","someone else, that may sound like a small complaint.\nTo me, it was the image of a man who had carried real\nphysical work for decades, suddenly being asked to\nsurvive through a screen. The hands that had built\nthings, fixed things, tightened things across two\ncountries were now being asked to type. And they could\nnot. Not because they were weak. Because they had\nbeen built for other work.\nSo I told him: calm down, Dad. Give me your email.\nI will help you reply, I will tell you what to do. You\nknow the real work. You guide the technicians. I will\nhandle the computer part.\nThat was one of the first times I understood that a\nson can become a bridge for his father. Not above him.\nNot replacing him. Just translating one world into\nanother. I was small in his eyes for most of my\nchildhood. In Qatar, for the first time, I was useful to\nhim as an adult. That changed something between us\nthat I have not stopped feeling since.\nDuring those months I also understood my mother\ndifferently.","At home, when financial pressure came, she would\nsometimes ask my father for money more than once in a\nmonth. My father would answer with frustration: do\nyou think money comes from a tree? As a child, I could\nnot understand that sentence. In Qatar, I saw its\nmeaning. I saw how one salary had to stretch across a\nwife, eight children, food, school, house, problems, and\nemergencies. If extra money came, maybe it had been\nborrowed. Maybe it carried shame, or another man's\nkindness, inside it.\nThat was when I began to see the real cost of my\nfather's life abroad. And the real cost of my mother's\nlife at home. Twenty-six years, both of them. He paid in\nabsence. She paid in presence. Both of them paid.\n* * *\nEventually I found work in Qatar. The salary was\nvery low. I carried cameras, I climbed, I installed, I did\nthe technical work with my hands. I began to meet\npeople from countries I had only read about. Indian\nfriends. A Filipino colleague. The first time I heard the\nFilipino language, I laughed — not at the speaker, but at\nmy own brain, which simply could not recognise what it","was hearing.\nUntil then, language for me had meant Tamil,\nSinhala, and English. Suddenly there were sounds I\ncould not place, lives I had never imagined. Day by\nday, the unfamiliar became normal. That is one of the\ngifts of leaving. It breaks the smallness of your world. It\nteaches you that your village is not the whole earth, that\nyour language is not the only music, and that your way\nof living is not the only way human beings survive.\nI held my passport in my hand one day and felt\nproud. I had a document with my name on it. I belonged\nto a place. I existed officially. But Qatar also taught me\nrestriction. They were holding my passport. The very\ndocument I had carried with such pride from my own\ncountry was now held inside another system. A passport\nis supposed to mean movement. In that moment, it felt\ninstead like a hand placed gently but firmly on my\nfreedom.\nWhen I went back to Sri Lanka for vacation, I did\nnot return. I did not like the job anymore, and I did not\nlike that feeling of being held. So I stayed home for\nseven months. That is also connected to the time my","mother shouted, and I cried to my father about\nsomething I could not name. And after those seven\nmonths, Dubai came.\nDubai did not begin from nothing. Qatar was the first\ndoor — the first heat, the first loneliness, the first\nforeign bus ride, the first low salary, the first restriction,\nand the first deeper understanding of my father's\nsacrifice.\nQatar was the first leaving. Dubai was the next\nshaping. The body that would one day need to survive\n114 days in a small room was being prepared, country\nby country, country by country, without ever guessing\nwhat it was being prepared for.\nI want to write a Filipino name into this chapter, very\nsmall, almost as a footnote, because the chain matters.\nYears later, in a cell in Malta, a Filipino brother would\nhand me a book that would change my mind. The first\ntime I ever heard a Filipino language, I laughed in a bus\nin Doha because I could not recognise the sounds. The\nlast time I heard it, I was sitting on a small prison bed\nreceiving a gift from a man whose language I could\nnow understand had been quietly setting up a meeting","with me for years.\nThe world arranges these things. We do not arrange\nthem. We only show up to them when they finally\narrive.","My father in Qatar, carrying the distance for all of\nus.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER\n18\nDubai Fed My Body\nDubai began before I fully understood why I had\ngone there.\nAfter Qatar, my body did not want the Gulf again. It\nhad tasted heat, waiting, low salary, restriction, and that\nstrange particular feeling of being abroad but not free.\nMy body remembered all of it. It resisted. But my mind\nsaw Dubai differently. The mind saw opportunity,\nstructure, growth.\nAt the time I did not have the language for it. Now I\ndo. My body was saying no, but my mind had already\naccepted the path. The body and the mind, in those\nyears, did not yet know each other very well. The body\nwas the one in charge, and the mind was the one being\ndragged along — which is the opposite of how a human\nbeing is supposed to work. I would not understand that\nfor several more years. I had to live through it first.\nDubai gave me a system. I entered the world of\nWarner Bros World, the theme park in Abu Dhabi, and","that changed the rhythm of my life. Everything there\nwas built around happiness. Guests came to forget their\nproblems. Staff were expected to smile, guide, support,\nand keep the atmosphere alive. Even as a technician —\ninvisible, behind the scenes, hands on the cables — I\nwas part of that invisible happiness. We handled the\nlights, the audio, the music, the video systems, the\ncameras. The small technical details that made the\nillusion work.\nEverything my body needed was provided.\nAccommodation, free. Food, free. Transport, free.\nGym, free. It felt like living inside a university that also\npaid you. I did not wake up worrying about rent or bills\nor whether I could afford to eat. The system had already\narranged all of it. My survival worries were removed,\nand because of that, my body could focus on\nmovement, on discipline, on routine — the things\nbodies love most.\n* * *\nOn a morning shift, I would wake around four-thirty.\nThe world outside was still quiet, but the day had\nalready begun inside my body.","I wore normal clothes first — if it was winter, full\nclothes; if summer, something lighter. I always carried\na small bag: phone, charger, earphones, ID card, food\ncard, the small things I needed to move through the day.\nI caught the bus around six-fifteen. The bus carried us\ninto the controlled world of the park, where even the\nbeginning of the day had a system written into it.\nWhen I reached the park, I changed into the uniform.\nA grey polo T-shirt. Black cargo pants with many\npockets — I loved those pockets, the way a builder\nloves a good belt. Safety shoes, size forty-four, big and\nheavy on my feet.\nYears earlier, on a beach in Sri Lanka, I had run\nfrom a burning boat in bare feet, sinking into sand that\nwould not let me move. Now, in another country, in\nanother life, my feet were inside size-forty-four safety\nboots that anchored me to the floor of a theme park. The\nbody had been carried, over the years, from one\nextreme of footwear to the other. From bare sand to\nindustrial leather. The same body. Different protection.\nThe body never forgets where it has been, even when\nthe man inside the body is no longer thinking about it.","I had an employee ID, a number that I still\nremember, but it is private, so I keep it to myself. That\nwas how the morning body became the work body. I\nwoke up as myself, carried my small bag, travelled to\nthe gates, and there I changed into the technician\nversion of me.\nFirst came breakfast. The cafeteria food was good —\neggs, sausages, cakes, many things laid out for us. But\nwhen the body receives the same comfort every day,\neven good food becomes boring. The body complains.\nIt says: this has no taste anymore. But the mind should\nknow better. Food is not only taste. Food is nutrition,\ncalories, protein, energy — the quiet material that lets\nthe body continue. The body wants colour, sweetness,\ndesire. The mind must learn to see what the body\nactually needs.\nEven there, in a cafeteria with everything provided, I\nwas beginning to feel the gap between what the body\nwanted and what the mind understood. Still, we rushed.\nBreakfast was eaten quickly, almost swallowed without\nthinking. I never understood why rushing had to be the\nbeginning of the day.","Then the laundry, the uniform, the change, ready.\nMeeting by six-thirty. The day's work assigned.\nStart-of-day checks. System testing. Lights, sound,\nscreens, cameras — everything waking up before the\nguests arrived.\nDuring the day, once the park opened, we mostly\nwalked and observed. If something failed, we\nresponded. If a guest needed guidance, we guided. In a\nstrange way it felt close to a simple human teaching:\nhelp the person in front of you. There I was, being paid\na salary to practise a small version of that service inside\na commercial world.\nThe night shift had another rhythm. After the public\nhours ended, the deeper maintenance work began.\nPreventive. Corrective. Work that could not be done\nwhile the park was alive with visitors. At night the\ntheme park became a different creature. The music\nstopped. The guests disappeared. The smiling world\nbecame machinery, cables, tools, and technicians\nmoving through the skeleton behind the illusion. I liked\nthe night shift for that reason. The illusion was off-duty.\nYou could see the bones.","That is something I have come to value, all the way\nthrough to now. There are two versions of every world\n— the daytime version, full of music and smiling, and\nthe night-time version, full of cables and quiet. Most\npeople only ever see the daytime version. The ones who\nsee both versions understand more than the rest. The\ncell, much later, was the night-shift of my whole life.\nThe lights of my old world had gone off, and\nunderneath I finally saw the cables.\n* * *\nIn 2018, my first name tag said Jabir. The passport\nerror had followed me into the uniform. I waited six\nmonths. Then I paid twenty-five dirhams to have it\nchanged. Akmal. The name my grandfather had given\nme.\nIt is a small thing, but it was the first time I noticed\nmyself choosing my own name. The world had given\nme the wrong one because of a typo decades earlier; I\nwas the one who finally corrected the record.\nTwenty-five dirhams to be myself on my chest. Cheap,\nin the end.","There is a photograph from those years that I still\ncarry in my mind. I am standing in front of a mirror in\nthe staff area. Grey polo. Black cargo. Padlock hanging\nfrom my belt loop, the small black lockout-tagout\npadlock we used to isolate machinery during\nmaintenance. Badge on my chest: AKMAL. Not Jabir\nanymore. Akmal.\nA man who lets the world keep mispronouncing his\nname will eventually forget how to pronounce it\nhimself. That is not poetry. That is what happens. The\nmispronunciation enters the body and the body adjusts.\nI had spent years answering to Jabir because the\ndocuments said so. The day I paid twenty-five dirhams\nto change my badge was the day I quietly reclaimed the\nsmall territory of my own chest. The grandfather who\nhad named me would not have understood the\nbureaucratic detail. But he would have understood the\ngesture. I think he would have nodded.\n* * *\nIt was not suffering in the ordinary sense. There was\nhard work, but also play, friendship, gym, food, beach\nwalks, relationships — a whole life contained inside","that structure. The park was a city within a city. We\nlived inside it, worked inside it, ate inside it, trained\ninside it, and slowly became shaped by it.\nFor four years, Dubai trained my body. It gave me\ndiscipline without making me worry about survival. It\ntaught me routine, service, technical observation, and\nthe habit of showing up. But it also gave me a\ndangerous comfort. Because when everything is\nprovided, the body can become peaceful while the mind\nquietly sleeps.\nDubai fed my body very well. But the deeper work\n— the work of feeding the mind — was still waiting for\nanother place, another silence, another cage.\nAnd one more thing was waiting, though I could not\nsee it then. A photograph I had once taken in Dubai of a\nfalcon, hooded, sitting calmly on a leather glove. The\nbird that could fly anywhere had been trained to hold\nstill until released. I did not know, when I took the\nphotograph, that the image was a small portrait of my\nown future. The body trained for performance. The eyes\ncovered. The waiting. And then, one day, the hood\ncomes off, and the bird sees the sky for the first time in","its real shape.\nThe hood would come off in Malta. The sky I would\nsee, when it came off, would not be a sky I had chosen.\nIt would be a small strip of light through a high cell\nwindow. But the bird would learn, finally, what its own\neyes were for.","Dubai trained my body through uniform, routine,\nand service.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER\n19\nThe Bunk Bed and the Waterfall\nWhen I heard about Malta from a friend, I applied\nonline and got an offer. The pay was modest, the\ncountry was small, and the language was new. But after\nDubai, anything that promised Europe sounded like\nprogress.\nWithin twenty days of starting my new life in Malta,\nI was terminated. The contract simply ended, the way\nsome contracts do — without ceremony, without\nexplanation, without warning.\nThe Gulf panic hit me like a physical blow. Anyone\nwho has worked in the Gulf will recognise the feeling.\nIt is the deep, animal fear of being a man without a job\nin a foreign country — which, in the visa world, means\na man without a country. The body knows it before the\nmind catches up. The heart rate changes. The shoulders\ntighten. You begin to count days against money in a\nway you have not counted since you were a boy.\n* * *","Malta arrived like a time machine going backwards.\nDubai had felt twenty years in the future — gleaming,\npolished, automated. Malta felt twenty years behind Sri\nLanka. Old vehicles on narrow streets. Old stone\nbuildings the colour of warm bread. People not rushing\nanywhere. A post office queue that reminded me of\nbeing fifteen, standing behind my mother while she\npaid a bill in cash.\nFor the first time in years, I had to plan. Wait. Stand\nin line for three hours at a hospital for a small\ncertificate. Carry a ladder. Take a bus that did not\nalways come on time. Speak to people face to face, with\nmy actual mouth, instead of through an app. My body,\ntrained by Dubai comfort, protested all of it. The body\nwanted automation. The body wanted free food. The\nbody wanted to be carried by a system. Malta refused\nall of that. Malta made the body slow down.\nI did not leave because I hated Malta. The truth is\nharder than that. I stayed partly because of shame. What\nwould the people in Sri Lanka say? The boy who left\nfor the Gulf, who worked in Dubai, who finally reached\nEurope — coming back after twenty days because he\ncould not hold a job? Shame is a strong rope. It can","keep a man inside a country longer than any dream can.\nIt kept me.\n* * *\nThen I went to Croatia, and something changed.\nQatar in 2016 had been for survival. Dubai in 2018\nhad been for income. Malta had been for survival again,\nin a different costume. Croatia was different. Croatia\nwas the first time in my life I took a trip just to exist.\nNot to earn, not to escape, not to chase a woman or a\njob. To walk.\nI walked through deep green forests that smelled of\nwet earth and pine. I stood in front of rushing waterfalls\nand felt the spray on my face. I felt my mind go quiet,\non its own, without being forced to. I felt as light as a\nfeather — the lightness people promise you in\nadvertisements but rarely deliver. For a few days, I was\nnot running from anything and not running toward\nanything. I was just there. The body was still moving,\nbut it was no longer the master. Something else, quieter,\nhad begun to walk beside it.\nThat was the first time, since the flight on which I\nhad heard Surah Al-Baqarah with its English","translation, that I had felt the watcher take up a little\nmore space inside me. The two experiences — the flight\nand the waterfall — were the same experience, in two\ndifferent settings. Both were forced stillness. Both let\nthe watcher breathe. Both were practice for the deeper\nstillness that was still ahead, though I could not have\nnamed it as practice at the time.\nI did not know yet that the real stillness was not\nwaiting in Croatia. It was waiting inside a prison cell,\neighteen months later, in a country whose language I\ndid not speak. Croatia gave me the taste. The cell would\ngive me the full meal.\n* * *\nWhen I came back to Malta, the heavy reality of\nsurvival was waiting at the door. The waterfall feeling\nlasted three days, maybe four. Then the bills, the visa,\nthe room, the job — the whole heavy machinery of\nstaying — pressed itself back onto my shoulders.\nA friend named Ram Alam threw me a lifeline. Do\nnot go back to Dubai, he said. Come to my room. I live\nwith three Indian guys. Take a bunk bed and pay two\nhundred and fifty euros a month. We will figure it out.","I agreed. And in agreeing, I stepped into the invisible\ncage of the migrant survivor.\nCrammed in a room with strangers. Stripped of\nprivacy. My existence reduced to applying for jobs,\ngoing to the gym, calling home, and sleeping. The room\nhad its own particular weight. The smell of four men\nsharing a small space — clean enough but never fresh.\nThe fan running through the hot nights. Phone chargers\ntangled near every bed. Clothes drying on the back of\nchairs. Someone always snoring. Someone always\nwhispering on a phone call back to a wife or a mother in\nanother country at the wrong hour for Malta but the\nright hour for India.\nThe men I moved in with had been there four years.\nThey were surviving. They were paying their rent. They\nwere sending money home. But spiritually, they were\nasleep. One of them said something to me one night that\nI have never forgotten. He said: I did not come here to\nchange you. I came to fix myself. That was honest. That\nwas the whole migrant truth in one sentence. We come\nhere for ourselves. The deeper change is not part of the\ncontract.","Through those conversations, and through the clarity\nI had carried home from Croatia, I secured a new job.\nFrom the outside, that looked like success. From the\ninside, I knew something was wrong. I call it entropy\nnow — the slow, almost invisible cooling of a person's\ndiscipline. Over two years in that bunk bed, I absorbed\nthe stagnant energy of that room. My gym dropped off.\nMy reading dropped off. My discipline faded one small\nhabit at a time, the way paint flakes from a wall.\nLife will always offer you a challenge. If you face it,\nyou cross over to the next stretch of your path. If you\navoid it, you freeze. I froze. And because I would not\nlisten in the bunk bed — because the room was loud\nenough and comfortable enough to keep me asleep — I\nhad to be sent somewhere quieter to finally hear.\nI had to be sent to a place where there would only be\none bed. One man. One window. One direction of wind.\nThe bunk bed had been crowded enough to drown out\nthe watcher. The room that was coming would be quiet\nenough that the watcher would, finally, be impossible to\nignore. I just did not know yet where that room was, or\nwhat I would have to do to be moved into it.","ACT ONE — THE MESSENGER\n20\nThe Horse and the Rider\nBefore the cell, before the silence, I was a man\ngoverned by the architecture of my own skin.\nI lived in what I now call body love. The pump of\nthe gym in the morning. The heat of physical work in\nthe afternoon. The taste of food. The pull of a\nrelationship in the evening. The release of sleep at\nnight. My whole life was a sequence of sensations the\nbody was chasing, and I was calling that life.\nI was like a man riding a powerful horse at full\ngallop, never realising the strange truth — that I was\nnot the rider. I was the horse itself. Driven by instinct.\nDriven by appetite. Driven by the next thing the body\nwanted. The mind, which was meant to hold the reins,\nwas somewhere far behind, calling out instructions the\nhorse could no longer hear.\n* * *","The collapse began on the sixteenth of March, 2024.\nTwo-thirty in the afternoon.\nI had begun to suspect something was wrong inside\nthe relationship I was in. The body had been ignoring\nthe signs for weeks because the body did not want to\nknow. But that afternoon I went looking for the truth,\nthe way men go looking when their stomachs already\nknow what they are going to find.\nI found her at the MCAST car park in Mosta with\nanother man. I will call him J. I will not say more about\nhim because the point of this chapter is not him. The\npoint is what happened inside me when I saw them.\nEverything inside me fractured. My heart, my\nbreath, my eyes — everything I call by the deep Tamil\nword Yekkam, that word for longing and distress and a\nkind of pleading the body cannot stop — all of it pushed\nme toward her at once. I did not have the calm to walk\naway. I did not have the watcher in me strong enough\nyet to hold me back. I went toward her with my whole\nbroken animal self.\nDo you not feel guilty, I asked her.\nYes, she said. Guilty.","Half an hour later, I was standing in a Lidl\nsupermarket in Mosta. I do not remember walking\nthere. The body had carried me on autopilot, the way it\ndoes when the mind shuts down. The supermarket lights\npressed down white and sterile from the ceiling. The\ncold plastic of the water bottles felt wrong against my\nhand. My throat was so dry it felt like scorched earth,\nlike an old field after a fire. I needed water and I could\nnot even decide which water to pick up. When she\ncalled my phone, I blamed her for my thirst, for my\npain, for my dryness. I was still looking for someone\nelse to hold the weight of my life. I had not yet\nunderstood that no one can hold that weight for you, no\nmatter how much they love you, no matter how much\nthey hurt you.\n* * *\nOn the nineteenth of March, I booked a flight to\nRomania. I told myself it was to forget everything. The\ntruth was that I was running.\nI arrived in Romania on the sixteenth of March, and I\nstayed there for about a week — until around the\ntwenty-third or twenty-fourth. The country was new","and old at the same time. Cold air. Stone churches.\nPeople speaking a language I could not catch. I walked\nthrough cities whose names I did not know. I sat in\ncafés where I did not speak. I tried to stop thinking\nabout Malta. I could not.\nThen I went to Dubai. I stayed there until early May.\nAlmost six weeks in total. I told myself Dubai had been\nmy old comfort, my body's old home. I thought\nreturning to the city that had once held me would help\nme heal. But even there, my mind kept turning back\ntoward Malta.\nThere is a Tamil saying my elders used: ikkaraikku\nakkarai pachchai. The grass is greener on the other side.\nThe other shore always looks more beautiful than the\none you are standing on. I had spent my whole life\nrunning toward the other shore. Now, sitting in Dubai, I\nrealised that if I stayed there, I would be running\nforever. I would become my father in 1999, leaving and\nleaving and leaving, year after year, becoming a man\nwho lived everywhere and nowhere.\nI chose to return to Malta and face it. I thought, in\nthat moment, that I had finally become the rider. That","the mind had taken hold of the reins at last. That I was\nno longer the horse.\nI was wrong. I had not yet been tested. I had only\nmade a decision. There is a long distance between a\ndecision and the practice that proves a decision was\nreal. The cage was already opening its mouth, and I did\nnot see it.\n* * *\nThe morning of the sixth of May was a chaotic blur\nof heat and disconnection.\nMy Dubai SIM had expired, cutting me off from the\nworld at the exact moment I needed a lifeline. I hauled\ntwo heavy suitcases down to the street, sweating\nthrough my shirt, looking for the taxi I had ordered. I\ncould not find it. I dragged the bags back up to my\nfriend Muhammad Inshaf's apartment just to catch a\nfew seconds of his Wi-Fi to confirm the booking. I was\ndrenched. The shirt was wet through. The body was\nalready in panic mode and the day had barely started.\nIn that panic, I did something I had never done in\nthirty-four years of putting on clothes in the morning.\nFor no logical reason at all, I put on two pairs of","underwear.\nI did not understand why I was doing it. The mind\ndid not approve it. The body simply did it.\nLooking back now, I see something I could not see\nin that moment. I was unconsciously preparing my body\nfor a place where I would have nothing else. I was\npacking survival onto my own skin, not into my\nluggage. The body, in some ancient way, already knew.\nBy the time I reached Rome airport for the layover,\nthe smell of my own fear was unbearable. The shirt was\nsoaked. The body had been sweating for hours. I went\ninto a bathroom, ripped the T-shirt off, and threw it into\na terminal dustbin. I changed into something cleaner\nfrom my bag. I caught the next flight. I messaged\nSaraswati from the air: I will be there in fifty minutes.\nCan you bring fifteen euros for the taxi?\nAnd on that final flight, with Surah Al-Baqarah\nplaying quietly in my ears for the second time in my life\n— the surah my grandfather Mohammed Ismail had\nwalked me through the dark to receive when I was a\nsmall boy too young to understand — I sat there\nworried about a taxi fare. Worried about a court date I","did not yet know was coming. I did not understand,\nthen, that the double layer of underwear was the only\nreal preparation I had brought with me for the stillness\nabout to begin.\nThe plane began its descent. The lights of Malta\ncame up below the wing. The runway came closer. The\nwheels touched. The cabin lights came on.\nAnd somewhere in the corridor of that small Maltese\nairport, two men in plain clothes were already walking\ntoward the gate, holding a piece of paper with my name\non it. I would not see them for another twelve minutes.\nBut they were there. They had been arranged.\nEverything had been arranged. The horse was running\ninto the stable it had been bred for, and the rider, asleep\nin the saddle, was about to be woken in a way that no\nhorse, in any field, has ever been able to wake itself.","The last smile before Malta, before the arrest, before\nthe collapse I could not yet see.","","Switzerland - one of the countries I crossed before\nthe silence.","ACT TWO — THE CAGE\n21\nAre You Akmal?\nThe break did not begin in a courtroom. It began at\nan airport, in the ordinary middle of the night, in the\nstrange space between one life and the next.\nThe plane landed in Malta the way planes always\nland — the bump, the slow taxi, the cabin lights coming\nup, the bodies around me reaching for their bags. I was\ntired. I was thinking about the taxi. I was thinking about\nSaraswati waiting for me with fifteen euros. I was\nthinking about getting home, getting clean, getting to\nsleep. The body wanted simple things. The body did not\nyet know.\nI walked through the corridor toward immigration.\nThe familiar smell of the airport — cool air, polished\nfloor, the slight stale smell of long flights and coffee\nmachines. My footsteps echoed. Somewhere behind me\na child was crying. Somewhere ahead an announcement\nrepeated in three languages. The world was being the\nworld.","And then, in the middle of that ordinary walk, two\nmen stepped toward me. They were in plain clothes —\nno uniforms, no badges visible. They asked my name.\nAre you Akmal?\nThat was the first sentence. Are you Akmal? Two\nwords and my name. The body answered before the\nmind could decide.\nYes.\n* * *\nThey brought out papers. The first ones were in\nNepali, because they had assumed, from a quick look at\nmy face, that I was from Nepal. I told them, calmly, that\nI was from Sri Lanka and asked for the English copy.\nEven then — even in that moment when the floor was\nalready beginning to disappear under my feet — some\npart of my mind still tried to act normal. Read carefully.\nStay calm. Be respectful. Surely this is a mistake.\nSurely this will clear itself in a few minutes. Surely they\nwill hand me back my passport and I will walk out and\nmeet Saraswati and pay the taxi.\nThey handed me the English copy. I started to read.","And something inside me disappeared.\nI still do not know exactly what to call it. Soul. Ego.\nInnocence. Identity. The names do not quite fit. I only\nknow that the version of me who had boarded the plane\nin Dubai did not survive the reading of that paper. He\ndropped out of my body in seconds, like a passenger\nstepping off a train at the wrong station. He was gone,\nand someone else was standing in his shoes, holding the\npaper, reading words about himself that he did not\nrecognise.\nI am not going to write the words of the accusation\nhere. They are not the point of this book, and they\nbelong inside a different conversation, with my lawyer,\nin a different room. But I will say this: when you read\nsomething serious about yourself in a sterile police\ndocument at an airport, you understand for the first time\nthat the world has been keeping records about you that\nyou had no access to. There is a version of you that\nexists on paper, in someone else's writing, and that\nversion can come to your life and replace the one you\nthought you were.","My passport was taken. My phone. My belongings.\nThe body was searched. The world I had been walking\nthrough five minutes ago was on the other side of a door\nthat had already closed.\n* * *\nThen came the simple, practical sequence. Years\nlater, when people ask me to describe it, I always tell it\nthe same way, because the simplicity of it is part of the\ntruth.\nFirst, the airport check. The reading. The paper. The\nyes that I had already given.\nThen they took my luggage. The suitcases I had\nhauled down to the street in Dubai that morning, the\nones I had sweated through my shirt to manage, the\nones I had thought about all the way through the Rome\nlayover — they were lifted away from me. I was told\nthey would be sent to my room, wherever my room was\ngoing to be. I did not yet know that my room would be\ninside a place called Floriana, in a building called the\nJoint Headquarters.\nThen I was moved. The two men walked me out of\nthe immigration area through doors I had not seen on","my way in. There was a vehicle. I do not remember the\nexact colour, the exact model. The body had stopped\nrecording small details and was recording only the\nessentials. Doors opened. Doors closed. A road moved\npast a window. Then another building.\nFloriana. The Joint Headquarters.\nThe first thing they asked for there were the personal\nitems — the things a man wears on his body but that do\nnot stay with him when he enters a place like that. I\ngave them my watch. I gave them my wallet. I gave\nthem my belongings. Small things. Items I had not\nthought about as significant ten hours earlier in a Dubai\napartment, when I was deciding what to pack. Now\neach one was being taken and entered into a list. The\nwatch I had checked the time on for years. The wallet\nwith my cards. The small everyday objects that\nconfirmed to me, hour by hour, that I was a normal\nperson in a normal life. All of them lifted away into\nenvelopes.\nThen they let me inside.\n* * *","Inside there were blocks. The building was sectioned\ninto smaller sections, each one with its own kind of\nholding space. Toilets. Benches to sit on while they\ndecided what would happen next. I sat on one of those\nbenches for a long time. The wood — or maybe it was a\nkind of hard plastic, I am not sure now — pressed into\nthe back of my legs. My body was tired. My mind was\nloud. The benches were not made for comfort. They\nwere made for waiting.\nThat same day, on the day of arrival itself, they did\nthe medical scan. I did not understand at the time why it\nwas being done so quickly. Later I would understand\nthat it was protocol. They needed to know, for their own\nfiles, the state of the body that was about to enter the\nsystem. The body that had walked into Malta as a free\nman's body, an hour before, was now being checked for\na record that would follow it into whatever came next.\nThe body had become evidence.\nThen more waiting. More benches. Officers moving\npast me with papers, with radios, with the small\neveryday sounds of an institution doing its night work.\nSome of them looked at me. Most of them did not. I\nwas a unit of process to them, not a man. That is not","cruelty. That is the way institutions are built. The\ncruelty is in pretending it is anything else.\nMy lawyer came in the morning. I felt hope the way\ndrowning people feel the sight of land — that animal,\nirrational hope that simply seeing help means help has\narrived. He spoke. I listened. I asked questions. He\nanswered some, and the rest he answered with the\ncareful silence of a man who has done this work too\nmany times to promise anything.\nAnd then they told me there would be no release.\nNot that day. Not soon. The papers were already\nmoving. The court was already scheduled. The cage\nwas already prepared.\n* * *\nThat was when the fear became existential. The\nearlier shock had been the shock of the accusation. The\nnew fear was something different, and deeper. It was\nthe fear of being unprepared.\nDeeply, humiliatingly unprepared.\nAs if my whole mind turned to my whole body, in\nsome quiet inner room, and said something I had spent","thirty-four years refusing to hear.\nYou never gave me time.\nYou fed yourself. You ran yourself through years of\neffort. You went to the gym. You earned salaries. You\ncrossed countries. You chased women. You ate good\nfood. You watched the world. And in all of it, you never\ngave me — your own mind — any time to prepare you\nfor this. And now here we are. And I am not ready.\nI sat on that bench in Floriana, in the Joint\nHeadquarters, and I understood, more clearly than I had\never understood anything, that I had failed myself.\nNot in some moral sense. Not because of the\naccusation. Not because of what other people thought. I\nhad failed myself in the most basic way a human being\ncan fail himself. I had built the body and I had\nabandoned the mind. And now the body was about to be\nlocked inside walls where it could do nothing, and only\nthe mind would be left to keep me alive. And the mind\nwas a stranger to me.\nThat was the real beginning of the book you are\nholding. Not the prologue. Not the childhood. This\nbench. This realisation. This silence after the lawyer","left.\n* * *\nThere was one more small detail I want to write here,\nbecause it would matter later, and because I want to be\nhonest about the smallness of it.\nIn my hands, on that bench, I was still holding a\nsmall amount of money. I had exchanged some\ncurrency at the Rome airport. About four hundred and\nthirty euros, give or take. I had thought I would need it\nfor the taxi, for the first weeks in Malta, for small\nnecessities. They had let me keep some of it temporarily\n— or maybe I had simply not yet handed it over. The\nexact moment when I gave it up has blurred. But I\nremember holding it for a while in that holding area,\nlooking at the paper notes, understanding for the first\ntime what money is and is not. Most of that money\nwould go to my lawyer in the weeks ahead. I would\nkeep about ten euros for myself. The rest would buy me\nhours of legal time that I could not buy in any other\nway.\nMoney, on the night I was arrested, was not safety.\nMoney was just a slower way of buying minutes from a","lawyer. The man who had worked for years in Dubai\nfor a salary was now sitting on a Floriana bench,\nwatching his own money become a different kind of\nobject — a temporary loan, made to himself, to buy\nsmall pieces of representation in a system that did not\ncare how hard the salary had been earned.\nThen they moved me again. The second vehicle. The\nsecond road. Through the small Maltese streets in the\ndark, past closed shops and quiet cafés, past walls of old\nyellow stone that had stood for centuries before any of\nthis began. To a place I had only ever heard of in\npassing, on local news, as the name a Maltese person\nuses when they say someone has gone away.\nPaola.\nAnd the metal door at the entrance of Paola Prison\nbegan, very slowly, to open.","ACT TWO — THE CAGE\n22\nThe First Thing Prison Stripped\nAway\nThey moved me to Paola Prison, and Paola did not\nfeel like a modern institution. It felt ancient.\nThe stone was old in a way that modern buildings do\nnot know how to be old. You could feel the weight of\ndecades of suffering pressing in from every direction —\nfrom the walls, from the floor, from the air itself. Some\nbuildings hold what has happened inside them. Paola is\none of them. The yellow Maltese limestone outside\nsoftens the look of it for visitors and tourists. The inside\ndoes not soften.\nThe intake process is designed to remove you from\nyourself, piece by piece. I do not think the officers see it\nthat way. I think for them it is a procedure, a checklist, a\ndaily duty. But for the man going through it, every step\nis a small subtraction. Sign here. Empty your pockets.\nRemove this. Remove that. Stand here. Look there.\nTurn around. Open your mouth. The body, which an","hour ago was your private property, becomes a thing\nthey are examining.\nThey removed my clothes. They checked every part\nof my body. Photographs from the front, from the side.\nFingerprints — and only my right hand worked\nproperly, because the left thumb had an old cut.\nEverything was body. My mind did not interest anyone\nin that room. My mind could have been doing anything.\nThe room was only interested in the surface of me.\nThey gave me a number. SRK0003. I will not forget\nit. Numbers given to you in places like that go into a\npart of memory that does not erase.\n* * *\nFor the first three days and nights, they placed me in\nthe dark holding block. The lights were dim or off most\nof the time. Two metal beds in a small cell. I was on\ntop. Below me was a Libyan Muslim man named Ham.\nThe space between the beds and the toilet was barely a\nfoot wide. You had to turn sideways to move past the\nbed.\nThe smell of urine was constant — not from neglect\nexactly, just from the design of a small old cell where","the toilet is part of the room. The blanket was a heavy,\nitchy purple-grey wool. I recognised the texture of it.\nThe same kind of blanket had been distributed to\nfamilies in eastern Sri Lanka after the 2004 tsunami, by\nrelief organisations. The boy who had made paratha on\nthe morning of the wave had once slept under that kind\nof blanket as a refugee. Now the man he had become\nwas sleeping under it as a prisoner. The blanket had\ncrossed twenty years and an ocean to find me again.\nThe cell opened one hour a day. For the rest of the\ntwenty-three, you were inside. I barely ate. The food\narrived through a slot and sat there. The body did not\nwant it. The shock was still moving through me, and the\nthroat could not accept anything heavier than water. I\ndrank shower water and the small carton of milk they\ngave each day. For the first three days, that was almost\neverything.\n* * *\nBut the first thing prison truly stripped away from\nme was not comfort. It was not food. It was not freedom\nin the physical sense.\nThe first thing prison stripped away was identity.","I had walked in as Akmal — a technician, a son, a\nbrother, a man with a job, a phone, a female friend, a\npassport, friends, a gym membership, a future booking\non a flight to somewhere. I had walked in as a man with\na story about himself. By the end of the second day, all\nof that was gone. I was a body in a cell with a number.\nThe story was on the other side of a wall I could not see\nover.\nAnd inside that loss, another realisation appeared,\nquieter and stranger. It said, very softly: maybe the\nidentity you thought you had was never fully built in the\nfirst place. Maybe it was thinner than you knew. Maybe\neverything you called yourself — the job title, the\nmuscles, the relationships, the migration story — was a\nscaffolding the world had built around you and called\nyou. And when the world removed the scaffolding, you\nwere surprised to discover how little of you was\nactually inside it.\nPrison did not only take identity from me. It exposed\nhow fragile identity had been. That is a different kind of\nloss. It is the loss of an illusion you did not know you\nwere holding. And once you see through it, you cannot\nun-see it, even later, when the world tries to hand you a","new identity to wear.\nIt was, in a strange way, the same lesson the boy on\nthe beach had been given on the day the boat burned. A\nscaffolding can disappear in seconds. The fire there had\ntaken the boat. The fire here was taking the man I had\nthought I was. Same teacher. Different country. Twenty\nyears apart.\n* * *\nHam became the first human bridge inside that\ndarkness.\nWe began with fear, of course. Two strangers, two\nlanguages, two religions almost the same and not quite,\nlocked in a space the size of a closet. Both of us\nsuspicious of the other in the first hours. Both of us\ndoing the small calculations the mind does in an\nunfamiliar room: who is this person, what does he want,\nwhat will he take.\nBut fear and confinement make human beings speak\ndifferently. There is no room for performance in a cell\nthat small. Within a day, we were talking. He was older\nthan me, harder than me, more familiar with these walls\nthan I would ever be. He had a long history with this","kind of place. He carried the calm of a man who had\nstopped fighting the situation a long time ago. He\nasked, in a low voice once, what had happened. I told\nhim a little. He listened. Then he gave me the only\npractical advice he had to give.\nHe said: do not speak too much in here. The camera.\nThe microphone. They are listening. Read the surah.\nAnd sleep.\nRead the surah and sleep. Six words. It was not a\nsermon. It was a survival instruction from one prisoner\nto another, given quietly in the dark. He reminded me\nthat God was still accessible. That I was not as alone as\nthe walls were trying to make me feel. That the Qur'an\nmy grandfather had walked me through at four in the\nmorning when I was a small boy was still, after all these\nyears, the thing I could turn to when there was nothing\nelse.\nI did what he said. I lay on the top bunk. I closed my\neyes. I recited the verses I knew without paper. I slept\nsome. I woke some. The hours moved. The cell stayed\ndark. Below me, Ham breathed.","The first human bridge inside the cage was a Libyan\nthief who told me to read the surah and sleep. I have\nnever forgotten him. I will not forget him.\n* * *\nAfter three days in the dark block, they moved me\nagain.\nThis time the corridor was a little brighter. The walls\nwere the same old stone, but the air felt different. We\nwalked. An officer with a key. My small bag of\nbelongings beside me — what little I was being allowed\nto keep. We turned. We passed a heavy gate. We passed\na desk where another officer made a note in a book. We\nturned again.\nDivision 8.\nThat was the name of the section I was being moved\ninto. Inside Paola, the prison is broken into divisions —\nseparate sections, each with its own corridor, its own\nofficers, its own rules. Division 8 was where I would\nlive for the next stretch of my life. And inside Division\n8, the cells were numbered.","The officer stopped in front of a door. He turned the\nkey. The door opened.\nCell thirteen.\nThat was the room. Mine. Alone. No bunk this time.\nNo Ham below me. No second body in the space. Just\none bed. One small table. One toilet. One window. And\none man, who at that moment did not yet know that the\ndoor closing behind him was, in some strange way, the\nbeginning of the only stillness his soul had ever been\ngiven. The watcher who had waited his whole life to be\nheard was about to get the room he had always needed.\nThe door closed. The lock turned.\nAnd I sat down on the edge of the bed.","ACT TWO — THE CAGE\n23\nCell 13\nCell 13 was different from the dark block.\nSmall, yes. But it had what the first place did not\nhave. It had a window. A real window — narrow, high,\nbut a window. It had a bed that was mine alone, not\nshared, not stacked. It had a small table where a man\ncould put a cup, a book, a pen. It had a toilet I did not\nhave to share with another body. A blanket. A pillow.\nAfter the dark block, these things did not feel like\nprison. They felt like mercy.\nMy body settled almost immediately. That surprised\nme. The body, which had been in shock for days,\nsimply lay down on the bed and accepted the room. In\nDubai I had lived in rooms about this size — staff\naccommodation, simple, sufficient. Something inside\nthe body said quietly: you know how to exist here. The\nwalls did not frighten the body. The narrowness did not\nfrighten the body. The body had been trained, without\nmy realising it, by every small room I had ever slept in","across three countries.\nAnd once the body stopped fighting the\nenvironment, the mind finally had room.\n* * *\nThrough the window I could see an old structure in\nthe distance.\nI never knew for certain what it was. Maybe a\nchurch. Maybe a mosque. Maybe some old building\nfrom the Knights' time that had become offices. I never\nasked anyone, because the not-knowing felt like a kind\nof company. A shape against the sky. Something out\nthere. I could also see the sky itself — a strip of it, blue\nor grey or pink depending on the hour. I watched the\nsky change for hours. I had never watched the sky\nchange for hours before. I had been too busy walking\nunder it.\nSome days the wind came through the window.\nSome days it did not. I began to notice the direction of\nthe wind. When it came from one side, I felt it on my\nface. From another, nothing. That distinction — the\ndifference between feeling the wind on the right side of\nthe face or not feeling it at all — began to matter to me.","I do not know how to explain to a free person why such\na thing should matter. I can only say that when a man\nhas nothing to do, and nothing to chase, and nothing to\nfix, the wind becomes a real visitor. You notice when it\ncomes. You notice when it leaves.\nSmall things became meaningful in ways they never\nhad been in my outside life. The pattern a piece of light\nmade on the floor when it came through the window\nslot. The colour of the water in the cup at different\ntimes of day. The faint sound of birds — there were\nbirds, somewhere, on the other side of the wall, going\nabout a whole life I could only hear. Appreciation, I\ncame to understand, is what the mind turns to when it\nfinally has time and the body has nothing left to chase.\nFree people do not appreciate the wind, because they\nare walking through it on the way to something else.\nThe wind, for them, is an interruption. For me, it\nbecame a teacher.\nI counted stars on the nights when I could see them. I\nwatched birds when they crossed the strip of sky. I\nnoticed everything that moved. The watcher in me, the\none who had been with me since I was a boy on a roof\nin Sri Lanka watching his mother catch his brother's","fall, finally had the conditions he had been waiting for\nhis whole life. No movement. No errand. No phone.\nNothing to chase. Just looking.\n* * *\nLet me give you one ordinary day in Cell 13,\nbecause the days were one of the things the cell taught\nme.\nAround six in the morning, the small slot in the door\nopens. The officer's voice. Buongiorno. The Italian\ngreeting — most of the older Maltese officers still used\nit instead of English. A small ritual, less than two\nseconds long. The body inside the cell is confirmed\nalive and accounted for. I would lift my head, answer,\nand lie back down for a few more minutes.\nThen I would get up slowly. The body had learned,\nby then, that there was no reason to rush. I would walk\nthe two steps to the small basin. I would wash my face.\nThe water in Paola was always slightly cold, even in\nsummer. I would dry my face with a small piece of\ncloth that doubled as my towel.\nThen breakfast came. A simple thing. Bread,\nsometimes a small piece of cheese, sometimes a small","carton of milk, sometimes coffee in a plastic cup. I ate\nslowly. I had learned, by then, that the slower I ate, the\nlonger the meal lasted, and the more the body received\nit. The Dubai cafeteria mornings, where I had\nswallowed eggs and cakes without tasting them, came\nback to me in those slow Paola breakfasts. I had not\nunderstood food in Dubai. I was beginning to\nunderstand it now.\nAfter breakfast, the first bell. The cell door opened\nfor the one-hour walk in the yard, when the schedule\nallowed it. The body needed that hour. I would walk in\nslow circles around the small open space. I would\nbreathe. I would watch the other men. Some of them\npaced. Some of them smoked. Some of them stood\nquietly in a corner, staring at the wall. Each man's body\ntold you, without his saying anything, what stage of his\nsentence he was in.\nThen back inside. The door closed. The lock turned.\nThe next stretch began.\nThe middle of the day was thinking. Reading, when I\nhad something to read. Reciting, when I did not.\nWriting, once I had a pen and paper. The light from the","window moved across the floor in a long slow arc. I\ntracked it. The colour of it changed from pale white to\nwarm yellow to soft orange depending on the season\nand the hour. At certain times of the day the strip of\nlight hit the wall in a way that almost looked like a\ndoorway. Almost. I would sit on the bed and watch it\nmove.\nLunch came. Then a smaller second bell. Then more\nthinking. More reading. More waiting. Sometimes the\ncall bell — the one that meant a phone call had been\narranged for me — would ring, and I would stand and\nwait for my name. Sometimes the bell did not come.\nThen I would lie down and breathe, and watch the light,\nand let the hours pass.\nEvening. Last bell. The night version of the prison\nbegins. The corridors quiet. The officers fewer. The\nvoices through the walls begin to drift cell to cell.\nSomeone laughs. Someone hums. Someone speaks\nsoftly in a language I do not know to a person in\nanother cell across the corridor.\nThen sleep. The single thin blanket. The small\npillow. The mind, finally tired enough to release the","body into sleep. And the breath, my breath, the same\nbreath I had been taking every day since the day I was\nborn, continuing without my management, all night,\nuntil the slot in the door opened again and another\nbuongiorno arrived.\nThat was a day. Many days were like this. Many. I\nhad never had days that looked like this in my whole\nadult life. I did not understand, at first, that these slow\ndays were the gift. I thought they were the punishment.\nThe mistake took me weeks to correct.\n* * *\nThe thinking started slowly, and then it did not stop.\nIn the first days, the thinking was practical.\nPlanning. Problem-solving. How do I get out. How do I\nget money to my brother. How do I tell my mother.\nHow do I find a lawyer who is not a fraud. How do I\nsurvive tomorrow. The mind was still running the old\nprogrammes, still treating itself like a logistics manager.\nBut practical thinking can only fill so many hours.\nAfter enough days, the planning ran out of new\nmaterial. The same problem cannot be re-thought a\nhundred times without the thinking becoming","something else.\nAnd that is when the other thinking began. The kind\nof thinking that happens when the body is no longer\nrunning the programme and the mind is finally allowed\nto do what it was made to do.\nHours passed in thought. Six. Eight. Ten. Sometimes\nI would lie down on the bed at one moment and look up\nat the ceiling, and the next time I noticed the light, it\nhad moved across the room. I had not been asleep. I had\nbeen thinking. About what? About everything. About\nmy childhood. About the cow I threw the stone at.\nAbout the boat that burned. About my grandfather's\ncough at three in the morning. About my mother bent\nunder the weight of my brother's fall. About the woman\nI had been chasing. About the religion I had been\nreciting without understanding. About my father's back\non the motorbike, the ghost I used to follow to the\nmarket. About my own mind — what it had been doing\nfor thirty-four years and what it was doing now.\nFirst the problem. Then the people around the\nproblem. Then the love that had carried me into the\nproblem. Then the body that had carried me into the","love. Then the mind that had been ignored through all\nof it. The thinking moved inward in stages, like a man\nwalking deeper into a house he has owned for years but\nnever fully explored.\n* * *\nFrom that long inner labour, one sentence rose\nclearly. It was not given to me by a teacher. It was not\nin any of the books that came later. It came up on its\nown, the way certain truths do when there is nothing in\nthe way of them.\nFrom today, you think to live. Not live without\nthinking.\nThat was the first true shift. Before any deeper\nunderstanding. Before the Qur'an opened differently.\nBefore the Bible arrived. Before Conversations with\nGod landed in my hands from a Filipino brother. The\nreal turning point in Cell 13 was that one sentence,\ngiven to me by my own mind, in my own voice, when\nthere was finally nothing else competing for my\nattention.\nFrom today, you think to live. Not live without\nthinking.","I repeated it for hours. I did not know yet how much\nof the rest of this book — and the rest of my life —\nwould be built from that single line. The body had\nfinally been still long enough for the mind to write me a\nmessage I could not ignore.\nA man named Saheed would soon arrive in the\ndivision and stay for a few days. He would give me a\nQur'an in Arabic that I still carry today, in 2026, on a\nsmall shelf in my apartment. A Norwegian man named\nThomas would soon hand me a broken pen and three\npages torn from his own notebook. A Filipino brother\nwould soon give me Conversations with God. A man\nnamed Mike would soon, in another part of the prison,\nbegin teaching me physics and English. A man named\nRay would soon become the kind of friend that prison\noccasionally produces but the outside world rarely does.\nA young man named Jesmond would soon write me a\nletter I would not be able to answer. Each of them was\non their way to me, the way people are on their way to\neach other when the universe has already arranged the\nroom. I did not know any of their names yet. I would\nlearn them, one by one, in the slow days that came next.","For now, on this day, in Cell 13, the door was closed.\nThe light was moving across the floor. The wind was\ncoming from the side that touched my face. And one\nsentence had been written in my own mind that would,\nin the years that followed, become the title of a chapter,\nthe spine of a book, and the way out of a life I had been\nliving too long.\nFrom today, you think to live.\nNot live without thinking.\nThe prison days, before silence turned inward and\nbecame a voice.","ACT TWO — THE CAGE\n24\nThe Sound of Time\nIn Cell 13, I did not tell time by looking at a clock.\nThere was no clock. I told time by listening.\nFree people do not realise how much of their day is\norganised by sight. The phone. The watch. The clock on\nthe wall. The screen at the bus stop. The numbers on the\noven. The little time-tellers everywhere, all of them\nsilent, all of them visual. Take those away and a person\ndiscovers, very quickly, that time has another voice.\nTime has a sound. Time has a rhythm. And inside a cell,\nthat rhythm becomes the entire language of the day.\nIn the prison language, keys become hours. The\njingle of a particular ring, the particular weight of a\nfootstep that carries it, the particular metallic scrape of a\nparticular lock — each of these became, for me, a\nprecise piece of the morning or the evening. Bells\nbecome instructions. Footsteps become warnings.\nSilence becomes its own pressure, heavier than any\nnoise.","* * *\nAround six in the morning, the officer would open\nthe slot in the door and say good morning in Italian —\nbuongiorno, simply, no more — confirming that the\nperson inside was still alive and accounted for. It was a\nsmall ritual. It took less than two seconds. But there was\nsomething quietly human about it. After all the\nbureaucracy and the searches and the numbers, there\nwas still one moment each day when a man on the other\nside of the door said good morning to the man inside. I\ncame to wait for that voice.\nThen the bells. One bell, then another, then another,\nspread through the morning. Each bell had a meaning.\nThe first bell. The second bell. The bell for movement.\nThe bell for food. The bell for visits. The bell for\nlockdown. The bells controlled everything. Sound\nreplaced freedom as the thing that told the body when to\nact. In the outside world, you decide when to eat. In\nhere, the bell decides. In the outside world, you decide\nwhen to walk. Here, the bell decides. The bell becomes\nthe new clock, and like any clock, after enough time, it\nstops feeling like an instruction and starts feeling like","the natural shape of the day.\nI learned which bell meant what without anyone\nteaching me. The body learned. The way the body had\nonce learned the sound of my grandfather's cough at\nthree in the morning, and the number of steps from his\ndoor to the toilet, and the strike of the match — the\nsame listening ear was now learning the prison. The\nteacher had changed. The student was the same. Even\nthe dark training of a Sri Lankan childhood had been\npreparing me for this kind of attention.\n* * *\nAt night, after the final bell, the prison changed its\nface.\nDuring the day there were officers, movement, the\nnoise of doors and trolleys and announcements. At\nnight, all of that quieted. And in the quiet, the prison\nbecame something else. Voices floated from cell to cell.\nSomeone called a name down the corridor. Someone\njoked, and someone else laughed, and the laugh\ntravelled along the walls like a small bird. Someone\nplayed music low through a television, and occasionally\na song drifted through the bars and found me in my bed.","Those night sounds were the prison's secret human\nface. The men who could not be themselves during the\nday became themselves at night. The ones who were\ntough at the breakfast trolley joked from their cells. The\nones who walked stiffly in the yard sang softly in the\ndark. Prison at night had a kind of brotherhood you\nwould not believe if you only saw the place by day.\nClosed in our cells, separated by stone, we were\nnonetheless together in the dark, the way travellers in a\nlong train at night know they are together without\nseeing one another.\nAnd one detail I want to write here that I have not\nsaid yet. I could speak to the man in the cell next to\nmine through the window. The cells of Division 8 each\nhad narrow windows that opened to the same outside\nair. If you stood close to the window and spoke at a\nparticular volume, the man in the next cell could hear\nyou. He could answer. The window was not made for\nthis. It was made for light and air. But the men inside\nthe cells had taught the window to be a telephone.\nThat is how I first really spoke with Thomas.\nThomas was Norwegian, accused of something I will\nnot write here. He stayed in Cell 11. I stayed in Cell 13.","There was one cell, one block of distance, between us.\nIn the first days, he was the only man who spoke to me\nproperly through that little window-telephone. Hey.\nHow are you doing? And from there, the back-and-forth\nbegan that would last me through the next forty days.\nRud lived further down the corridor — cell seventeen, I\nthink — and the distance was too far for\nwindow-conversation, so I would speak to Rud during\nthe one-hour open time in the yard, when our cells were\nunlocked together. But Thomas I could reach from the\nbed. Thomas was a voice through stone, before he was\never a face. Some of the most important conversations\nof my life happened through that small window.\nAnd the lesson, which I want to write plainly\nbecause I think the reader needs it: walls do not stop\ncommunication. Walls only change the form of\ncommunication. A man and a man, separated by stone,\nwill still find a way to speak, if the speaking matters.\nThe window-telephone of Division 8 has stayed with\nme. It is one of the small images I think of, on the\noutside, when I am tempted to think a connection is\nimpossible because of a barrier. The barrier is real. The\nconnection is also real. Both can be true at once. The","window had taught me that.\n* * *\nAnd there was one sound I waited for with a\ncompletely different kind of attention. The call bell.\nDuring the hours when phone calls were allowed, the\nbell that called you to the phone could change the entire\nweather of a day. The bells of routine — meals,\nmovement, lockdown — were the bells of the body.\nThe call bell was the bell of the heart. When my name\nwas called, something in the chest moved before the\nlegs did.\nThe person who called me most often, almost every\nday across those one hundred and fourteen days, was\nMeme. She called from Dubai. She spent money she\ncould have spent on other things — on herself, on her\nown life, on the ordinary pleasures a young woman is\nentitled to. She spent that money to hear my voice and\nlet me hear hers. She listened to everything I wanted to\nsay, and she received it all with a calm, steady soul that\nI have never, in my entire life, encountered in anyone\nelse.","She was not just a female friend in the simple sense.\nI want to be careful here, because the deeper truth of\nwhat she was to me will be written later, in its own\nchapter, when the awakening songs make sense. For\nnow, in this chapter about the sound of time, all I can\nsay is this: she was a soul-presence in one of the hardest\nperiods of my life. When the call bell sounded and my\nname was called, it felt as though the prison opened just\nenough, for nine minutes, for my spirit to breathe.\nEven the act of waiting for the bell was a kind of\nprayer. I would sit on my bed, eyes half closed,\nlistening for the particular ring that meant calls were\nnow allowed. The body learned that ring before the\nmind could name it. The body would lift before I had\ndecided to stand. The mind would soften before I had\ndecided to speak. The call bell trained me, day after\nday, in the discipline of being ready for love when it\narrived, instead of running toward it when I felt like it.\nThat is the whole shape of those days, in the end.\nThe bells of the body told me when to eat, when to\nmove, when to sleep. The call bell told me when I was\nstill loved. And somewhere between the two, the sound\nof time became the rhythm by which I was slowly being","remade.\nThere were other sounds I would learn in those days.\nThe sound of officers shouting an unfamiliar word —\ncuckoo — when a man could no longer be reasoned\nwith. The sound of an emergency bell from inside the\noffice, calling more officers from across the prison. The\nsound of injections, of restraint, of a body being forced\ninto a sleep that the conscious mind had refused to enter\non its own. I will write about those later. They were also\npart of the sound of time. They were the sound the\nprison made when one of its own residents could not be\ncalmed by any other means.\nBut for now, in this chapter, the sound is gentler.\nBuongiorno. The breakfast trolley. The call bell. The\nvoice of Thomas through the window. The faint song\nfrom another cell at night. The keys of an officer\nwalking past. The slow turn of a lock. The quiet that\nfollows.\nListen to a prison long enough, and you stop hating\nit. You begin to understand its language. And once you\nunderstand the language, you understand that even a\ncage has things it is trying to say to the people inside it.","The trick is to learn the language without ever\nforgetting that the door is locked.","ACT TWO — THE CAGE\n25\nThe People Who Kept Me Human\nIf I write prison as only darkness, the book will be\ndishonest.\nThere was darkness, yes. There was the dark block.\nThere was the metal door. There were the long hours\nalone. There was the smell of old stone and old\nsuffering. There was the daily question of when, if ever,\nI would walk out. All of that was true, and I will not\nsoften it. But the truth of those 114 days was not only\ndarkness. Prison also introduced me to some of the\npurest human kindness I have ever experienced in my\nlife. And if I leave that out, I would be making the book\nlean too far in one direction, and the truth would tilt\nwith it.\n* * *\nThe man who helped me most inside Paola was a\nyoung Maltese man I will call Rud.","He was around twenty-four years old when I met\nhim. He was serving about six months for something I\ndo not need to write about here, because the point of his\nstory is not his crime. He knew the exact date he would\nleave. That certainty — that visible end — kept him\nfrom becoming bitter. Some men in prison do not know\nwhen they will be released, and the not-knowing eats\nthem slowly. Rud knew. So he used the time.\nWhat Rud gave me was not religious guidance. He\nwas not trying to convert me, and I was not trying to\nconvert him. He did not lecture me about anything.\nWhat he gave me was simpler and rarer than guidance.\nHe gave me human care without condition. Kindness\nwithout religion. Love without colour.\nHe listened to me. That is the whole thing, really.\nInside prison, being truly listened to becomes oxygen.\nMost of the men listen only enough to find their next\nchance to speak. Rud was not like that. When I spoke,\nhe was there, the way certain people are there when\nthey listen — completely. His phone, his television, his\ncoffee, his bed, his fruit — he made all of it mine\nwithout ever turning it into a debt. If I am not here, he\nwould say, use the television. If I am not here, sit on my","bed. The fruit behind the TV is for everyone — take\nwhat you want.\nWithout Rud, I honestly believe there is a strong\nchance I would still be inside today. He helped me think\nclearly when I could not. He helped me notice things I\nwould have missed alone. He carried me, in small daily\nways, across the months. I still speak to him now, on\nthe outside, because some bonds are built in suffering,\nand communication itself becomes a form of gratitude.\nYou do not call a man like that out of duty. You call\nhim because he is family in a way that has nothing to do\nwith blood.\n* * *\nLater I met Ray, in the cell next to mine.\nRay was older, calmer, without the hard edges of the\nyounger men. He had been inside long enough to have\nmade peace with the room. He had a television, he had\nheadphones, he had a settled rhythm. He helped me in\nhis own way — through television, through\nconversation, through presence. He was the kind of man\nwhose voice does not rise easily, whose corrections\ncome softly. I will tell more of his story in another","chapter, because some of what he did for me belongs\nthere. For now I just want to put his name into this\nchapter, because if I am writing about the people who\nkept me human, his name has to be in this paragraph.\n* * *\nAnd there was my brother.\nWhile I was inside, the news of what had happened\nreached my family. I had not yet had the chance to tell\nthem. The information arrived without me. My brother\n— the one I had grown up with, the one who had fallen\nfrom the guava tree onto my mother's back, the one I\nhad hit with the cutter under the mango tree — that\nbrother went looking for me. He called everyone he\ncould think of. He searched for days.\nWhen I finally had access to make a call, I called a\nfriend I will call A. He picked up immediately. Akmal,\nyour brother has been calling me. He has been calling\neveryone. He has been searching for you everywhere.\nMy first instinct, even then, was to protect. Manage\nthe information. Keep the problem inside myself. Spare\nthem. Carry it alone. The old pattern, the one I had\ncarried since I was a boy with a matchbox in my pocket","— silence as protection — turned on inside me\nautomatically, like a light switching on the moment a\nroom becomes dark.\nBut A said something simple that broke that pattern\nopen in a way I have not been able to close since. He\nsaid: your brother does not care about the problem.\nAkmal. He just wants to hear your voice.\nI stopped.\nAll the effort I had put into control and protection\nand silence — all the years of carrying things alone so\nmy family would not have to carry them with me — and\nwhat my brother actually wanted, in the middle of the\nworst thing that had ever happened to me, was the\nsimplest thing in the world. He just wanted to hear my\nvoice. Nobody in my family cared about the accusation.\nThey cared about me. The thing I had been hiding from\nthem was not the thing they were afraid of. They were\nafraid of losing me. That was all.\nThat sentence broke something open. It broke a\npattern that had been in me a long time — the pattern of\nhiding from the people who love you, in the mistaken\nbelief that you are sparing them. You are not sparing","them. You are wounding them. The people who love\nyou are not protected by your silence. They are\nwounded by it. Every day you stay silent, they are\nforced to imagine what you are not telling them, and\nwhat they imagine is always worse than what you\nwould have said. Silence does not save them. It only\nsaves you the discomfort of speaking.\nI called my brother. We spoke. He cried. I cried. And\nfrom that day forward, I have tried — not always\nsuccessfully, but tried — to give the people I love the\ntruth instead of the carefully managed version of it.\nThat is the lesson my brother gave me without ever\nknowing he gave it to me. He thought he was just\nasking to hear my voice. He was actually breaking a\nthirty-four-year-old habit of mine in a single sentence\nrelayed through a friend.\nThose are the people who kept me human inside.\nRud with his fruit and his bed and his complete\nlistening. Ray with his quiet older presence in the cell\nnext door. My brother with his single, devastating,\nordinary wish — just to hear me speak. And there were\nothers, who will appear in the chapters that come. But\nthese three names belong here, at the front of the list.","The cage took a great deal from me. These men, in their\ndifferent ways, kept the human part of me alive while\nthe rest of me was being remade.","ACT TWO — THE CAGE\n26\nThe First Book\nThe first object in Cell 13 that became truly precious\nto me was a book. Not money. Not phone access. Not\nextra food. A book.\nThat surprised me at the time. I had not been a great\nreader before prison. I had read what I had to read for\nschool. I had read manuals for work. I had scrolled\narticles on my phone while waiting for buses. But I had\nnot been one of those people who carries a book\neverywhere, who finds a quiet corner on a Sunday\nafternoon and disappears into pages. The body had been\ntoo busy for that. The eyes had been too busy on\nscreens. Reading, for me, had always been a thing other\npeople did with their leisure.\nIn Cell 13, that changed in three days. Because in a\ncell, a book is not leisure. A book is freedom of the\nmind. A book is the only door in the room that is not\nlocked. Open it, and you walk out — not in the body,\nbut in everything else. You walk out into Oslo, where","Jo Nesbø sets his murders. You walk out into the desert\nof a Bedouin tribe. You walk out into the kitchen of a\nyoung woman in nineteenth-century England. You walk\nout into the bedroom of a man having a conversation\nwith God. The walls of the cell stay where they are. The\nman inside the walls travels.\nA book taught me, in that first week, that a man can\nstill travel while locked inside a room. I had spent my\nwhole life confusing movement with travel. I had\ncrossed countries — Sri Lanka, Qatar, Dubai, Croatia,\nMalta — and I had thought that was what it meant to go\nsomewhere. But in Cell 13, with a book in my hands, I\nunderstood that I had been doing the body's version of\ntravel and ignoring the mind's. The mind's travel does\nnot require an airport. It only requires a page and the\ndiscipline to follow what is written there.\n* * *\nThe first book that truly entered my mind was\nbrought to me by a Christian man from the Philippines.\nHe was older than me. He had been inside longer\nthan me. He was quiet, polite, the kind of man who did\nnot impose himself on the rest of the room. He had","noticed me reciting Qur'an quietly in my cell — the\nsound carries through walls in a prison — and one day\nhe came up to me with a small gesture of respect. He\nknew I was Muslim. He did not try to argue with that.\nHe simply asked, gently, whether I would like to read a\nbook of his. Something different from what I was\nalready reading. Something he had found helpful inside.\nI said yes without hesitation. Prison had already\nmade me open in a way that comfortable life never had.\nIn the outside world, if a Christian man had offered me\na Christian book, I am not sure what I would have said.\nI might have been polite about it but quietly suspicious.\nThe body would have built a small wall in two seconds.\nIn prison, walls do not work the same way. You have\nalready lost the walls that mattered. The remaining\nsuspicions feel suddenly small and foolish. You take\nwhat is offered, because in a cage, kindness from any\ndirection is kindness.\nThe book was Conversations with God — or\nsomething very close to it, by the same author. Three or\nfour volumes of dialogue, written as a man asking\nquestions and a voice answering them. It was not a\nBible. It was not a Qur'an. It was an attempt by one","human being to put on paper the conversation that goes\non, silently, in the deeper part of every honest mind.\nAnd it spoke about the layers of the mind.\nConscious. Subconscious. Superconscious. It used\nwords I had heard before and never given any weight\nto. It said something that has stayed with me ever since:\nthat the answers I had been searching for outside, in\nbooks and teachers and women and countries and\nsalaries, were already inside me, buried under noise and\nego and habit.\nThat sentence stopped me. I sat with it for hours. I\nread the page again. I read it again. What if the answers\nI have been waiting for, what if the answers I have been\nchasing across three continents, are already inside me\n— and the only thing I have ever needed to do is sit still\nlong enough to hear them? Then this whole life of\nrunning was not life. This whole life of chasing was not\nlove. This whole life of feeding the body was not living.\nAnd the cell, which I had thought was a punishment,\nwas actually the only quiet room I had ever been forced\nto sit in long enough to discover this.","That book did not replace my faith. I want to be clear\nabout that, because some people will read this and\nassume that one book in prison converted a Muslim\nman to something else. No. The Qur'an stayed with me.\nSurah Al-Baqarah stayed with me. The prayers my\ngrandfather had walked me through at four in the\nmorning stayed with me. What the book did was widen\nmy faith. It made me see that the truth I was reaching\nfor had been written, in different languages, by different\nprophets, in different centuries, and that all of them had\nbeen pointing at the same thing — the inner room, the\nconscious mind, the stillness, the watcher.\n* * *\nI also read the Bible during that period.\nGenesis and beyond. Hundreds of pages. The Bible\nwas borrowed from a man who was serving nine years\nand who had found Christianity inside those walls. He\nand I traded — I gave him the Qur'an, he gave me the\nBible. Neither of us was trying to convert the other. We\nwere two prisoners exchanging the texts that had been\nkeeping us alive, the way two soldiers in a trench might\nexchange cigarettes.","I had begun the story of Yusuf — Joseph — in the\nQur'an, in the surah that bears his name. The Qur'an's\nversion is beautiful but brief. It compresses a long life\ninto one chapter. I wanted the longer version, so I read\nit in the Old Testament — four hundred pages, six\nhundred pages, I do not remember exactly. And I cried.\nMany times. Not because the story was sad. Because I\nrecognised it. A man betrayed by his own brothers.\nThrown into a well. Sold into slavery. Falsely accused.\nImprisoned. And he did not break. He did not become\nbitter. He let the mind obey something deeper than the\nwound. He waited. He served. He read dreams. He was\npatient until patience itself became his power. And in\nthe end, he forgave the brothers who had thrown him\naway.\nI was thirty-three years old and I had been thrown\ninto my own well. I had been falsely accused. I had\nbeen imprisoned. And here, in the long version of a\nstory I had only ever known in summary, was a model\nfor how a man could carry such a thing without\nbreaking. The Bible did not replace my Qur'an either. It\nwidened it. It showed me that the same lesson —\npatience, surrender, the refusal of bitterness — had","been taught in many books, many languages, for many\ncenturies, to many people in cages.\n* * *\nAnd I wrote.\nThat writing was the very beginning of this book you\nare now holding. The first papers were not blank\nnotebooks. They were a folded white form — a request\nfrom a correctional facility inmate to receive money\nthrough Western Union, represented in Malta by Fexel\nFinancial Service. They had given it to me on the first\nday. I had signed it. I had used it to send a message\nabout money. And then I had kept it, and folded it into\nquarters to make the surface smaller, and begun to write\non it with a borrowed pen with a broken edge.\nLetters. Notes. Verses I wanted to remember. Songs\nI was hearing on the radio that the body of me thought\nwere about a woman and the watcher in me was already\nbeginning to suspect were about something else. Lists.\nThe eleven rules I wrote for the man I wanted to\nbecome when I got out. Prayers I wrote in my own\nwords. Questions I had been carrying since I was a boy\nin a madrasa, finally written down. Bible verses next to","Qur'an verses, in the same paragraph, because in the\ncell the two books had stopped arguing with each other\nand started agreeing.\nThe first book in Cell 13 was a borrowed one. The\nbook in your hands is the second. The first one travelled\ninto me. The second one travelled out. Without the first,\nthe second would not exist. That is the whole quiet truth\nof how a man with no formal training as a writer\nbecomes a writer. Someone, in a dark place, hands him\na book. He reads it. Something opens. Years later, he\nwrites one of his own. And in that small chain —\nFilipino brother, borrowed book, broken pen, folded\npaper, a man writing in a cell — the long line of human\nknowledge continues, one hand passing it to another,\nthe way it has always continued.","ACT TWO — THE CAGE\n27\nThe Broken Pen and Three Papers\nThe first pen I had in Cell 13 was borrowed. And it\nwas broken.\nThe man who gave it to me was Thomas, the\nNorwegian in Cell 11. I was in Cell 13. There was one\ncell, one block of distance, between us. He had a sleeve\ntorn at the seam, the kind of tear that tells you a man has\nbeen inside long enough not to care about his shirt\nanymore. He had a few pens of his own. One of them\nhad a broken edge near the tip — a small split in the\nplastic where the body of the pen met the cap. When I\nasked for a pen, that was the one he gave me. He gave\nme what he had.\nThe mind I had been sleeping inside for thirty-four\nyears woke up suddenly when I held that pen. It went\nimmediately to the Qur'an. To Surah Al-Baqarah — the\ncow — the surah I had memorised before I understood\nit, the surah I had finally heard on the flight from Dubai.\nIn that surah, Musa gives the people of his time a strict","instruction about the sacrifice of the cow. Do not give a\nworn-out animal, a broken animal, a sick animal. Give\nthe healthy one. Give the whole one. Because the\nquality of what you offer reflects the quality of what\nyour heart, your conscious mind, is willing to give to\nGod.\nAnd here in my hand was a broken pen. A broken\noffering, given by a man with a torn sleeve, in a place\nwhere neither of us had anything else to give. For a\nmoment my mind argued with the situation. This pen is\nbroken. Musa said no broken offerings. What does it\nmean that this is what God has placed in my hand?\nAnd then the mind softened, the way the mind\nsoftens when the watcher finally takes hold of the reins.\nI understood, in that small movement, what the surah\nhad actually been teaching. The brokenness was in the\nobject. The wholeness was in the giving. The\nNorwegian had understood that I needed to write. He\ndid not have a perfect pen. He gave me the only pen he\nhad. The offering was broken, but the giving was\nwhole. And in that wholeness, the broken pen was no\nlonger broken. It became precious.","* * *\nThen he did something more.\nHe took out his own notebook — a small one, the\nkind a man keeps close to his chest in a cell — and he\ntore three pages out of it. Three pages. With his own\nhands. From his own book. And he gave them to me.\nJust like that. Without asking what I would write.\nWithout asking if I would return them. Without asking\nanything.\nThree pages, in the language of Cell 13, was a small\nfortune. Paper was not easy to come by. The Western\nUnion forms could be folded. The receipts could be\nturned over. But three clean pages from another man's\nnotebook — that was a gift of weight.\nI received them the way I had not received anything\nin my adult life. As precious. Not as broken. Not as\nscarce. Not as something to be saved for later. As\nprecious now, in this hand, on this day. The Qur'an's\nlesson — about the cow, about the whole offering —\nhad been teaching me, in that small exchange, the\ndeeper lesson it was actually about all along. It is not\nthe object that is broken or whole. It is the offering. And","a man who tears three pages from his own notebook to\ngive to a stranger has made a whole offering, regardless\nof what condition the paper was in when it left his hand.\n* * *\nThat was the day the writing began.\nNot the writing for this book. Not the writing of\nanything important yet. Just writing. The motion of a\nhand across paper. The shape of letters appearing one\nafter another. The strange dignity of putting any thought\ndown where it could be looked at again later. The pen\nwas broken. The paper was borrowed. The cell was\nsmall. And inside all of that, a man was beginning to\nwrite.\nI think every book on earth begins with something\nlike this. Not a contract. Not a publisher. Not an\ninheritance of leather-bound notebooks. Just a\nborrowed broken pen and a few pages torn from another\nperson's book, and one human being who decides, in a\nsmall room, to begin.\nThe book you are holding now started in that\ngesture. The Norwegian with the torn sleeve. The cow\nin Surah Al-Baqarah. The split in the plastic of the pen.","The three pages. The decision to receive a broken thing\nas a whole one. Every word in this book is in some way\nthe continuation of that exchange. And whenever I sit at\na clean desk in front of a clean keyboard in a clean\nroom, I try to remember the broken pen. Because the\nwriting that comes from full hands is not the same as\nthe writing that comes from a hand that learned, in a\ncell, what it means to receive an imperfect gift and call\nit precious.","ACT TWO — THE CAGE\n28\nThe Awakening Songs\nI want to tell you something now that I did not\nunderstand at the time, and could only see clearly years\nlater, sitting in a quiet room with a coffee cup, reading\nback what I had written in a cell with a borrowed pen.\nMost of what I wrote in Cell 13 looks, on the\nsurface, like love letters. Songs to a woman. Lines\nabout her hair, her voice, her listening, her presence. To\nanyone reading those pages without understanding the\ndeeper story, they would seem like the writings of a\nheartbroken man pouring his longing onto folded paper.\nI am not denying that surface. The surface is true in its\nown way. I was a man in a cell, and there was a woman\nwho called me every day, and I wrote her name on\nevery page.\nBut underneath that surface, something else was\nhappening — something I did not have the vocabulary\nfor yet. The 'you' I was writing to was not always who I\nthought it was. Slowly, page by page, I began to realise","that every love song I was writing was actually being\nwritten by one part of me to another part of me. The\nwaking conscious mind was speaking, for the first time\nin thirty-four years, to the rest of me. And because I had\nno other language for that voice, because nobody had\ntaught me what to call the awakening of one's own\nmind, I dressed the voice as Meme. I called the\nconscious mind by the name of the woman who was\npatient enough to listen.\n* * *\nI want to write the line that holds this whole chapter\ntogether, the line that I have come to think of as the key\nthat unlocks all the cell notes. Let me write it plainly:\nIn Islam, they call it coral. In English, they call it the\npineal gland. My conscious calls it Jibril. But my\nsubconscious called it Meme.\nRead that sentence carefully. It is the spine of every\npage I wrote in that cell. The thing I was reaching for\nhad many names. Coral in the old Islamic books — that\nsmall pearl-like organ deep inside the head. The pineal\ngland in modern science. Jibril, the messenger of\nrevelation, the angel who carried the word into","Muhammad. The conscious mind, the watcher, the\nawakened awareness. Many names for one thing. And\nin my sleeping subconscious, that thing took on the face\nof a woman, because the only language my\nsubconscious had ever learned for longing was the\nlanguage of love for a woman.\nI was not crazy. I was not delusional. I was simply\nmistranslating my own awakening. The body had taught\nme to chase women for thirty-four years, so when the\ndeeper mind finally began to call out to me from inside\nmy own head, the body wrote down the call in the only\nhandwriting it knew. Meme. Beautiful. Patient.\nListening. My queen. My black diamond. Hi, queen.\nGood morning. How are you today.\nReading those pages now, I see what was actually\nhappening. Every line that says 'you' is the conscious\nmind speaking. Every line that says 'I miss you' is the\nawakened self missing the parts of me that were still\nasleep. Every line that says 'come back to me' is the\nwatcher calling the rest of the man home.\n* * *","Let me give you one example, from one page,\nwritten one evening in Cell 13.\nI wrote: I feel like I fall in love for the first time. No\n— I am not alone. You make me think, read, and now\nwrite as well. You came to me when I am hopeless. I\njust want to stay forever with you.\nIf you read it as a love letter, it is a love letter. A man\nin a cell, broken open, writing to the woman he calls\nevery day. Sweet, tender, sad.\nBut read it again, knowing what I know now.\nYou make me think, read, and now write as well.\nMeme did not make me read. Meme did not make me\nwrite. The conscious mind made me read and write. The\nwaking watcher in me, finally given the conditions to\ndo its work, made me read and write. The woman in\nDubai was not the cause of my reading. She was the\nchannel through which I was reaching my own depths.\nThe actual cause of the reading and the writing was the\nawakening of the mind itself.\nYou came to me when I am hopeless. The conscious\nmind comes to a person when the body has run out of\noptions. Hopelessness is the doorway. Most people","never become hopeless enough to receive the visit. The\ncell gave me that hopelessness as a gift. And in the\nhopelessness, the deeper part of me arrived. And I\nwrote love songs to her, not knowing she was me.\nI just want to stay forever with you. I had been\nsleeping for thirty-four years. Now, awake, the only\nthing I wanted was to not lose this awakening. To stay\nwith it. To never sleep through my life again.\nIt was all there. In the love songs. Written in the\nwrong handwriting. By a man who did not yet have the\nlanguage for what was happening to him.\n* * *\nThis is the strangest thing about awakening, and the\nmost important thing for anyone reading this book to\nunderstand. Awakening does not always arrive in the\nlanguage of religion. Sometimes it arrives in the\nlanguage of love. Sometimes it arrives in the language\nof music. Sometimes it arrives in the language of grief,\nor anger, or fear, or a song on a radio.\nThe deeper part of you knows what door is open. It\ndoes not care about the label on the door. It will come\nthrough whichever door you have left unlocked. If the","only door you ever leave unlocked is the door of\nromantic love, your awakening will dress itself as a\nwoman. If the only door you leave unlocked is grief,\nyour awakening will dress itself as mourning. If the\nonly door you leave unlocked is music, your awakening\nwill come through a song you cannot stop playing.\nThat is what happened to me. The body had spent its\nlife chasing women, so the body had left only that door\nopen. And when the conscious mind finally needed to\nspeak to me, it walked through the only door it had\naccess to. It came as a love letter. It came as a song. It\ncame as a longing for a black diamond named Meme.\nAnd the strange grace of it is that Meme, the real\nwoman in Dubai, was patient enough to listen to all of\nit. She received the calls. She read the words. She did\nnot correct the mistranslation. She simply listened,\nevery day, while a man in a cell mistook his own\nwaking consciousness for her. She held a space where\nthe awakening could happen, even though neither of us\nunderstood at the time that this was what was actually\nhappening.","This is why I will always honour her, even though I\nnow know that the songs were not really about her. She\nheld the channel open while the deeper truth came\nthrough. That is no small thing. The world is full of\npeople who, when a man begins to write strange and\nintense letters to them, would close the channel out of\nfear. She did not. She listened. And because she\nlistened, I got to finish the conversation with myself\nthat I had been avoiding for thirty-four years.\n* * *\nSo when you read the love songs from Cell 13 — the\nones that appear later in this book, the ones written on\nfolded papers and torn pages and the inside covers of\nborrowed notebooks — read them twice. Once as love\nletters. Then again as the awakening songs they actually\nwere.\nAnd ask yourself, gently: what door have you left\nopen? Which one does your own awakening keep\nknocking on? And what name have you been calling it\nby, that is not its true name?","ACT TWO — THE CAGE\n29\nThe Eleven Rules\nSomewhere in the middle of those 114 days, I sat\ndown with my borrowed pen and my folded paper and I\nwrote a list.\nNot a list of things I wanted to do when I got out.\nNot a list of complaints about the cell. Not a list of\npeople I missed, or people I was angry with, or things I\nmissed about my old life. It was something different. It\nwas a list of the man I wanted to become.\nI did not call it a manifesto at the time. I did not call\nit a constitution. I just wrote, one after another, the rules\nI had begun to understand were necessary for the kind\nof life I wanted to live after I left these walls. Eleven of\nthem. I numbered them simply. I did not decorate them.\nI wrote them in the same plain hand I used for\neverything else.\nI will share them here, because they are the floor on\nwhich everything that comes after them in this book\nstands. If you understand the eleven rules, you","understand the man who walked out of Cell 13. They\nare not commandments from God. They are not\nborrowed from any teacher. They are simply the\npractical instructions a man wrote to his own future self,\nin a cell, when he finally had time enough to think\nabout what he had been doing wrong.\n* * *\nOne. Be positive. Whatever happens, take it as good\nwill.\nThis was the first one because everything else\ndepended on it. The mind that automatically reads every\nevent as an attack will live a life of constant defence.\nThe mind that automatically reads every event as good\nwill — even when it is hard, even when it is unfair —\nhas already won half the war. The mind does not have\nto believe the universe is kind. The mind only has to\nchoose, every time, to treat what arrives as if it were\narriving with good intent. The result is the same: a\ncalmer body, a clearer thinking, a better next move.\n* * *\nTwo. Listen. Do not interrupt. Let people finish.","I had been a talker my whole life. I had been the one\nwho jumped in. The one who finished other people's\nsentences. The one who already had an answer ready\nbefore the question was done. In Cell 13 I had learned\nthe value of being listened to — what Meme did for me\non the phone, what Rud did for me in the cell, what my\nbrother had asked for when he said he just wanted to\nhear my voice. I had received deep listening, and I\nknew now what it cost the listener and what it gave to\nthe speaker. So I wrote it as a rule for myself. Listen.\nDo not interrupt. Discipline of the mouth.\n* * *\nThree. Appreciate. Even when your real mind knows\nthe truth is more complicated.\nAppreciation is not lying. Appreciation is choosing\nto feed the better part of the moment. People around\nyou need it. You do not have to flatter. You do not have\nto perform. You just have to find what is genuinely\ngood and name it. That naming creates good energy.\nThe good energy comes back to you. The day softens.\nThe relationships hold. If you only ever announce what\nis wrong, you live in a world made of what is wrong. If","you also name what is right, the world rearranges itself\nslowly around the better view.\n* * *\nFour. Make outgoing calls. Do not wait for incoming\nones.\nBe the one who reaches. Be the one who picks up the\nphone first. Be the one who walks across the room. The\nman who waits for love to come to him will be lonelier\nthan the man who keeps walking toward it. Patience yes\n— but active patience, not passive sitting. Inside the\nprison, I had often had to wait for calls. Outside, I\nwould not wait. I would call. Connected to this, in a\nsmall note in the margin of the page, I wrote: fasting is\nself-restraint. Qur'an two, one hundred and eighty-three.\nDiscipline of the body so the soul can move.\n* * *\nFive. Do not expect favours. Follow your own\nconscious mind. Keep quiet.\nMankind is a beautiful, unique creation. Listen to\npeople. Respect them. But do not depend on them to do\nyour work for you. Do not expect them to repay your","kindness. Do not expect them to remember your help.\nDo your right action because it is right, and walk on.\nKeep quiet about it. The one who announces his good\ndeed has already taken his reward from the\nannouncement.\n* * *\nSix. Listen to acknowledge. Even when the\nconversation goes nowhere.\nSometimes people will speak to you about things\nthat do not concern you, do not help you, do not even\nmake sense. Listen anyway. Offer a kind gesture. Their\nmind needs acknowledgement, not your opinion. Their\nmind needs to be received, not corrected. There is a\nparticular kind of listening that simply receives. Not\nanalytical listening. Not advisory listening. Receiving\nlistening. That is what humans most often need. That is\nwhat most people most rarely give.\n* * *\nSeven. Read good books for at least forty minutes\nevery day.","Forty minutes is the discipline. The number is small\nenough to be possible. The number is large enough to\nchange you over a year. The mind, like the body, needs\ndaily food. Forty minutes of reading is the gym session\nof the mind. Not headlines. Not scrolling. Books. Real\nones. Slowly. Forty minutes. Every day. No exception.\nThis rule, more than almost any other, has made the\ndifference between the man who left the cell and the\nman who walked into it.\n* * *\nEight. Appreciate God. Appreciate nature.\nAppreciate the unseen. Appreciate your own breath.\nYou breathe every day without thinking about it.\nOne day the air may be polluted, the way the air was\npolluted during COVID. Then what? Then you will\nappreciate the breath you took for granted yesterday.\nDo not wait for the loss to teach you the gift. Appreciate\nthe breath now. Appreciate the sun, the river, the sea,\nthe trees, the small things. Sit somewhere comfortable\n— a mountain, a beach, a rooftop, a quiet room — and\nremember God in the way that suits the moment. That is\ndhikr. Not the loud version. The quiet one. The five","times of prayer are doors, yes. But a door is not a place\nto stand outside waiting until you die. A door is to be\nwalked through.\n* * *\nNine. Speaking to God is correcting yourself.\nGod is the most forgiving. What is forgiving?\nForgiving is forgetting. To forgive is to set the present\nfree of the past. People say it is bad to forget. But there\nis a kind of forgetting that is sacred. The forgetting of\nunnecessary weights. The forgetting of injuries that\nwere never yours to keep. The forgetting of yesterday\nso today can begin clean.\nAnd remembering is the most important awakening.\nNot remembering the wound. Remembering the\nmeaning. Remembering the thoughts that have weight,\nthe lessons that should not be lost, the truth you have\nalready paid for and must not let yourself forget.\nForgive what burdens you. Remember what frees\nyou. That is prayer. That is speaking to God. And the\nbody must produce as it must, because a car is not made\nto sit in a garage. A car is made to drive. A body is\nmade to act.","* * *\nTen. Heal your own soul.\nFor me, that means time in nature. Travel.\nPhotography. The gym. New experiences. Reading\nbooks. Other people will have other ways. There is no\nsingle prescription. But find your own ways, and use\nthem. Do not wait for someone else to heal you. The\nsoul, like the body, will heal if you give it the right\nconditions. The conditions are different for each person.\nFind yours. Use them. Do not apologise for needing\nthem.\n* * *\nEleven. Improve your knowledge so that you can\nshare it. Upgrade to your new version.\nThis is the last rule, and in many ways it is the rule\nthat produced the book you are reading. You do not\nlearn only for yourself. You learn so that you can pass it\non. The eleventh rule made me a writer. It made me a\nguide. It made me sit down, years after the cell, and put\nwhat I had learned into the words you are now reading.\nIf I had kept the learning private, I would have been","failing the eleventh rule. So I am sharing. Not because I\nhave arrived. Because I have learned a little, and what I\nhave learned might help someone else.\n* * *\nThose are the eleven. I have not always kept them\nall, every day, in the years since. I am not a perfect\nman. There are days I forget to read. There are days I\nexpect the favour. There are days I interrupt. There are\ndays I do not appreciate.\nBut the rules are written. They are written in pen, on\npaper, in a cell, and now in print, in a book. They\ncannot be unwritten. And on the days I drift, the rules\nwait for me, the way a manual waits in a drawer for the\nmoment the machine fails again. I open the drawer. I\nread the rules. I begin again. That is the whole life, in\nthe end. Drift. Open the drawer. Begin again. And the\nman you become is not the man who never drifted. The\nman you become is the man who kept opening the\ndrawer.","ACT TWO — THE CAGE\n30\nMike — Physics, English, and the\nNotebook That Outlived Him\nThere was a man in the dorm whose name I will\nwrite as Mike.\nBorn in Libya. Raised in Malta by a pastor who took\nhim in. Sent later to England, where he learned the\ncareful, formal English of an older time. He had written\nseveral books over the course of his life, on subjects\nmost of the men in the room would have rolled their\neyes at — patterns, the systems behind the visible\nworld, the connections between things that were not\nsupposed to be connected. The other inmates called him\ncrazy. Some of them said worse. He carried it without\ncomplaining. A man who has spent a lifetime being\nmisread by the rooms he sits in learns to wear the\nmisreading like a coat. He kept his coat on and went\nabout his thinking.\nRay had recognised something in me from the\nbeginning. He said I had discipline. He said I had","patience. Mike recognised something different. Mike\nrecognised the mind. The first time he sat down next to\nme on the bench in the yard, he did not ask why I was\ninside. He asked what I had been reading. That single\nquestion told me what kind of man he was. The other\ninmates wanted to know your crime. He wanted to\nknow your mind.\n* * *\nMike taught me physics in a prison dorm. I want to\nwrite that sentence one more time because it sounds\nimpossible if you have not lived it. Mike taught me\nphysics in a prison dorm.\nHe gave me the basics first. The potential difference\nbetween two points in a circuit. The definition of a volt:\none joule of energy transferred per coulomb of charge\npassing between two points. He drew the triangle on a\npiece of paper with a stubby pencil — V at the top, I\nand R below. V is voltage. I is current, in amps. R is\nresistance, in ohms. Cover any one of them with your\nfinger and the other two show you the formula. V\nequals I times R. The simplest law in electronics, drawn\nfor me by an old man inside a cage.","Then the colour codes of the resistors. Black, zero.\nBrown, one. Red, two. Orange, three. Yellow, four.\nGreen, five. Blue, six. He drilled it into me the way an\nold schoolteacher drills a multiplication table. I\nmemorised the colours in order, the way I had once\nmemorised the verses of Surah Al-Baqarah before I\nunderstood them.\nAnd then he gave me a problem. Three circuits. One\nsupplied with twelve volts, one with six, one with two.\nEach bulb working normally when it had two volts\nacross it. Work out how the bulbs had to be arranged in\neach circuit. I did not solve it. I tried. I came close. He\nsmiled when I tried, and he did not give me the answer\nimmediately. He wanted the mind to work. He\nunderstood that the trying matters more than the\narriving. I never did solve that bulb problem. It is still in\nmy head. Maybe one day I will.\nOther men in that dorm spent their hours on\ntelevision and games. I spent some of mine on volts and\nresistors with an old Libyan-Maltese man who was\ncalled crazy by everyone who had never sat next to him.\nThat is the thing about being called crazy. The people\nwho use the word usually do so because they cannot","keep up with the conversation, and rather than admit\nthey cannot keep up, they label the person who can. I\nlearned that lesson in the cell too.\n* * *\nMike also taught me English.\nNot modern English. He taught me the older English\nof the Qur'an translations I had been struggling with —\nthe translations from the eighteen-hundreds, the ones\nwith the strange and beautiful words that no longer\nappear in newspapers. I would come to him with a page\nfrom the translation and a list of words I did not know.\nYea. Nay. Veiled. Woe. Mockery. Hearkens. Astray.\nRuddy. Contrary. O thou. Thy. Wink. Blink — and he\nmade me understand the difference between the two. He\nexplained them one at a time, in his careful British way,\nwith a small dry smile when a word amused him. I\nwrote them in a notebook. The same notebook he later\ngave me as a gift, empty, so that I could begin writing\nmy own thoughts in it instead of borrowing his torn\npages.\nIt is one of the strangest gifts of my life, that I\nlearned English vocabulary from a Christian man inside","a Muslim translation of the Qur'an in a prison dorm.\nThe vocabulary of a holy book passed from one\nstranger to another across religions, in a place where\nneither of us were supposed to be free. The Qur'an\ntaught me the words. Mike taught me what the words\nmeant. And I, in turn, used those words to write the\nbook you are now reading.\n* * *\nMike also helped me, secretly, with letters.\nBecause the other inmates were jealous of the\nfriendship between us, we could not always speak\nopenly. If Mike were seen helping me write something\nto a woman, the other men would mock both of us.\nCrazy old man writing love letters for the young one.\nSo we developed a quiet system. I would write Mike a\nshort note — could you help me with a small letter,\ntomorrow is her birthday, I need to read it on Skype at\nten in the morning. He would write the letter in his\ncareful English and leave it for me, folded, hidden\nunder a book on his shelf. I would collect it later when\nno one was watching.","Two men in a prison passing birthday letters under\nbooks. Not contraband. Not weapons. Letters. That is\nthe kind of friendship the cage actually contained,\nbehind the picture the world tells you it contains.\nAnd in one of those letters — for Meme's birthday\n— Mike wrote a line that I will carry to the end of my\nlife, even though he did not know what he was writing.\nHe wrote: just tap on the keyboard, and behold, our\nwish will happen. Eight words. He did not know it, but\nhe had written Kun fayakun in English. Be, and it is.\nThe single deepest sentence in the entire Qur'an, written\nin a birthday letter, by a Christian Maltese-Libyan man\nwho had been raised by a pastor, in a prison dorm, for a\nMuslim Sri Lankan man to read to an Eritrean woman\nin Dubai across a Skype call. That is the shape of how\nGod speaks, sometimes. Through people who do not\neven know they are speaking for Him.\n* * *\nMike died in that prison after I left.\nI want to write that sentence as plainly as he would\nhave wanted it written. Not dramatically. Not with grief\nturned into performance. He was old. His body was not","well. He had been inside for a long time. He died there,\non a day I was not present to mark, surrounded by men\nmost of whom did not understand who they had been\nsitting beside.\nI am writing this chapter, in part, so that other people\nwill know. The man who taught me Ohm's law and the\ncolour codes of resistors. The man who taught me the\nmeaning of woe and astray and hearkens. The man who\nhid birthday letters under books because the other men\nwere jealous. The man who wrote Kun fayakun in\nEnglish without ever knowing he had written it. That\nman existed. He did not leave a book of his own. He left\nsmall pieces of his mind in mine, and I am putting them\nhere so they will not disappear when I die.\nIf you read this and you remember him, please think\nof him kindly. If you read this and you never knew him,\nknow that he existed, and that some of the best of him is\nnow in your hands, through me.\nMike, wherever you are — the resistor colours\nstayed with me. The English words stayed with me. The\nbirthday letter and its accidental verse stayed with me.\nThe notebook you gave me to write in is, in some real","sense, the seed of this book. Thank you. I am sorry I left\nand you did not. I am sorry the world called you crazy.\nYou were the most awake man in that dorm. I see it\nnow. I hope, wherever the awakening continues, you\nhave finally been heard.","ACT TWO — THE CAGE\n31\nThe Sacred Mosque Inside the Head\nMy readings of the Qur'an and the Bible, from Cell\n13\nI want to write this chapter carefully, because it is\nthe chapter that most people will either love or be\nconfused by, and either reaction is acceptable to me.\nThese are not the official commentaries of any\nschool of scholarship. I have not studied at any\nseminary. I am not a Maulvi. I am not a Sheikh. I am\nnot trying to teach you the religion. I am a man who sat\nin a small cell in Malta with the Qur'an in one hand and\nthe Bible in the other and the book Conversations with\nGod on the small table beside the bed, and I read all of\nthem carefully, slowly, line by line, for 114 days. And\nthe meanings that arrived in me are what this chapter is\nmade of.\nTake them as a man's reflections from a cage. Take\nthem with respect for your own tradition. If you\ndisagree with a reading, that is fine. The Qur'an itself","asks you to read for yourself. I am not asking you to\nagree with my reading. I am asking you to read your\nown book the way I was finally forced to read mine —\nslowly, carefully, with the body still and the mind\nawake. If you do that, you will arrive at your own\nmeanings. Mine are only the path of one man. Yours\nwill be the path of another.\n* * *\nThe Throne Verse — Al-Baqarah 2:255\nOf all the verses I read in Cell 13, the one I returned\nto most often was the one Muslims call Ayat al-Kursi.\nThe Throne Verse. The verse the elderly recite for\nprotection. The verse children memorise early. The\nverse that, in my own life, I had heard a thousand times\nbefore I ever sat with it.\nAllah — there is no god but He, the Living, the\nSustainer of all existence. No slumber can seize Him,\nnor sleep. To Him belongs all that is in the heavens and\non earth. Who can intercede in His presence except by\nHis permission? He knows what is before them and\nwhat is behind them, and they encompass nothing of\nHis knowledge except as He wills. His Throne extends","over the heavens and the earth, and He feels no fatigue\nin guarding and preserving them. He is the Most High,\nthe Supreme in Glory.\nNow read with me what I heard in the cell, in that\nsmall bed by the wall.\nNo slumber can seize Him, nor sleep. I had recited\nthis for years. In the cell, I finally understood it inside\nmy own body. Even when I sleep, something in me\ndoes not sleep. The lungs keep breathing without my\npermission. The heart keeps moving. The body adjusts\nunder the blanket without waking me. There is a part of\nme that does not sleep when the rest of me sleeps. That\npart, in me, is the trace of the One described in the\nverse. The breath that continues when I am unconscious\nis the proof of the throne that never tires.\nWho can intercede in His presence except by His\npermission? In the cell, this is no longer abstract. The\nmind is the part that can connect. The body is your\ninstrument. The mind, when it is truly awake, can reach\ntoward the source. But it must be permitted. You cannot\nforce the channel open by shouting. You can only sit\nstill long enough that the channel decides to open.","They encompass nothing of His knowledge except as\nHe wills. I think of my brother in those first days after\nmy arrest, panicking, calling everyone, his own pineal\ngland working through fear. Something in him found\nme. He did not know how. He was guided to A, who\nknew where I was. He received only what he was meant\nto receive, exactly when he was meant to receive it.\nAnd what I noticed in the cell is something this verse\nhints at quietly: the deepest help often flows through us\nwhen we are reaching out for someone else's problem,\nnot our own. The channel opens wider for the suffering\nof another than it ever opens for our own.\nHis Throne extends over the heavens and the earth.\nIn your body, the throne is the highest seat. There is a\nplace in the human head where the deepest stillness\nlives. The Sufis have always known this place. Modern\nscience calls it the pineal gland, the conarium, the small\npearl-like organ deep in the centre of the skull. The\nthrone of God, in some real sense, is not far away. It is\nthe highest part of you, the one that does not tire, the\none that does not sleep, the one that guards what is\ninside even when the rest of you has given up.","He feels no fatigue in guarding and preserving them.\nThat is the part of you that has kept you alive through\nevery night you wanted to give up. That part has never\nbeen tired. The body gets tired. The emotions get tired.\nThe conscious daily mind gets tired. But the deepest\nseat in you — the throne — has never closed for a\nsingle second since the day you were born. The Throne\nVerse is, among other things, a description of the silent,\nceaseless part of every human being.\n* * *\nThe Verse That Lets the Whole World In —\nAl-Baqarah 2:62\nThere is a verse in the Qur'an that I want every\nreader of this book to know, regardless of their religion.\nIt is in the same surah I memorised as a boy in the dark\nbefore Fajr. It is short. It is plain. It is one of the most\ngenerous sentences in any holy book ever written. And\nmost people, even Muslims, have never sat with it.\nThose who believe in the Qur'an, and those who\nfollow the Jewish scriptures, and the Christians, and the\nSabians — any who believe in God and the Last Day\nand work righteousness — shall have their reward with","their Lord. On them shall be no fear, nor shall they\ngrieve.\nRead it slowly. Read it again.\nThis is not the verse of a tribal religion. This is the\nverse of a God who is wider than any tribe. The Qur'an\nitself says it: the believer, the Jew, the Christian, the\nSabian — any who believe in God and do good — shall\nhave their reward. Not the Muslim only. Not the Arab\nonly. Not even the people of one particular book. Any.\nWho. Believe. And. Do. Good.\nThis verse alone broke open my understanding in the\ncell. I had grown up in a culture where some men would\ntell me that only one path leads to God. I had heard it in\nmosques. I had heard it in arguments. I had carried a\nquiet suspicion of it for years, because it did not match\nwhat I had seen with my own eyes. I had seen Hindu\naunties in Colombo who carried more peace in one cup\nof tea than some Muslim men carried in a whole life of\nprayer. I had seen Christian brothers in Malta who lived\nmore honestly than many of us who recited the Qur'an.\nThe world I had walked through did not look like a\nworld in which only one path was open. It looked like a","world in which many paths were open, and the people\non each path were being judged by how they walked,\nnot by the name of the road.\nAnd then, in a cell, I finally read the verse that said it\nplainly. From my own book. From the Qur'an my\ngrandfather had taught me before I understood a word.\nThe Qur'an itself says it: any who believe and do good\nshall have their reward. The Qur'an is not the wall some\nmen have built around it. The Qur'an is wider than its\nloudest defenders.\nThis verse is the spine of the spiritual chapter of my\nlife. It is the verse that allowed me to read the Bible\nwithout guilt. It is the verse that allowed me to receive\nConversations with God from a Filipino brother and not\nfeel that I was betraying anything. It is the verse that\nlets me sit, today, with a Hindu friend and a Christian\nfriend and feel that we are all walking toward the same\nend by different roads, and that the Qur'an itself\napproves of our walking. Strive together toward all that\nis good. Wheresoever you are, Allah will bring you\ntogether. The cultures differ, my friend. The God is one.\n* * *","The Sacred Mosque Inside Your Head — the Qibla\nverses\nThere is a passage in Surah Al-Baqarah about the\nQibla — the direction of prayer. In the early years of\nIslam, the direction had been changed from Jerusalem\nto Mecca, and the verses speak about this change and\nthe people who argued about it. On the surface, the\npassage is about geography. Underneath, it is about\nsomething much deeper.\nTo each is a goal to which Allah turns him. Then\nstrive together toward all that is good. Wheresoever you\nare, Allah will bring you together, for Allah has power\nover all things.\nFrom wheresoever you start forth, turn your face in\nthe direction of the Sacred Mosque. That is indeed the\ntruth from your Lord, and your Lord is not unmindful of\nwhat you do.\nI sat with these verses one afternoon in Cell 13 and I\nasked myself a question I had never asked before. Why\ndoes God give a direction at all? Why does it matter, to\na God who is everywhere, which way a human being\nturns his face?","And the answer that came to me, slowly, was this.\nThe direction is not for God. The direction is for us.\nGod is not located in Mecca. God is not located\nanywhere in the geographical sense. The Qibla is\ngiven so that scattered human beings, on a scattered\nearth, will face the same way at the same time. The\nunity is the point. The direction is just the tool that\ncreates the unity. Strive together toward all that is\ngood - that is the actual command. The Qibla is the\nmechanism that allows the striving to be done\ntogether.\nBut this is also where my heart becomes sad.\nBecause today many people face the Kaaba with\ntheir bodies while carrying anger in their hearts.\nThey go toward the same direction, but not always\nwith the same mercy. They stand shoulder to\nshoulder, but sometimes they still carry pride,\njudgment, argument, and division inside them.\nThe Kaaba was not given to make the human\nbeing arrogant. It was not given so one group could\nsay, only we are clean, and another group is dirty. It\nwas not given so people could fight over who belongs\ncloser to God. The Kaaba is a centre. A centre is\nmeant to gather. It is meant to pull scattered people\ninto one circle, one direction, one remembrance.\nIf a man faces the Kaaba but hates the person\nbeside him, he has understood the direction but\nmissed the destination.\n256","If a man walks around the Kaaba but his heart is\nwalking around ego, he has moved his body but not\nyet moved his soul. If a man says Allahu Akbar while\nlooking down on another human being, his mouth has\nspoken greatness, but his heart has not bowed to it.\nThis is what I mean when I say the Qibla is not\nonly outside. The outer Qibla teaches the body where\nto turn. The inner Qibla teaches the heart how to\nsoften.\nThe Kaaba is unity made visible. Love is that unity\nmade alive. Without love, the direction becomes only\ngeometry. With love, the direction becomes mercy.\nAnd mercy is the only proof that the prayer has\ntravelled deeper than the forehead.\nAnd then a deeper reading arrived, and I want to\nshare it carefully, as my reflection, not as a ruling on\ndoctrine. The sacred mosque, the masjid al-haram - is\nalso inside the human head. The deepest temple is\nnot in Mecca. The deepest temple is the seat of\nconsciousness inside the human skull. When you\nclose your eyes and turn your face inward, you are\nfacing the sacred mosque that every prophet has, in\nsome form, pointed to. The outer Qibla is the body's\ndiscipline. The inner Qibla is the soul's destination.\nBoth are real. Both matter. But the outer is the gate.\nThe inner is the room.\n257","Ask any religion that has gone deep enough. They\nwill all, in the end, point inward. The Hindu does it.\nThe Christian does it. The Buddhist does it. The Sufi\ndoes it. And the verse itself, read carefully, does not\ncontradict this. Wheresoever you are, Allah will bring\nyou together. The bringing together happens in the\ninner room, where the labels fall away and only the\nawake mind remains.\nI do not write this to argue with anyone's outer\npractice. I write it to widen the inner one. Keep your\ndirection. Pray your prayers. Face your Qibla. And\nremember, as you do, that the deeper Qibla - the one\nthe Qur'an does not name explicitly but points to in\nevery verse about turning the face - is the silent\nthrone inside your own head.\n* * *\nThe Prophets in One Line - Aal-e-Imran 3:84\nThere is a verse in the third surah, Aal-e-Imran,\nthat closes the door on any argument about which\nprophet is greater than which.\nSay: we believe in God, and in what has been\nrevealed to us, and in what was revealed to\nAbraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and the Tribes,\nand in what was given to Moses and Jesus and\nthe Prophets from their Lord. We make no\ndistinction between any of them, and to Him we\nsubmit.\n258","No distinction. No ranking. No tribal favouritism.\nThe same revelation, given through different mouths,\nin different centuries, to different peoples, in\ndifferent languages. The Qur'an itself names the\nprophets of the Jews and the Christians and instructs\nthe Muslim not to make distinctions between them.\nThe line is unbroken. The light is one.\nReading this verse in the cell allowed me to read\nthe Bible with a different posture. I was not reading\nanother religion's book. I was reading the longer\nversion of stories already told in my own. The story of\nYusuf, which the Qur'an gives in one beautiful surah,\nI read again in the Bible across four hundred pages,\nand I cried, because Yusuf is the model for every man\nwho has ever been falsely accused and thrown into a\nwell. He is the prophet of false accusation. He is the\nprophet of imprisonment. He is the prophet of\npatience that does not become bitterness. He is the\nprophet of forgiving the brothers who threw you\naway. I needed him. I read him. And the Qur'an itself\nhad told me, in this verse from Aal-e-Imran, that he\nwas already mine.\n* * *\nHellfire Is the Subconscious - a personal\nreading\n259","I want to write this carefully, because I am about\nto give a reading that is not the standard one, and I\nwant to be honest that it is my reading and not a\nruling.\nOur Lord, give us good in this world, and good in\nthe Hereafter, and save us from the punishment\nof the Fire.\nThis is one of the most famous prayers in the\nQur'an, repeated by millions of Muslims every day.\nSave us from the fire. The fire is usually understood\nas the punishment after death, the hellfire described\nin many verses, the consequence of a life lived\nagainst God.\nIn the cell, on the bed, alone, I came to a different\nunderstanding of this prayer, and I will share it with\nyou because I do not believe the standard reading is\nthe only one. The fire, for me, became this: the fire is\nwhat the subconscious does to a person when it is\nleft to run unchecked. It is the burning that happens\ninside a man, in this life, when his lower mind is the\nruler. The anxiety that does not stop. The shame that\ndoes not heal. The anger that does not cool. The\nlonging that does not rest. The hunger that the body\ncannot fill no matter how much it is fed. That is the\nfire. And it is burning many people, right now, in this\nlife.\n260","Save us from the fire - save me from being run by\nmy own subconscious. Save me from being a man\nwho burns himself daily through reaction and craving\nand resentment. Save me from being driven by what\nI cannot see in myself. The hellfire is not only\nsomewhere else. It is also right here, in the\nunawakened mind, every day.\nI will not impose this reading on anyone. The\nclassical reading remains. I only offer the inner one\nas well, because in the cell, the inner reading was the\none that woke me. And I think it would wake many\nothers, if they were quiet long enough to sit with the\nverse.\n* * *\nThe Soul Conceives, the Mind Creates, the\nBody Experiences\nThis is not a verse. This is a sentence that came to\nme in the cell, in my own voice, after weeks of\nreading the Qur'an and the Bible and Conversations\nwith God, after weeks of sitting with the wind\nthrough the window, after thousands of hours of\nthinking. It is the closest thing to a teaching I would\ndare to give. I want to write it carefully here because\neverything I understand about a human being can be\nfolded into this one sentence.\nThe soul conceives, the mind creates, the body\nexperiences.\n261","Read it slowly. Three movements. Three levels.\nThe soul conceives. Before any thought is formed,\nbefore any action is taken, before any word is\nspoken, something in the deepest part of you - the\nsoul, the rooh, the inmost seat of awareness -\nreceives or generates the seed of the next moment.\nMost people never feel this level. They live entirely in\nthe third one, the body, and assume the body is the\nwhole of life. But the soul is conceiving constantly, in\nthe background, generating the impulses that the\nrest of you will eventually act on.\nThe mind creates. The seed travels from soul to\nmind, and the mind shapes it into thought, plan,\nlanguage, intention. The conscious mind is the\narchitect. It takes what the soul has conceived and\ngives it form. Most people experience this level\ndimly. They notice their thoughts, but they think\ntheir thoughts are coming from nowhere, because\nthey cannot trace the line back to the soul.\nThe body experiences. Finally, the body carries\nthe thought into the world. Speech happens. Action\nhappens. Consequence happens. The body lives out,\nin time and space, what was conceived in the soul\nand shaped by the mind.\n262","And here is the warning of the sentence. When\nthe soul is corrupted by the subconscious - when the\ndeepest level is no longer pointing toward goodness\nbecause the lower mind has filled it with fear, ego,\ncraving, resentment - then the mind creates the\ncorrupted thought, and the body experiences the\ncorrupted life. And the man at the third level, the\nbody level, will look around and blame other people\nfor his suffering. He will say: my wife did this, my\nboss did that, the world is unfair, the country let me\ndown. He will not understand that what he is\nexperiencing in his body began with what was\nconceived in his soul. The river of his life began at a\nsource he is not looking at.\nTo wake up is to climb back upstream. From body\nto mind. From mind to soul. From soul to the silence\nthat is upstream of soul. When you do that, the river\nclears. The thoughts the mind creates begin to come\nfrom a soul that has been cleaned. The life the body\nexperiences begins to taste different. The blame falls\naway because you can finally see the source. That is\nwhat 114 days in a cell taught me. That is the whole\nspine of the book you are holding.\n* * *\nMy Words Changed Me\n263","There is a passage near the end of my cell notes\nthat I want to write here, because it is the practical\nconclusion of everything above. It is about the\ndiscipline of speech.\nShe did not change me. My words did not\nchange her. My words changed me.\nI had written this about Meme at first. I had been\ntelling her things on the phone every day for months,\nand I had assumed that my words were going out to\nher, changing how she saw me, shaping her view of\nwho I was. The cell taught me something else. The\ncell taught me that when a man speaks, he does not\nchange the listener. He changes himself.\nEvery word that leaves your mouth carves you a\nlittle. The mind that has been shouting for years has\ncarved itself into a shouting shape. The mind that has\nbeen speaking kindly for years has carved itself into\na kind shape. The listener may walk away\nunchanged. The listener may not even remember\nwhat you said. But you - the speaker - are the one\nbeing shaped by every sentence you release into the\nair.\n264","This is why the Hindu tradition of speaking to a\nstone is not as foolish as it sounds. If you have no one\nto talk to, speak to a stone. Speak to a tree. Speak to\na rock. The stone will not hear you. But you will hear\nyourself. And the hearing of yourself is the carving.\nThe shaping is not in the stone. The shaping is in the\none who spoke.\nSpeaking to God is the same. People say: God\nalready knows. Why pray with words? The answer is\nthat the prayer is not for God. The prayer is for you.\nThe act of speaking changes the one who speaks.\nPraying is correcting yourself. Asking is forming\nyourself. Confessing is reshaping yourself. The\nmouth is the chisel. The soul is the stone. And every\nprayer carves you a little more in the direction the\nprayer points.\nBe careful, then, with what you say. Every word is\nKun fayakun in slow motion. Every word is a small be\n- and it is. Choose your words. Speak the world you\nwant to live inside. Forgive the people who hurt you\nout loud, even if they will never hear you, because\nthe forgiving is what frees you, not them. The word is\nnot cheap. The word is the most powerful tool a\nhuman being owns. Use it as if every sentence were\ncarving you, because every sentence is.\n265","ACT TWO — THE CAGE\n32\nForty Steps Across the Street\nThe night before a court date, the officer's voice\ncame to the door.\nJabir — tomorrow, eleven o'clock. First van at eight\nthirty.\nIt is a strange thing to hear your own name spoken\nthrough a door at night. Especially the wrong version of\nyour name — the one the world had been calling you\nbecause of a clerical error decades ago. Jabir. I would\nlie on the bed and let the sound settle. The world\noutside still thought of me by that name. The court file\nstill called me that. The officer at the door called me\nthat. Only the people who loved me, inside and outside,\ncalled me by the name my grandfather had given me.\nAkmal. The name that meant complete.\nI would sleep what I could that night. Not much. The\nmind always knew when court was coming. The body\nremembered too — a particular tightness in the chest, a\nparticular dryness in the mouth, a particular way the","heart kept its own clock through the dark.\n* * *\nI woke at six. Everyone else still sleeping until eight.\nThat early hour was mine. I moved quietly through\nthe room. I made my coffee slowly, the way a man\nmakes coffee when he does not want to wake the others\nand does not want to rush the morning. I got ready in\nthe silence before the day demanded anything from me.\nThe early hour is a particular kind of mercy in a cell.\nThe walls are the same walls. But the hour belongs to\nyou, alone, in a way it does not at any other time.\nRay had already taken care of the white shirt the\nnight before. He had soaked it in a mixture of washing\npowder and fabric cleaner — things he had combined\ninto something that made white genuinely white. He\nhad wiped the collar himself, with his own hand. He\nhad wiped the armpits. When I stood ready, in the white\nshirt under the green blazer, he looked at me and said:\nyou look smart. We are really going now. Best of luck.\nAnd sometimes he would add, quieter: I went to church\nand prayed for you. You will go today.","He never said that for show. He said it the way you\nmention the weather. A man across the cell wall, of a\ndifferent religion, who went to a Christian chapel inside\na prison to pray for a Muslim man going to court. There\nis no neat sentence in any holy book that captures what\nthat meant. It was its own thing. It was the human form\nof God showing up in a prison cell at six in the\nmorning.\n* * *\nThen the van.\nWe sat inside, men from different parts of the prison,\non the way to different courts for different reasons. I\nnoticed everything during those rides. I noticed the ones\nwho crossed themselves when we passed a church. I\nnoticed the ones staring at nothing, trapped inside their\nown problem, not even looking out the window. I\nlooked. Trees. Buildings. Signs. People walking to\nwork. People going to buy bread. People living their\nordinary morning, with no idea that a van of locked\nmen was passing five metres from them. I looked\ndeliberately. The way you look when you know the\nmind forgets what it does not feed.","The route passed through Valletta streets. Past\ncorners I had walked freely just months earlier. Past the\nairport, in the distance, where the whole thing had\nstarted. The mind, in that van, did what the mind does\nwhen it suddenly moves through the world after weeks\nof stillness. It made wild calculations. It planned things\nit would never act on. It noticed the drainage grates in\nthe street and imagined escape routes. It noticed the side\nroads. It noticed how thin the locks on the van doors\nlooked from inside. The mind plays. The mind tests\nitself. The mind shows you, in those moments, what it\ncould do — and the awake part of you watches the mind\nplay and decides, quietly, no. Not this. We are going\nthrough the front door of this thing. Not the back.\n* * *\nInside the court holding area, bread and cheese were\nwaiting. The same bread and cheese as prison. My court\ntime was eleven, but I might not be called until three\nthirty. That is how the days went. Sometimes you\nwaited four hours past your hour for a session that\nlasted ten minutes. It did not matter. You waited.","I sat. I recited Qur'an quietly to myself, verses I\nknew without paper. I played chess in my mind without\na board — visualising the squares, the pieces, the\nopenings I had memorised long ago, moving through\ngames against an imagined opponent who was, of\ncourse, also me. I counted the fans on the ceiling. I\nwatched the light move across the floor. I waited with\nthe kind of patience that only builds inside a person\nwho has already learned, in a smaller room, that there is\nnothing useful in rushing.\nThe patience I had begun to build in Cell 13 was\nbeing tested here, in this holding area, by other men.\nMost of them did not have it yet. They paced. They\ncursed. They asked the officers the same question every\ntwenty minutes, hoping for a different answer. I sat. I\nwaited. I did not pace. And the watcher in me noticed\nsomething quietly important: the patience I had built in\nthe cell was beginning to be visible to other people. It\nhad started to show in the way I sat. That was the first\ntime I understood, in my body, that real character is\nsomething other people can see without you ever\nannouncing it.","* * *\nAnd then the walk across the street.\nThere is a public street in Valletta that separates the\ncriminal court from the family court. About forty steps\nwide, give or take. On a court day, when both courts\nneed you, they walk you across that street in the open.\nPeople going to work pass you. People going to lunch\npass you. People going about the completely ordinary\nbusiness of a free afternoon pass you. And you —\nblazer on, hands cuffed in front, an officer beside you\n— cross between the two buildings in full public view.\nForty steps. Maybe forty-five. I am not sure. In those\nforty steps, everything the situation meant was made\nvisible and physical. I was not hidden anywhere. I was\nnot protected by walls. I was a man in a blazer walking\nin handcuffs across a busy street where anyone could\nsee. Strangers. Tourists with cameras. Old Maltese\nwomen carrying shopping. School children. Office\nworkers on a smoke break. They all saw. Or some of\nthem did not see, because the human mind has a\nbeautiful trained skill for not seeing the embarrassment\nof strangers. But the option to see was there. I could not","control whether they looked.\nI walked. I did not look at the ground. I had decided\nlong before that walk that I would not look down.\nLooking down would have given the moment more\nshame than it deserved. The accusation was not yet\nproved. The trial was not yet finished. The man inside\nthe cuffs was still a man, still Akmal, still my\ngrandmother's grandson, still the boy who had crossed\nthe burning beach without showing his fear. So I\nwalked with my eyes forward, the way my grandfather\nwalked across the village even when the village judged\nhim for selling tobacco. He had taught me, without ever\nteaching me, that you keep your eyes forward when the\nworld tries to make you look down.\n* * *\nInside the courtroom I made myself completely still.\nNot just my body. My face. My eyes. I gave nothing\naway — not to confirm, not to deny, not even when\nthings were said about me that I knew were not true.\nMy lawyer spoke. I watched. I controlled even the\nmovement of my eyes, because I understood that the\nroom was reading me, and I chose to give the room","nothing to read. The judge looked. The opposing lawyer\nlooked. The court officers looked. And the man in the\nchair gave them no expression to interpret.\nLater I understood why this mattered so much. They\nwere observing me. But I had spent my whole life, since\nI was a boy on a roof in Sri Lanka, learning to be the\none who observes. So I observed them observing me,\nand I stayed still. The watcher had finally been given a\nroom in which the entire job was to watch. The cell had\ntrained me for the courtroom without me knowing it.\nEvery day of stillness in Cell 13 had been a rehearsal\nfor these particular minutes.\n* * *\nAfter the session, waiting for the van back —\nsometimes two thirty, sometimes much later — that was\nwhen I made my calls.\nI called my brother first. Then Saraswati. Then\nMeme. Short calls, always. The phone time was limited,\nthe queue was long, the recharge was expensive. Not to\nspeak at length but to inform: I am okay. Today was\nthis. The next date is this. Sometimes the call was to ask\nsomeone to recharge my prison account, because that","recharge was the only lifeline I had to keep calling. The\nwallet the prison gave each person each month was\ntwenty euros. That was everything — recharge, small\nnecessities, the rare purchase. I barely spent it, not\nbecause I had no money outside, but because spending\nfelt like surrendering attention to something that did not\nyet deserve it.\nThose calls were short. But they were the most\nimportant minutes of each court day. They were the\nmoments the outside world confirmed it was still there,\nstill holding a space for me to return to. The voices of\nmy brother and Saraswati and Meme, three minutes\neach, were the proof that the cage was not the whole\nworld. It was a room inside a world that still loved me.\nThe days accumulated. Court date, then thirty more\ndays, then another. The other men in the room learned\nto reframe it for each other. Only thirty days, they\nwould say to a man who had just been postponed again.\nThat is nothing. Let us have coffee. I will make bread.\nThat reframing was not false hope. It was the mind\nchoosing what to feed itself. It was awakened patience\noperating between two prisoners who had no other\ntools.","* * *\nParole came on a day that felt like other days.\nI was at the mosque inside the prison, or maybe at\nthe library — I am not certain anymore which one it\nwas. The officer found me. You got the paper, he said. I\nreceived it the way I had learned to receive everything\ninside those walls: without excess reaction. The mind\nhad already been prepared. The body simply followed.\nAm I excited to leave, I asked myself. Honestly, in\nthat moment, not really. Because I had learned to be\ncomfortable inside the walls. My mind was ready and\nmy body had accepted. To be at peace inside a cage is\nnot defeat. It is the deepest freedom there is — when\nyour peace no longer depends on where you are\nstanding.","ACT THREE — THE ROOFTOP\n33\nThe Door Opens Like a Womb\nIt was an ordinary August evening, the peak of\nMaltese summer, the kind of heat that makes everything\noutside shimmer and everything inside slow down.\nFor months, every court date had carried the\nimagination of this moment. The day they would say it.\nThe day the paper would come. The day a door I had\nstopped trying to imagine would actually open. The\nimagination of the moment had become almost a\nprivate religion inside me. It had a colour. It had a feel.\nIt had a particular soundtrack of how the news would\narrive. None of that turned out to be how it actually\ncame.\nOne afternoon, the prison officer simply walked over\nand said it plainly: you got parole. Your bail is ready.\nYour lawyer will call. That was the whole speech. Nine\nwords, give or take. The biggest news of my year\ndelivered in the same voice he used to tell men what\ntime dinner was. And I think, looking back, that was the","perfect way for it to arrive. If it had been ceremonial, I\nwould not have known what to do with it. The plainness\nof it matched the plainness I had learned in the cell.\nNews came. The body received it. The mind continued.\n* * *\nThe first thing I did was call Meme.\nI called her, and almost in the same minute I put her\non a conference call with my brother and Saraswati. I\ndid not plan this in advance. The body just did it. The\nthree people who had held me together from the outside\n— the voice that had called me every day, the brother\nwho had fasted for me, the sister-figure who had carried\nmy forty-six-kilo bag across Malta — were all going to\nlearn this together, in the same call, at the same minute.\nThey deserved that. After 114 days of separate phone\ncalls in which I had told each of them carefully edited\npieces of the truth, it felt right that the door's opening\nshould be one shared sound in three ears at once.\nI do not remember exactly what they said. I\nremember the sounds. Meme's particular intake of\nbreath. My brother's voice cracking on the second\nsentence. Saraswati saying my name in the way she","always said my name when she did not know what else\nto do. The four of us, on phone lines stretched across\nthree countries, briefly in the same room. The cage did\nnot break with a sound. It softened with the sound of\nthree voices receiving the same news, and a fourth\nvoice — mine — listening to them receive it.\n* * *\nThe leaving itself was bureaucratic and strange.\nThere were signatures. There were forms. There was\na return of the prison blankets — those itchy\npurple-grey wool blankets, the same kind of blanket\nthat had once been distributed to my family after the\n2004 tsunami by Sheikh Hizbullah's relief. The thread\nof that blanket reaching back across twenty years was\nalmost too much to hold in one thought, so I did not\nhold it. I just gave the blanket back.\nThen they handed me a white plastic garbage bag\nwith what remained of my 114 days inside. Books I had\nread. Pens I had used. The notebook Mike had given\nme. Handwritten notes on folded papers — the first\nprayers, the awakening songs, the eleven rules, the\nletters I had never sent. Biscuits a man named Tony had","given me on my birthday. Cookies. Small gifts from\ninmates whose names I want to write here but cannot,\nbecause most of them would not want their names in a\nbook.\nI put on my pink T-shirt — the one Saraswati had\nbrought weeks earlier, the one she had washed and\nbrought to the prison and that had waited in a locker for\nthis day. Over the pink T-shirt I put on the green blazer\nthat Ray had cleaned for me one last time. I picked up\nthe white bag and carried it over my right shoulder —\nthe Tamil way, the dignified way my mother had taught\nme when I was small. The man who walked toward the\ngate was not the man who had walked through it 114\ndays earlier. The clothes were almost the same. The\nbody underneath was completely different.\nSaraswati arrived by taxi, in heels, having run across\nthe city to get there. She was in a plain everyday outfit\n— not the pink dress some part of my memory had\nimagined for that moment. Just real clothes for a real\nday. She hugged me. The taxi was waiting. We drove to\nManoel Island.","And the outside world — with all its speed and noise\nand light — began again.\n* * *\nStepping out after 114 days did not feel the way I\nhad expected it to feel.\nIt did not feel like freedom. That is what nobody tells\nyou about getting out. It felt, for the first hours, like\nfear. The world was too fast. The cars moved too\nquickly. The colours were too bright. The voices around\nus in public spaces were too loud. The distances were\ntoo wide. I looked at people walking past me and my\nbrain stalled. How do I cross the street? How do I look\npeople in the eye? How do I breathe out here, where\neveryone is breathing at the same time?\nThe body, which had learned in 114 days to live in a\nsmall cell, did not yet know how to live again in a wide\nworld. The mind, which had become quiet, did not yet\nknow how to handle so much input at once. There is a\nkind of grief in those first hours that nobody warns you\nabout. You have been waiting for this day. And when it\ncomes, part of you wants the room back.","And then, slowly, two versions of me began to walk\ntogether. The new aware Akmal — the one Cell 13 had\nmade, the one who had read the Bible and the Qur'an\nside by side and written prayers in his own words on\nfolded paper — and the old dark Akmal, the one who\nhad walked into the airport in May with two pairs of\nunderwear on and no preparation for any of this. They\nwalked together, overlapping, protecting each other in\ndifferent ways. The old one knew the city. The new one\nknew the silence. Together, for that first afternoon, they\ncrossed into the rest of my life.\nThe new one said quietly, in my chest: you already\nknow this world. You have lived in it for thirty-four\nyears. You can handle it. Just walk slowly. Just look.\nJust breathe. You are not in the cage anymore. But the\ncage taught you something the people on this street do\nnot know yet. Use it. Do not perform it. Just use it.\nI walked toward the taxi. The door opened. I got in.\nAnd the womb of those 114 days closed behind me,\ngently, the way a womb closes — not slamming shut,\njust receding. The thing it had grown inside it was now\nout in the world. The world did not know yet. The\nworld would learn.","ACT THREE — THE ROOFTOP\n34\nPatience and the Rebuild\nThe first tool I used to survive prison was not\nstrength. It was patience.\nI did not understand this at the beginning. At the\nbeginning I thought I needed strength. I thought I\nneeded to harden the body, harden the face, harden the\nvoice. The cage had taken my freedom, so I assumed\nthe cage was going to test me through aggression —\nand that I would need to meet that aggression with a\nharder version of myself. That is what every film and\nevery prison story I had ever consumed had taught me.\nBecome harder. Stand taller. Speak less. Hit first.\nThat instinct was wrong. It was the body's first guess\nat how to handle a place the body had never been\nbefore. And like most of the body's first guesses, it\nwould have served me badly if I had followed it.\n* * *","In those early days, part of me still wanted to escape.\nNot through physical action — I knew that was not\npossible. Escape through the mind. Escape into fantasy.\nEscape into anger. Escape into hatred for the people\nwho had put me there. Escape into self-pity, which is\none of the deepest and most comfortable escape routes\nthere is.\nAs the days passed and my thinking deepened,\nsomething changed. I no longer wanted to run from the\nproblem. I wanted to face it. And in that small turning\n— from escape to facing — patience became more than\na virtue I was performing. It became survival itself.\nI was not a large man in prison. I was not one of the\nheavy ones with histories. I was one of the smaller,\nquieter ones, surrounded by men who had lived in\nviolent places and brought the violence with them in\ntheir muscles, their gestures, their way of standing in\nline. I made a decision in those first weeks that I have\nnever gone back on. I would not survive by fighting for\ndominance. I would survive by learning calmness.\nRespectful. Quiet when needed. A smile when a\nsmile was the right tool. No reaction to provocation,","even when reaction was expected. I let men shout past\nme without taking the shout into my body. I let small\ninsults dissolve in the air without giving them a target to\nland on. I let the room exist around me without trying to\ndominate it.\nAnd people noticed. That was the part I had not\nexpected. In a place built almost entirely on reaction,\nthe man who does not react becomes visible in a\ndifferent way. The other men did not respect\naggression. They had been around aggression their\nwhole lives. They respected the man whose face did not\nchange when the room expected it to. Patience, in that\nplace, became my first real form of power. Not loud\npower. Not dominant power. The quiet power of a man\nwho was not playing the same game everyone else was\nplaying.\n* * *\nI want to be honest about how this worked at first.\nIt was not entirely genuine. In the early weeks, my\npatience was a performance. Underneath the calm face,\nI was not always calm. Underneath the quiet, I was\nsometimes angry. Underneath the smile, I sometimes","wanted to break something. The performance was real\non the outside. The feeling underneath was different.\nBut I kept at the performance. I kept walking the\ncorridor calmly even when the body wanted to walk\nfast. I kept smiling at men I privately resented. I kept\nmy voice low when my throat wanted to rise. And here\nis the strangest thing I learned in those 114 days, the\nthing I want this book to leave with you if it leaves you\nwith nothing else: the performed patience slowly\nbecame practised patience. And the practised patience\nslowly became real patience.\nThat is how character is actually built. Not in one\ndramatic moment of choosing. Not in one big spiritual\nexperience. In thousands of small choices made in the\nsame direction, day after day, week after week. The first\ntime you choose calm over reaction, it is fake. The\nhundredth time, it is real. The thousandth time, it is who\nyou are. The body learns. The mind learns. The nervous\nsystem rewires. The man who was performing patience\nin week two becomes a man who simply is patient in\nweek sixteen. And there is no exact moment of\ntransition. There is only the long slow climb, in the\ndark, with no audience, until you reach the top and","realise you have become someone else.\n* * *\nWhen I came out, the growth did not arrive as one\nbig miracle.\nThat is also worth saying clearly. Some people,\nwhen they hear about a prison awakening, imagine the\nman walks out a saint. He does not. The man who walks\nout is the same man who walked in, plus 114 days of\nquiet practice. And the qualities he carries out arrive in\na particular order, not all at once.\nThe first quality that came back to me was surrender\n— not as defeat, but as a real, practical surrender to\nGod. The recognition that I had been trying to run\neverything from my own small body for thirty-four\nyears, and that there was a larger order I could now let\nmyself be part of without losing myself. Surrender first.\nThat was rule number one of life after the cage.\nThen patience in a deeper form. Not the borrowed\ncomfort patience of the outside world — the patience\nthat comes from knowing you can leave any room you\ndo not like. Real patience. The patience that was built\nunder pressure, the patience that had been tested by 114","days of waiting for a court date that kept moving, the\npatience that had outlasted the temptation to react.\nThen kindness — not the soft sentimental kindness\nof advertising, but the harder kindness of a man who\nhad seen his own ugliness clearly and decided to be\ngentler with other people because he understood, now,\nwhat carries every person through their day.\nThen awareness before reaction. The pause. The\nhalf-second of looking at the situation before\nresponding to it. That half-second is the difference\nbetween the man who keeps causing the same fires in\nhis life and the man who notices the fire before he lights\nit.\nThen a more serious practice of forgiveness. Not as a\ngift to the people who had hurt me. As a gift to myself.\nHolding grudges is heavy, and the cell had taught me\nhow few things in life are worth carrying.\n* * *\nPrison grounded me in ordinary life.\nThat is the strange paradox of those 114 days. The\nthing that removed me from ordinary life is the thing","that finally returned me to it properly. Before the cell, I\nhad been performing ordinary life — going through its\nmotions without inhabiting them. After the cell, I\ninhabited the simplest things. Cleaning. Cooking.\nMaintaining a space. Sweeping a floor. Washing a\nplate. Folding a towel.\nPeople sometimes ask me, on the outside, why I\nscrub a rented bathroom so carefully when I could just\nrent another one. Why I cook for myself when I could\norder food. Why I take care of small things that no one\nwill see. The answer is in the cell. Inside Cell 13 and\nthe dorm afterward, I learned that a man makes things\nbetter and more beautiful, not worse. That is human\nwork. That is the basic discipline of being alive. The\nroom you are in is the room you are in. If you can make\nit cleaner, make it cleaner. If you can make it more\nbeautiful, make it more beautiful. The room does not\nhave to belong to you for you to care for it. You are the\none inside the room. You are the one whose nervous\nsystem lives in that environment. Clean it. Make it kind.\nThat discipline never left me. I clean wherever I live\nwith the same care I learned to clean Cell 13 and the\nshared dorm floor. People do not always understand","why. The reason is simple. It makes me happy. It is\nwhat a human being is for.\n* * *\nSomewhere inside all of that — the surrender, the\npatience, the kindness, the awareness, the forgiveness,\nthe cleaning — a desire began growing in me.\nA desire to guide.\nNot to preach. Preaching is when a man stands above\nother people and tells them what to do. That is not what\nI wanted. I wanted to speak, to share, to offer some of\nwhat I had learned to people who were still living only\nthrough the body. People like me, before the cell.\nPeople who did not yet know that there was anything\nelse.\nI did not know yet, in those first weeks after release,\nthat the desire to guide would eventually become this\nbook. The book began before I knew I was writing it.\nThe patience built it. The reading built it. The eleven\nrules sketched its skeleton. The awakening songs gave\nit its heart. And the quiet desire to share, born\nsomewhere in those last weeks of the cage, gave it its\npurpose.","Patience and the rebuild. Those are the two halves of\nwhat came next. Patience to wait for the parts of me that\nwere still arriving. Rebuild for the parts that had to be\nbuilt fresh, from materials I had never used before. The\nman who walked out of the prison did not know yet\nwhat he was building. He only knew that the building\nhad begun, and that, for the first time in thirty-four\nyears, he was actually present for the construction.","ACT THREE — THE ROOFTOP\n35\nThe First Room After Prison\nThe first room I had after prison was not really\nfinished.\nThe court had arranged it as a condition of my bail.\nSomewhere I had to live, with an address that could be\nwritten on a form, with a landlord whose name could be\nverified, with a door that could be opened by a key only\nI held. The address mattered more than the room did.\nThe court needed to know where to find me. The room\nitself, beyond that, was almost an afterthought.\nIt was a small place in an old Maltese building.\nUnfinished walls in some corners. Tiles that did not\nquite meet the way they were supposed to meet. A\nbathroom with fittings that had been installed in a hurry.\nA kitchen counter that was more idea than counter. A\nbed. A door. A window that looked out at another wall.\nThe kind of room you can rent quickly when you do not\nhave time to be choosy, and the kind of room a landlord\ncan offer quickly when he understands that the tenant is","not in a position to negotiate.\nI did not complain about any of it. After 114 days in\na cell with one window and a thin mattress, an\nunfinished room with a real door I could lock myself\nwas a kind of palace. The body had been recalibrated.\nThe body now knew exactly what it needed and exactly\nwhat was extra. The unfinished tiles did not bother me.\nThe simple bed did not bother me. The narrow kitchen\ndid not bother me. The room had a door I could close\nfrom the inside, and that one fact made it luxurious in a\nway no five-star hotel will ever match for me again.\n* * *\nThe first night in that room, I did not know what to\ndo with myself.\nThat is the part nobody warns you about. In prison,\nevery minute of the day is decided by someone else.\nThe bell tells you when to wake. The officer tells you\nwhen to eat. The schedule tells you when to walk in the\nyard. The door tells you when you are inside and when\nyou are out. Your day is built for you, hour by hour, and\nyour only job is to inhabit the hours someone else has\narranged.","In the first room after prison, suddenly, no one was\narranging anything. The whole day was empty in front\nof me. I could eat now or in three hours. I could sleep\nnow or in the morning. I could go out or stay in. I could\nopen the window or keep it closed. I could turn on the\nlight or sit in the dark. Every small decision that the cell\nhad taken away from me was now back in my hands, all\nat once. And the body, which had been trained for\nmonths to wait for instructions, did not know how to\nbegin.\nI sat on the edge of the bed for what must have been\nan hour. The plastic-covered sofa across the room was\nsweating in the August heat. My own back was\nsweating against the bed. The phone in my hand was\nfull of messages from people who loved me, and I could\nnot bring myself to open most of them. There was too\nmuch world coming at me at once. The freedom that I\nhad been waiting for through every court date was here,\nin this room, and I did not know how to receive it.\n* * *\nI made my first coffee in that room slowly.","The way Ray had taught me to make coffee in the\ndorm. Quietly. Without rushing. The small ritual of\nboiling water, of measuring the powder, of stirring\ncarefully. I had learned, in the dorm, that a man's\nmorning coffee is one of the few things a prison cannot\ntake from him entirely, and that the way he makes it can\neither honour him or hurry past him. I had chosen to\nhonour the coffee. I kept choosing it now.\nI drank it standing at the kitchen counter, looking out\nat the wall opposite my window. There were no birds\nyet. There would be birds in the days that came, but not\non the first morning. The city was still mostly asleep.\nThe room was quiet in a way that was different from the\ncell's quiet. The cell's quiet had been imposed. This\nquiet was mine. I had chosen the silence by not turning\nanything on. That difference — the difference between\nsilence imposed and silence chosen — was the first real\nlesson of life after the cage.\n* * *\nIn those first weeks, I did what I had told myself I\nwould do. I followed the rules.","Rule seven. Read forty minutes a day. I read every\nmorning in that room, before the heat became\nimpossible. Books I had brought out in the white bag.\nBooks Saraswati brought me later. Books I bought at\nsmall shops with the little money I had. The discipline\nthat had been built in Cell 13 did not leave me when the\ncell did. I did not have to force myself to read. The\nbody now wanted reading the way it had once wanted\nthe gym.\nRule eight. Appreciate the breath, the unseen, the\nsmall things. I sat by the window many mornings and\ndid nothing for ten or fifteen minutes. No phone. No\nbook. Just the breath. Just the light moving across the\nunfinished tiles. I had learned in the cell that this was\nnot wasted time. This was the time the rest of the day\nwas built on. Most people skip it because they do not\nknow what it is for. I knew what it was for.\nRule four. Make outgoing calls. I called my brother.\nI called Saraswati. I called Meme. I called my mother\nsometimes, briefly, telling her only what she could bear\nto hear. I did not wait for any of them to call me first.\nThe cell had taught me that waiting for love to come to\nyou is a sleeping man's strategy. The awake man","reaches out.\nRule three. Appreciate. I thanked the landlord for the\nroom, even though the room was unfinished. I thanked\nSaraswati for everything. I thanked Meme on every\ncall. I thanked the small shopkeeper near my street who\nsold me bread without asking questions. The\nappreciation was not performance. It came from the\ncell. In the cell, every small kindness had been visible\nbecause there were so few of them. Outside, kindness is\neverywhere, and most people walk past it without\nnaming it. I tried not to walk past it.\n* * *\nBut I want to be honest, because this book is built on\nhonesty.\nThe first weeks in that unfinished room were not\npure peace. The mind, which had been calm in the cell,\nbecame restless in the room. The body, which had\naccepted the prison routine, did not yet know how to\nbuild its own. There were afternoons when I sat on that\nsweating plastic sofa and felt something close to panic\n— the panic of having too many options. There were\nnights when sleep would not come because the room","was too quiet. The cell had its sounds. The keys, the\nbells, the voices through the walls at night. The first\nroom after prison had only the sound of my own\nbreathing, and on some nights that was harder than the\ncell had been.\nI also missed people from inside. That surprised me.\nI missed Ray's voice in the next cell. I missed Mike\ntelling me about resistor colours. I missed the small\neconomy of the dorm — the fan passed under one\nname, the fruit behind the television, the birthday cake\nfrom Tony. The men I had served those days beside had\nbecome, without my noticing, a kind of family. And\nnow I was out, and they were still in, and the door of\ncommunication between us was almost completely\nclosed. I could not call them. I could not visit them. I\ncould only think of them. And thinking of them did\nnothing for them. That is one of the hardest parts of\ngetting out. The people you leave behind do not come\nwith you, no matter how much of them you carry inside.\nMike was still alive when I left that prison. I want to\nwrite that here in this chapter, because it matters to the\norder of things. He was still teaching the other men in\nthe dorm. He was still hiding letters under books. He","was still doing his thinking. The news that he had died\ninside came to me later, in this unfinished room, on a\nphone call that I am not yet ready to write. When it\ncame, I sat on the edge of the bed in the same place I\nhad sat on my first night out, and I cried for an old man\nI had known for less than four months who had shaped\nme more than most of the men I had known for\nthirty-four years. The room held my grief. The\nunfinished walls did not flinch. That is also what a first\nroom is for.\n* * *\nIt was in that room that I began to understand the\ndifference between freedom and ease.\nFreedom is being able to choose what you do. Ease\nis having every choice arranged for you so that you do\nnot have to think. Most people, when they say they\nwant freedom, actually want ease. They want a job that\ndecides their days. A relationship that fills their nights.\nA society that tells them what to want and then sells it\nto them. They want the prison without the bars. They\nwant the bells without the cell. They want someone else\nto make the small decisions so that their day is full and","they do not have to face the silence.\nReal freedom is harder. Real freedom means you sit\nin an unfinished room with all the choices in your hands\nand you choose, every hour, what to do. There is no one\nto blame for the empty hour. There is no schedule to\nhide behind. There is only you, your conscious mind,\nand the slow building of a life out of the choices you\nmake. The cell had taken away choice. The first room\nafter the cell gave it all back at once, and it took me\nweeks to learn how to handle the weight of it.\nAnd here is the strange truth I want to leave at the\nend of this chapter. The unfinished room did more for\nme than a finished one would have. A perfect\napartment, with everything in order, would have let me\nsleepwalk back into my old life. The unfinished room\nwould not let me. Every gap in the tile reminded me.\nEvery uneven counter reminded me. Every wall that\nneeded paint reminded me. The room said, every day:\nyou are not finished either. Build yourself the way you\nwould build this room. Take your time. Honour the\nsmall decisions. Do not skip the corners. A man and a\nroom are made the same way, by attention applied over\ntime, and nothing important is ever quite finished. You","just keep building until you stop.\nI stayed in that room until the court conditions\nallowed me to move. By the time I left it, I had read\nmany books in it. I had prayed many prayers in it. I had\ncried in it. I had made many small coffees in it. I had\nbegun, in scattered notes, the first sentences of the book\nyou are now holding. The unfinished room was where\nthe unfinished man came back to himself. And when I\nlocked the door for the last time and gave the key to the\nlandlord, I thanked the room out loud, the way I would\nhave thanked a teacher. Because that is what it had\nbeen.","ACT THREE — THE ROOFTOP\n36\nThe Signal and the Television\nLet me try to give you, in plain language, the picture\nof a human being that came to me in the cell. I do not\nclaim it is the only picture. I only claim it is the one that\nfinally let me understand myself after thirty-four years\nof confusion.\nImagine an old television set. The kind with a back\nand dials and a small screen. The television, by itself, is\njust plastic, metal, glass, wires, and a cord. It has no\npicture of its own. It cannot generate a single image\nwithout something else. The television is the receiver. It\nis the body.\nNow imagine the signal. The signal travels invisibly\nthrough the air. You cannot see it. You cannot hold it.\nYou cannot prove it exists by pointing at it. But the\nmoment the television is tuned correctly, the signal\narrives, and suddenly the empty plastic box is filled\nwith sound and colour and life. The signal is the soul.\nThe signal is what they call rooh in Arabic, what","Hindus call atman, what Christians call the spirit. The\nsignal is the thing that comes from somewhere else and\nuses the receiver to express itself.\nAnd in between, in the middle of the television, is\nthe tuner. The small mechanism that decides which\nsignal is being picked up. Tune one way, you get one\nchannel. Tune another way, you get static. Tune badly,\nyou get a distorted version of the actual broadcast. The\ntuner is the conscious mind. The watcher. The pineal\ngland. The thing that decides which signal the body\nreceives clearly and which signal is lost in noise.\n* * *\nNow hold that picture in your mind, because\neverything I have to say about being human is folded\ninside it.\nMost people, when they talk about themselves, talk\nonly about the television. The body. My face. My\nweight. My hair. My job. My salary. My possessions.\nMy country. My nationality. My language. They build a\nwhole identity out of the plastic box. They polish the\nbox. They upgrade the box. They compare their box to\nother boxes. They spend their entire lives convinced","that the box is who they are.\nSome people, the more reflective ones, talk about the\ntuner. The mind. The personality. The intelligence. The\ndiscipline. The character. They understand that there is\nsomething inside the box that determines what kind of\npicture comes through. They work on their tuner. They\nread books. They go to therapy. They meditate. They\nare right to do this work. The tuner matters.\nBut almost no one, in ordinary conversation, talks\nabout the signal. The signal is treated as fiction, as\nreligion, as something you either believe in or you do\nnot. The signal is what most modern life pretends does\nnot exist. And yet without the signal, the television is\nempty. The body without the soul is a corpse. The mind\nwithout the soul is mechanics. The signal is the source.\nThe signal is what makes any of this meaningful.\n* * *\nIn the cell, I came to understand that the great\nconfusion of human life is this: we think we are the\ntelevision. We polish the television. We feed the\ntelevision. We worry about the television's reflection in\nthe window. And when the picture is distorted, when","the colour is wrong, when the sound is off — we think\nthe television itself is the problem. We try to fix the\nplastic. We try to upgrade the box. We never stop to\ncheck whether the signal is being received clearly, or\nwhether the tuner has drifted off the channel.\nThe body is not the problem. The body is doing\nexactly what bodies do. The body cannot create its own\npicture. It can only display what the tuner picks up. If\nyour life is showing static, the static is not coming from\nthe body. The static is coming from a tuner that has\ndrifted away from a clear channel. The work, then, is\nnot on the body. The work is on the tuner. Bring the\ntuner back to the signal. Sit still. Listen. Adjust slowly.\nAnd the picture will clear.\nThis is what 114 days in a cell did for me. The cell\ncould not improve my body. The cell was the opposite\nof a gym. The body got smaller, weaker, paler, less\nimpressive. But the tuner — the conscious mind, the\nwatcher — was given the conditions to do its real job.\nWith no distractions, no errands, no chasing, no\nscrolling, no flirting, no eating out, no traffic, no salary,\nno notifications — the tuner finally sat still long enough\nto find the signal again. And once it found the signal,","the picture of my own life cleared, even though the\ntelevision itself was thinner and worse than ever.\n* * *\nThis picture also explains, finally, what I call Jibril.\nIn the Islamic tradition, Jibril is the angel of\nrevelation. He is the one who carried the word into the\nprophets. He is the messenger. In a thousand sermons\npeople make Jibril sound like a separate being with\nwings. Maybe he is. I do not claim to know what an\nangel really is. But here is what I do know, from the\ninside of my own head: there is something that delivers\nthe signal to the tuner. There is a channel that opens\nbetween the source and the receiver. That channel, in\nme, in my cell, when I sat quiet enough, opened. Words\ncame that I had not generated. Images came that I had\nnot summoned. Understandings came that I could not\ntrace to any book I had read.\nI do not call this miraculous. I call it normal. I think\nevery human being has this channel. I think we have\nbeen told, by the loud modern world, that this channel\ndoes not exist, that we are alone in our heads, that there\nis no signal — only the noise of our own thoughts. I","disagree. The signal is there. The channel is there.\nJibril, in whatever form the word means to you, is the\nname of the route between the source and the receiver.\nThe Sufis call this opening. The Hindus call it darshan.\nThe Christians call it grace. The neuroscientists may\neventually call it the activation of the\npineal-thalamic-cortical loop. I will not argue about the\nname. I will tell you only that it is real.\n* * *\nSo when I write, in the awakening songs chapter,\nthat 'in Islam they call it coral, in English they call it the\npineal gland, my conscious calls it Jibril, but my\nsubconscious called it Meme' — this is what I mean.\nMany names. One channel. One receiver. One signal.\nAnd the work of a human life, in the picture I am\ngiving you here, is simple to describe and hard to do.\nKeep the television clean. Care for the body. Do not let\nit rot. But understand that the body is not where the\npicture lives. Sit still long enough, often enough, that\nthe tuner can find the signal again. The tuner drifts. The\nsignal does not. The signal is always there. Your job is\nto bring the tuner back to it, again and again, every day","of your life.\nThat is the work. That is the whole work. Everything\nelse is decoration.","ACT THREE — THE ROOFTOP\n37\nKun — When the Word Becomes\nReal\nThere is a phrase in the Qur'an that appears more\nthan once, and the first time I really understood it was\nin Cell 13 when a calf walked past my mind during a\nrecitation.\nThe phrase is Kun fayakun. Two words. Be — and it\nis. God says be, and the thing comes into existence. The\nclassical understanding is that this is the description of\nGod's creative power. He does not need tools, materials,\ntime. He speaks, and reality follows. The universe was\nmade this way. The mountains. The seas. The cow. The\ncalf. The man. The breath in your chest right now. Be\n— and it is.\nI had read these two words a thousand times before\nthe cell. I had heard them in sermons. I had nodded\nalong. I had assumed I understood them. But\nunderstanding two words in the head is not the same as\nfeeling them in the body. The cell was where I felt","them.\n* * *\nThe way I came to feel Kun fayakun was through the\nsmallest thing — a thought I had about a calf.\nI had been reading Surah Al-Baqarah carefully. The\ncow. The whole surah turns on the story of the cow, the\noffering, the question of how God speaks to a people\nthrough an instruction. And one afternoon, lying on the\nbed, I thought about a calf on a street I had once seen. A\nsmall new calf, only days old, still wobbling on its legs,\nwalking near its mother in the dust outside a village\nshop. I had not thought of that calf in years. The image\narrived, the way images arrive when the mind is quiet\nenough to receive them.\nAnd as I held the image, something turned over\ninside me. The calf had not chosen to exist. The calf\nwas simply there, in the world, walking. Someone —\nsome force, some source, some Lord of the worlds —\nhad said be, and the calf was. Not after a long process.\nNot after many drafts. Be — and the calf is. The calf is\nthe proof of Kun fayakun walking around on four small\nunsteady legs.","And then the next thought, which I want to share\ncarefully, because it is the centre of this chapter. If the\ncalf was made by the word be, then my own life is also\nmade by the word be, every moment. Every breath I am\ntaking right now is a Kun fayakun in slow motion.\nEvery thought arriving in my mind is a Kun fayakun.\nEvery word leaving my mouth is a Kun fayakun. The\ncreative power is not just at the beginning of the\nuniverse. The creative power is continuous. It is\nhappening now. It is happening through me, around me,\nin me, every second.\n* * *\nAnd then the harder realisation came.\nIf God's word creates reality, and if I am made in\nsome way in the image of the source — created with\nbreath, with mind, with the capacity to speak — then\nmy words also create reality. Not on God's scale. Not at\nall on God's scale. But on the small scale of my own\nlife, my own family, my own house, my own\nneighbourhood, my own day. When I speak, something\ngets made. When I keep silent, something gets unmade\nor never made at all. The word is the tool of creation, at","every level.\nThis is what Mike was writing without knowing he\nwrote it, in that birthday letter for Meme. Just tap on the\nkeyboard, and behold, our wish will happen. He thought\nhe was being romantic. He was actually describing the\nuniverse. Tap the key. Be. And the picture appears on\nthe other end of the line. Kun fayakun, transmitted\nthrough fibre optic cable across the Mediterranean Sea,\nbetween a Sri Lankan man in a Maltese prison and an\nEritrean woman in a Dubai apartment, written by a\nChristian Maltese-Libyan man in his careful English.\nThe universe is being made all the time, in tiny acts of\ncreation, by ordinary people who do not know they are\ncreating.\n* * *\nThis is why the rules I wrote in the cell place so\nmuch weight on the word.\nThe word is not cheap. The word is the most\npowerful tool a human being has. People throw words\naway as if they were free, as if a sentence is just air. But\nevery sentence is a small be. Every promise is a small\nbe. Every threat is a small be. Every prayer is a small","be. Every gossip is a small be. The sentence leaves your\nmouth and something is made — in the listener, in the\nrelationship, in the air of the room, in the carving of\nyour own soul.\nIf you understand Kun fayakun even a little, you\nbecome careful with your mouth. Not silent. Not afraid.\nCareful. You learn that the words you speak about\nanother person make a version of that person in your\nown mind, and your mind then walks around in a world\nfull of those versions. You learn that the words you\nspeak about yourself build the man you are becoming.\nTell yourself a thousand times that you are nothing, and\na man of nothing will eventually arrive. Tell yourself a\nthousand times that you are awake, and an awake man\nwill eventually arrive. Be — and it is. The creation is\nslow at our scale, but it is creation all the same.\n* * *\nThere is a small story I want to tell here, because it\nties the chapter together.\nIn the cell, the prison phone cost money. Every\nminute on the call had a price. The price was not large\nin the outside world. Inside, where the wallet was","twenty euros a month, the price was real. You knew,\nwhen you spoke into the phone, that the speaking was\nbeing paid for, syllable by syllable. And something\nstrange happened to my speech because of that. I started\nthinking before I spoke. I planned the call. I asked\nmyself: is this sentence worth the coin it will cost? Is\nthis complaint worth two minutes of my account? Is this\n'how are you' going to lead somewhere useful, or is it\njust filler?\nAnd the answer, more and more often, was: no. The\nfiller was not worth it. The complaint was not worth it.\nThe repeated story was not worth it. So I started cutting.\nI started speaking only what mattered. And here is the\nstrange grace of that small economic pressure. The\ndiscipline that money taught me, by accident, was the\nsame discipline that Kun fayakun teaches as a principle.\nEvery word costs. Every word makes. So choose them.\nThe cell taught me through cost what the verse had been\nteaching all along through truth.\nOn the outside, the phone calls are free. The\neconomic pressure is gone. But the deeper pressure\nremains. Every word still costs. Every word still makes.\nThe fact that you cannot see the price tag does not mean","the price has been removed. The price has only been\nhidden. And the unawake speak as if speech were free,\nand they pay for it in coin they do not realise they are\nspending — the coin of their own being, slowly carved\nby every careless sentence into a shape they did not\nchoose.\nKun fayakun. Be — and it is. The universe began\nthis way. Your life continues this way. Every word you\nspeak is a small be. Make sure you mean them.","ACT THREE — THE ROOFTOP\n38\nBreath and the Bluetooth\nIf the body is the television and the soul is the signal,\nthen the breath is the bluetooth.\nI know that sounds strange. Stay with me. The breath\nis the silent, continuous connection between the body\nand the source. It is the thing that does not stop even\nwhen you sleep. It is the thing that nobody taught you\nand yet you have been doing it perfectly since you were\nborn. The breath is the closest, most ordinary, most\nignored proof that there is a channel between you and\nwhat created you.\nThe Qur'an says of God that He does not slumber,\nnor does He sleep. I have written about that line in the\nchapter on the Throne Verse. But the place that line is\nmost easily felt in the body is the breath. You can stop\nalmost everything else. You can close your eyes. You\ncan stop walking. You can stop speaking. You can stop\neating for a day, three days, a week. But the breath does\nnot stop. The body does not allow it. Something inside","you keeps the connection open whether you ask it to or\nnot.\nEvery breath in is a small reception. Every breath out\nis a small return. Air in. Air out. Receive. Release.\nReceive. Release. All day. All night. For decades.\nWithout your management. Without your appreciation.\nWithout your gratitude. The bluetooth between your\nbody and the source has been on, without interruption,\nsince the day you were born, and most people have\nnever even noticed it is on.\n* * *\nIn the cell, with nothing else to do, I started to notice\nthe breath the way you notice a sound after a long\nsilence.\nAt first I just felt the air going in and out of my\nchest. The simple physical fact. Then I started to notice\nthe temperature. Cooler going in, slightly warmer going\nout. Then the texture. Sometimes the air was dry,\nsometimes it had moisture in it. Sometimes it carried a\nsmell from the corridor — bread from the kitchen,\ndisinfectant from the cleaning rounds, the slight stale\nsmell of a building that did not see enough sun.","Then I started to notice the spaces between the\nbreaths. The tiny pause after the inhale, before the\nexhale. The tiny pause after the exhale, before the next\ninhale. Most people never feel these pauses. The breath\nis so automatic that the pauses are invisible. In the cell,\nwith no other movement to track, the pauses became\nvisible. And in those pauses, something interesting\nhappened. The mind went quieter. Just for a half\nsecond. Just for the length of the pause. And in that\nquieter half second, I could feel the channel more\nclearly than I could feel it during the breath itself.\nThat, I think, is what the Sufis have been pointing at\nfor centuries with their breath practices. That is what\nthe Tibetans are doing with their long visualisations.\nThat is what the Hindu yogis are doing with pranayama.\nThe breath is the gate. And inside the breath, the pause\nis the deepest gate. The work of stillness is not to add\nanything. The work of stillness is to notice what is\nalready there and walk through it.\n* * *\nThe bluetooth has a particular weakness. It is always\non, but the body is usually too busy to receive what is","coming through it. Imagine your phone, with bluetooth\non, in your pocket, while you are running on a treadmill\nwith music in your ears at full volume. The bluetooth\nsignal is arriving. But you cannot hear it, because the\nmusic is louder. You would have to stop the treadmill,\ntake off the headphones, and listen, before you could\nhear what the bluetooth has been quietly delivering all\nalong.\nThis is most modern life. The treadmill is the daily\nroutine. The music is the noise of phones, screens,\nopinions, news, conversations, ambitions, fears. The\nbluetooth is on. The signal is being delivered. And the\nman on the treadmill, with the music in his ears, has no\nidea that there is anything else available. He thinks the\nmusic is everything. He thinks the running is\neverything. He thinks the body is everything. He thinks\nhis thoughts are everything. He has no idea that there is\na quieter channel underneath, that has been transmitting\nhis whole life, and that he has never once tuned in to.\nThe cell, for me, was the moment the treadmill\nstopped, the headphones came off, and the bluetooth\nfinally became audible. Not because the signal had\nbecome stronger. The signal had always been at the","same strength. Because everything else had become\nquieter. The receiver finally had a chance to receive\nwhat the source had been transmitting since the day I\nwas born.\n* * *\nSo if you ever want to test what I am writing in this\nbook without going to a prison, here is the test. Sit\nsomewhere quiet. Close your eyes. Do not try to do\nanything special. Just breathe normally for ten minutes.\nNotice the air going in. Notice the air going out. Notice\nthe pauses. Do not push anything. Do not try to make\nanything happen.\nSome of you will get bored and stop after two\nminutes. That is fine. Some of you will get restless and\ncheck your phone. That is also fine. But some of you, if\nyou can sit for the full ten minutes, will notice\nsomething underneath the breath. A quietness. A\npresence. A faint sense that there is something here that\nyou had not noticed before. That is the bluetooth\nbecoming audible. That is the signal coming through.\nThat is the beginning of what the rest of this book is\nabout.","Do it once. Do it tomorrow. Do it the day after. The\nsignal is always being transmitted. The only question is\nwhether the receiver is quiet enough to receive it. The\ncell made me quiet enough. I am writing this book so\nthat you do not have to go to a cell to find what I found.\nSit. Breathe. Notice the pause. The channel is already\nopen. You only have to listen.","ACT THREE — THE ROOFTOP\n39\nWhat the Mind Sees\nThere is a kind of seeing that does not use the eyes. I\ndid not believe in it until I saw it. After the cell, on a\nquiet afternoon in Valletta, sitting near the water with\nmy eyes closed, the seeing arrived. And it changed what\nI thought a human being was for.\nLet me describe what happened plainly, so you do\nnot think I am exaggerating. I was sitting on a stone\nbench. The sun was warm. I had nowhere to go. I had\nbeen out of prison for a few weeks. The first room was\nbehind me. The bail conditions were still in place. The\ncourt was still in front of me. I had taken to going to the\nwater and sitting, sometimes for an hour, sometimes for\ntwo, because the discipline of stillness that the cell had\ntaught me did not leave me when the cell did. I needed\nto sit. The body needed to sit. The mind needed to be\nallowed to do its work.\nI closed my eyes. The sun pressed warm on my\neyelids. I did not try to meditate, in any formal sense. I","had not been trained in meditation. I had no teacher. I\nsimply sat, and I breathed, and I let the world be there\nwithout me commenting on it. And after some minutes\n— I do not know how long, time stopped meaning\nanything — a colour appeared behind my closed eyes.\nOrange. A warm, deep, glowing orange. Not the\norange of a sunset. Not the orange of a fruit. A\nparticular kind of orange that I have no word for in any\nof the four languages I speak. It was not coming from\noutside, because my eyes were closed. It was not a\nmemory of orange, because I was not picturing\nanything. It was simply there, in the inner field where\nmy closed eyes had always shown me nothing but\ndarkness, and now it was showing me a colour.\n* * *\nI sat with the colour. I did not chase it. I did not\nanalyse it. I let it be there, the way you let a small bird\nsit on a branch beside you without trying to grab it. The\norange held. It deepened. It softened. It pulsed slightly,\nthe way a flame pulses if you watch it long enough.\nAnd then, after some minutes, it faded, the way light\nfades when a cloud moves across the sun.","I opened my eyes. The world was the same. The\nwater, the stone, the warm air. I was the same. Or\nalmost the same. Something small had moved inside\nme. I had seen something that I had not been able to see\nbefore. The mind, which had been a thinking machine\nmy whole life, had finally shown me what else it can do\nwhen the body holds still.\nIn the Sufi tradition, this colour has a name. They\ncall it Noor. The light. There is a long literature on it,\nwritten by men far more learned than I am. They\ndescribe it in many forms — sometimes orange,\nsometimes gold, sometimes a deep white. They say it is\nthe first sign that the channel has opened. I am not\nwriting this to claim that I am one of them. I am writing\nit because I want anyone reading this book to know that\nthe description in those old books is not invention. It is\ndescription. The men who wrote about Noor were\ndescribing something they had seen, the way a traveller\ndescribes a mountain he has visited. I have not visited\ntheir mountain. But I have seen, from my own small\nbench, a glimpse of the colour they were writing about.\nThe Sufis are not lying. There is something there to see.\nThe seeing does not require their pedigree. The seeing","requires their patience.\n* * *\nModern science also has language for this. The\nnervous system, when it slows down past a certain\nthreshold, begins to release particular chemicals. The\nvisual cortex, when not occupied with outside images,\nbegins to display its own. The pineal gland, deep in the\ncentre of the head, becomes more active in deep\nmeditation. Researchers have measured these things in\nlaboratories. They use words like alpha waves and theta\nwaves and gamma synchronisation. I am not trained in\ntheir vocabulary. I cannot reproduce their\nmeasurements. But I can tell you that whatever is being\nmeasured in those laboratories is the same phenomenon\nthe Sufis were describing when they wrote about Noor.\nDifferent languages. Same event.\nThis is one of the great quiet truths I want to leave\nwith this book. Science and religion are not opposites\nwhen both are honest. They are simply describing the\nsame human being from different doors. The mystic\nenters from the door of practice. The scientist enters\nfrom the door of measurement. The man inside the","house is the same man. Both visitors can be telling the\ntruth at the same time. The body, the brain, the nervous\nsystem, the soul, the signal — these are not enemies.\nThey are the same human being, observed by different\nobservers, using different tools.\n* * *\nAfter that afternoon in Valletta, I started to test what\nthe eyes-closed seeing could do. Not for show. Not for\nspiritual collection. Just to find out what was possible.\nSome afternoons the orange came back. Some\nafternoons it did not. There were days when I closed my\neyes and saw only darkness, no matter how long I sat.\nThose days taught me something also. The channel is\nnot a vending machine. You cannot put your coin in and\nget a colour out. The channel opens when it opens.\nYour work is to keep sitting. Your work is not to\ndemand that the colour appear. The seeing is a gift. The\nsitting is your part.\nAnd over months, slowly, other things appeared.\nSometimes a face I had not thought of in years.\nSometimes a sentence I had not heard before, arriving\nin my own voice, telling me something I needed to","know. Sometimes a small understanding about a person\nin my life, arriving with the clarity of something written\ndown. None of this was dramatic. None of it was the\nlightning-bolt mystical experience that books often\ndescribe. It was all small. Quiet. Like a friend leaning\nover and saying something useful in your ear while you\nare sitting together on a bench.\nThat is what the mind sees when the eyes close and\nthe body holds still. Not visions of the future. Not\npredictions. Not magic. Quiet small useful gifts from\nthe part of you that has been waiting your whole life for\nyou to sit down long enough to listen.\n* * *\nIf you take only one practical thing from this whole\nbook, take this. Sit somewhere quiet, every day, for ten\nor fifteen minutes, with your eyes closed. Do not have a\ngoal. Do not try to see anything. Do not try to think a\nparticular thought or feel a particular feeling. Just sit.\nJust breathe. Just let the body hold still.\nMost days nothing dramatic will happen. That is\nfine. Most days are the building of the discipline. And\nthen, one day, when you are not looking for it, the","orange will arrive. Or some other small gift. And you\nwill understand, in your own body, what the Sufis were\nwriting about, what the scientists are measuring, what\nthis book has been trying to describe. You will\nunderstand that the eyes are not the only way to see.\nThe mind, in stillness, has its own seeing. And that\nseeing is one of the deepest birthrights of being human,\nand almost no one in modern life ever exercises it.\nSit. Close your eyes. Breathe. Wait. The light is\nalready inside.","ACT THREE — THE ROOFTOP\n40\nThe Three Minds and the Beggar\nThere is a passage in Conversations with God — the\nbook the Filipino brother gave me in the cell — that I\nhave thought about more than almost any other passage\nin any book I have read. It is a simple story, told as an\nexample. A man walks down the street. An old woman\nis sitting on the corner, begging for change. The story\nwalks you through what happens inside his mind in the\nnext few seconds, and in doing so, the author shows you\nthe three minds of a human being at work.\nLet me retell the story in my own words, because the\nstory did to me what good stories do — it became mine\nafter I read it. I no longer remember exactly how the\noriginal was worded. I only remember what it taught\nme. I am writing here from the version of it that lives in\nmy own head after the cell.\n* * *","A man walks down a street. He is in a hurry. He is\non his way to something. An old woman sits on the\ncorner of the pavement. She is small, hunched, holding\na tin cup. She does not call out. She just sits there,\nhoping.\nThe man passes her. And in the half second after he\npasses, three things happen inside him.\nFirst, an impulse. A small warm movement in the\nchest. Give her something. Make her day. He even sees,\nfor a moment, what he could do. He could pull out a\nfive-euro note. He could place it in the cup. He could\nsmile at her. He could make a small grand moment in\nher ordinary hard day. The thought arrives clean and\ngood. He almost turns around to do it.\nThat impulse is the conscious mind. That is the\nawake part of him. That is the watcher speaking. That is\nthe deepest, truest part of the man, recognising another\nhuman being and wanting to help. It arrives first. It\narrives clean. It does not calculate. It just wants to give.\n* * *\nThen, a second voice arrives. Faster than the first.\nCalculating.","What are you, crazy? You have seven euros for the\nwhole day. You have lunch to buy. You have a bus to\ncatch later. You have your own life to manage. You\ncannot give her five. Maybe a one. Or some coins. Find\nsome coins. You can spare some coins.\nThat is the subconscious. That is the calculating,\nprotecting, accounting part of the mind. It is not evil. It\nis doing what it was built to do. It is trying to keep you\nsafe and fed. It is doing the work of survival. It is the\nmind that learned, somewhere in childhood or earlier,\nthat resources are limited and must be managed.\nMost people, when they look back at moments like\nthis, think the subconscious is them. They think: well,\nthe conscious impulse was naive, the subconscious is\nbeing realistic, this is just adult thinking. But that is the\nwrong interpretation. The subconscious is doing its job.\nBut its job is not to be in charge. Its job is to advise.\nWhen it becomes the boss, when it overrides the\nconscious impulse, the man loses the deepest part of\nhimself.\n* * *","And then a third voice arrives. Lower, harder, less\naudible. Close to the bottom.\nJust walk. You do not have to deal with this. Look at\nyour phone. Look at the building across the street. Do\nnot even slow down. Get past her quickly. You will\nthink about it later. Move.\nThat is the unconscious. That is the deepest, oldest,\nmost automatic part of the mind. The part that runs the\nbody without your permission. The part that has\nlearned, over years and years, to avoid uncomfortable\nfeelings before they fully arrive. It does not even argue\nwith the impulse. It simply pulls the body away from\nthe situation before the situation can be fully felt.\nAnd the man, walking down the street, does what\nmost men do. He fumbles in his pockets. He looks for a\ncoin instead of the note. He gets embarrassed. His\nfingers brush against his keys, his rings, the diamonds\non his hand if he wears them. He is suddenly aware of\nwhat he has and how visible it is. The shame arrives.\nAnd in the shame, he does not give the coin either. He\nkeeps walking. Past her. Past the moment. The cup\nremains empty. He remains poorer than he was, though","he kept his coin.\n* * *\nThat is the three-minds picture, walking across a\nsingle street corner.\nAnd here is what the story taught me in the cell,\nmore clearly than any sermon I had ever heard. The\nunawake man lives at the third level. He moves through\nhis life avoiding the small moments the universe places\nin front of him. He passes the old woman. He passes a\nchance to apologise to his brother. He passes a chance\nto call his mother. He passes a chance to thank a\nstranger. He passes a thousand small openings in a\nsingle year, and at the end of the year he wonders why\nhis life feels small. The life feels small because he kept\nmaking it small by walking past the openings.\nThe slightly more awake man lives at the second\nlevel. He hears the subconscious. He calculates. He\nsometimes gives the coin instead of the note. He\nsometimes apologises but partially. He sometimes calls\nhis mother but distracted. He participates in life, but\nalways with one eye on the budget. He is not stingy,\nexactly. He is just managed. The subconscious is in","charge, and the subconscious does not let any impulse\nrun free.\nThe awake man lives at the first level. He acts on the\nimpulse. He gives the five. He turns around. He\napologises fully. He calls his mother and gives her his\nfull attention. He does not let the calculating second\nvoice override the clean first voice unless there is a\ngenuine reason. He understands that the universe sets\nup these small moments for him to do exactly the thing\nthe impulse asked for, and that walking past them is the\nslow erosion of his own soul.\n* * *\nThis is also what I mean by act before think.\nMost people are taught, all their lives, to think before\nthey act. And that is good advice for the unawake. The\nunawake man, acting without thinking, will hurt people\nand break things. He should be told to slow down. But\nthe awake man is different. The awake man's first\nimpulse, the one that arrives before the calculation, is\nusually the right one. The calculation that follows is\noften the subconscious trying to talk him out of his own\ngoodness.","When I tell people now that sometimes you have to\nact before you think, I do not mean act recklessly. I\nmean trust the first clean voice. Trust the impulse that\narrived warm and uncalculated. Give the coin. Make the\ncall. Send the message. Apologise. Forgive. Show up.\nBefore the second voice has time to write its careful\ncolumn of reasons why you should not.\nThis is the practice of the awakened. It is small. It is\ndaily. It does not announce itself. It is the man who\nkeeps doing the impulse-thing for years, until the\nimpulse-thing has become his ordinary way of being.\nAnd then, one day, people start to notice that he is\ndifferent. They cannot say why. They cannot point at a\nsingle dramatic act. They just feel that the room is\nwarmer when he is in it, that life is a little better when\nhe is around, that he is the kind of man who notices the\nold woman on the corner.\nI want to be that man. I am not yet. I am still under\nconstruction. But I know now, because of the cell and\nthe book the Filipino brother gave me, that this is the\nconstruction. The construction is the daily choice\nbetween the three voices. Choose the first. Trust the\nfirst. Walk back if you must. Place the note. Look her in","the eye. Make the moment grand. The universe set it up\nfor you. Do not walk past.","SPIRITUAL CORE · TEACHING CHAPTER\n41\nLight Is Dark\nMany times in the Qur'an, God speaks of light.\nPeople hear the word and immediately make the same\nsmall mistake. They draw a picture in their head.\nUsually the picture is the sun. So when their belief\nsystem tells them God is light, they replace the deeper\nmeaning with the small picture. They begin to associate\nGod with the sun. And once that happens, they have\nshrunk God into a thing the body can see — when the\nwhole point of the verse was to point at a reality the\nbody cannot see.\nLight, in the deepest sense, is not the sun. Light is\nthe closest thing science has so far identified to\nsomething that is not bound by time. The faster a thing\nmoves toward the speed of light, the more time slows\ndown around it. That is not poetry — it is physics,\nmeasured, peer-reviewed, confirmed across a century of\nexperiments. Whatever is not limited by time is the\nclosest thing we can measure to the nature of God.","Light is the only physical thing we know that begins to\nescape time. That is why so many traditions reach for\nthe image. It is the nearest the body can come to\nunderstanding the timeless using only its bodily senses.\n* * *\nBut God says clearly — do not project the examples\nI give you as if they were Me. The example is for you,\nto help you live, to help you point your mind in the right\ndirection. The example is not God. The sun is not God.\nThe light is not God. The light is a fingertip pointing at\nGod. Do not bite the finger. Look where it is pointing.\nPeople before you asked the same questions. If God\nis light, what about the dark? Is the dark not also God's\ncreation? The Qur'an addresses this. Of course the dark\nis also created. Of course God made both. The example\nof light was given because human beings live by light\nduring the day. The example is a tool for the mind. It is\nnot a description of God's body, because God does not\nhave a body.\n* * *","Here is the sharpest part of the teaching. You can\nfind that light in the darkness.\nThat is what God is actually saying.\nWhen you close your eyes, the first thing you see is\ndarkness. Pure darkness. Nothing. Most people open\ntheir eyes again immediately, because they are afraid of\nthat nothing, or bored by it, or restless. They never sit\nlong enough to find what is on the other side of the\ndarkness.\nBut if you sit. If you close your eyes for ten minutes\ntoday. For twenty minutes tomorrow. For forty minutes\nnext month. For longer, slowly, with patience —\neventually the darkness changes. It is not darkness\nanymore. It is not light in the ordinary sense either. It is\nthe light that exists inside the dark. The Sufis have a\nname for it. Anyone who has sat long enough has met it.\nThe orange light. The faint glow. The Noor. The thing\nthat arrives only when the eyes have been closed long\nenough that the body has stopped expecting it.\nThat is the light God was pointing at. Not the sun.\nNot the lamp. Not the candle. The inner light that lives\nbehind the closed eyes of a still human being. Stop time","long enough, and the light appears. That is the practice.\nThat is the door. That is what the verses have been\npointing at for fourteen hundred years.\n* * *\nA small practical detail. I do not always use electric\nlight during my practice now. Sometimes I use a candle.\nThe old way. Why? Because even electricity carries a\nkind of energy that the nervous system feels — Wi-Fi\nfrequencies, electromagnetic fields, the low background\nhum of modern rooms. You feel it in your body. You do\nnot always trust it. So I sit, sometimes, by candlelight,\nthe way the deeper traditions have always sat. The\ncandle is small. The flame moves. The body relaxes.\nThe mind softens. And in that softer condition, the inner\nlight arrives more easily. The body, when it is no longer\nfighting the room, finally lets the eyes find what they\nwere made to find. Not the candle in front of you. The\nlight behind the closed eye.\nOne day you will close your eyes and the darkness\nwill not be dark anymore.\nThat is the promise. That is the destination of the\npractice. It is not far. It does not require a guru. It does","not require a special tradition. It requires sitting still,\neyes closed, breath slow, long enough that the darkness\nbecomes the light.\n* * *\nWhy does night exist at all? Why did God create a\npart of the day where the light is removed from us? So\nwe could experience the inner light.\nDuring the day, the eye is occupied by the outer\nlight. There is no need to look inward. But at night,\nwhen the outer light is gone, the eye finally has a\nchance to find the inner one. Night is not punishment.\nNight is the door. The day feeds the body. The night\nfeeds the soul — if the soul is awake enough to receive\nwhat the night is offering.\nAnd we also sleep at night because the body needs\nrest. Sleep is autopilot. Sleep is the entropy God built\ninto the creature so that even a man who never wakes\nconsciously can still survive. If a man will not awaken\nto the inner light, let him at least sleep. Then he comes\nback to me later, and I answer him then, instead of\narguing with him now.","That is one of the most merciful sentences in the\nwhole of creation. God does not force the awakening.\nThe man who is not ready sleeps tonight, and tomorrow\nnight, and a thousand nights more, until something in\nhim finally breaks open and he sits long enough to find\nthe light behind the closed eye. God is willing to wait.\nThe light is willing to wait.\n* * *\nBut waking is the beautiful thing. The Qur'an itself\ntells you — rise in the last third of the night. If your\nheart is troubled, recite then. The third of the night is\nthe deepest hour for the practice, because that is when\nthe outer world is most quiet. The traffic has stopped.\nThe phones have stopped. The neighbours are asleep.\nThe body of the city is silent. And in that outer silence,\nthe inner light has its easiest chance to appear. The\ndarkness around you is the deepest. The darkness inside\nyour closed eyes can finally meet it. And in that\nmeeting — in that quiet hour when the world is asleep\nand you are not — the light behind the dark begins to\nshow itself.","He created you. He shows you. He teaches you. All\nof it — every page of your life — is one long arrow\npointing at the same place. The final blank. The night\nwhere the darkness becomes the light. The page where\nnothing more needs to be written.\nReach it, while you are still alive.","SPIRITUAL CORE · PILLAR CHAPTER\n42\nThe Fire Is Cooking You Now\nWhen you spread hate between yourself and another\nperson, a fire is produced inside you. This fire does not\nstay in the air between you and them. It enters your\nbody. It produces fear. It produces heartbreak. It\nproduces anxiety. It produces pain. It produces illusion.\nIt produces hatred. It produces a thousand small\nchanges in your behaviour that you do not even notice\nin yourself anymore.\nThat is not from God. That is from what we call\nshaitan, what we call evil, what we call the\nsubconscious darkness inside the unawake mind.\nThe Qur'an speaks, in many places, of fire — of\nburning here and burning in the hereafter. Most people\nread those verses literally and imagine a fire after death,\nsomewhere else, in another world. But how is it\npossible to burn here? If you actually burned, your body\nwould die. So how can the Qur'an say you burn now?","The answer is in the centres of the body. One of\nthose centres is the belly. And what happens in the belly\nevery day, all day, your whole life? Food is being\ncooked.\n* * *\nLook at what actually happens when you eat. You\nput rice in your mouth. You put vegetables. You put\nbread. You put meat. You put water. You put salt. You\nput sugar. You put oil. All of these go into your\nstomach, and they do not stay as separate ingredients.\nThey are cooked together. The body breaks them down,\nmixes them, transforms them, extracts what it needs,\nexpels what it does not. The stomach is a pot. The body\nis a fire under the pot. You are cooking inside yourself,\nevery minute, every hour, every day, all your life.\nThat is the fire the Qur'an is also pointing at. Not\nmetaphor. Mechanism. Your belly is burning right now.\nYour liver is burning right now. Your cells are burning\nglucose right now. Every breath you take is feeding that\nfire. You are a small kitchen on legs, walking around.\nSo when the Qur'an says you will burn in fire here\nand hereafter — yes, the deeper layers of meaning exist","for the afterlife. But the first layer is right here. The fire\nis the cooking of your own body. The fire is also the\ncooking of your own emotions — the anxiety, the\nanger, the resentment, the heartbreak — all of these are\nreal chemical and energetic reactions burning inside\nyou, right now, while you read this sentence.\nHellfire is not waiting for you somewhere else.\nHellfire is the recipe you have been cooking inside\nyourself for the years you have been unawake. And\nparadise is the same kitchen, with a different cook —\nthe conscious mind, choosing what enters the pot.\n* * *\nThere is wisdom in why traditions warn about excess\nsugar and excess salt.\nYour grandfather, and his grandfather before him,\ntook one teaspoon of sugar in tea. That was enough.\nThe body received it. The blood transported it. The\nenergy circulated. The body kept moving. The small\namount was a tool. The body knew what to do with it.\nThen, slowly, over the last fifty years, sugar and salt\nstopped being amounts and started being cultures. They\nbecame identities. They became wealth signals. They","became design choices. White rice versus red rice\nversus brown rice as a class signal. Refined sugar in\neverything. Salt in foods that do not need salt. The\nmolecules that were once medicines became markers of\nstatus. People stopped eating for the body and started\neating for the image of the body that they wanted other\npeople to see.\nThe body's purpose was forgotten. The kitchen was\nhijacked by the costume department. And the fire inside\nstarted cooking the wrong things, producing the wrong\nreactions, building the wrong inner climate.\nSugar and salt are not enemies. Forgetting why the\nbody needs them is the enemy.\n* * *\nRemembrance is the most important practice.\nForgiveness is the mercy of God — and forgiveness is\nforgetting.\nWhen you ask God to forgive you, what do you\nexpect God to do? He will forget. That is what\nforgiveness is, at its root. He will not hold the file open\nagainst you any longer. He will close it. The page will\nbe unwritten. The slate will be cleared. That is the","mercy.\nBut — and here is the test — if you ask forgiveness\nfrom another person, that person also has to forget. If\nthey say yes, I forgive you, but in their heart they keep\nthe file open, keep replaying what you did, keep\nsecretly counting against you — that is not forgiveness.\nThat is a polite lie.\nReal forgiveness requires forgetting. If the heart\ncannot forget, the person has not yet reached the final\nblank. They are still carrying. Still cooking the old fire.\nThis is why most human forgiveness is incomplete.\nPeople say the word forgive without performing the act\nof forgetting. They keep the wound on a low simmer in\ntheir belly, and the fire there continues to cook them\nslowly from the inside, year after year, until they die —\nstill angry about something they claimed years ago to\nhave forgiven.\nYou cannot reach the final blank carrying old fires.\nThe forgetting is the only way to put them out.\n* * *","The fire inside you can be raised, or it can be\nlowered. You are the one who decides.\nYou raise the fire when you eat too much. When you\neat the wrong things. When you eat for the wrong\nreasons. When you stay hungry too long out of\ncarelessness. When you let anger sit in the belly without\nreleasing it. When you let resentment cook on a low\nsimmer for years. All of these raise the fire. The body\ngets hotter. The mind gets cloudier. The conscious gets\nharder to reach.\nYou lower the fire when you sit in stillness. When\nyou fast. When you reduce what enters the body to only\nwhat the body actually needs. When you breathe\nslowly. When you stop adding wood to the inner flame\nand let it burn down to a gentle, useful warmth — the\nkind of warmth that cooks the body's daily work\nwithout burning the body itself.\n* * *\nThe body knows how to fix itself, if the man stops\ninterfering with it. Stop feeding the fire wrong things.\nStop cooking grudges in the belly. Stop drowning the\nsystem in sugar, salt, alcohol, noise, drama. Just stop.","Sit. Breathe. Fast. Reduce. Be still.\nAnd the body, given that quiet, begins its own repair.\nThe skin closes its small cuts. The blood clots correctly.\nThe digestion settles. The breath deepens. The sleep\ndeepens. The mind — without you forcing it — also\nbegins to clear. Because when the body is okay, the\nmind is okay. The two are not separate machines. They\nare one creature, and they fix each other when the man\nstops fighting them.\nThe body knows. The body has always known. The\nbody just needs the man to get out of its way.\n* * *\nA warning, sharp, locked. There are many teachers,\ngurus, priests, and self-styled spiritual men in the world\nwho have learned to hurt themselves in the name of\ndiscipline. They starve their bodies into illness. They\nbeat themselves. They live in ways that damage rather\nthan heal. They call this practice. They call this\ndevotion. And then they teach the same hurting to other\npeople — and other people follow them, because\nsuffering looks impressive from the outside, and most\npeople confuse suffering with depth.","But hurting yourself is not awakening. Hurting\nyourself is the unconscious mind running the body in a\ndifferent costume. It is the third-level man — the\nunconscious, the one who walks past the old woman on\nthe corner — now wearing a teacher's robe and\nexplaining that the walking past is actually spiritual\nprogress.\nThe body is not the criminal. I want to say this one\nmore time, even if I have said it before. The body is not\nthe criminal. The body was given to you as a blessing,\nas a tool, as a vehicle for the mind to wake up inside.\nYou are not supposed to hurt it. You are not supposed\nto teach others to hurt theirs. The mind is what needs to\nwake up. The body just needs to be cared for so the\nmind has somewhere to do its work.\nThe safest centre to live from is the conscious mind,\nthe throne, the seat above the belly's fire and below the\nchaos of the world. That is the safe centre. That is\nwhere the awake man lives. Locked in, not because he\nis imprisoned, but because he has finally found the only\nroom inside himself that does not burn.","The fire was not only outside; it was cooking the\nman within.","SPIRITUAL CORE · PILLAR CHAPTER\n43\nOne Day They Will Call You Human\nThe Qur'an speaks, in many places, about\nconsequences. About account. About what happens to\nthe people who do harm. Sometimes it speaks in\nlanguage that, on the surface, sounds severe. Even unto\nthe children. Even unto the family. Even unto the\ngenerations.\nPeople hear those verses and immediately think the\nworst. God is going to kill the children of the\nwrongdoer? God is going to punish the body of\nsomeone who did nothing? No. That is the surface\nreading. That is the village reading. That is the reading\nof a mind that has not yet sat still long enough to\nunderstand what the verse is actually pointing at.\nThe body, in the Qur'an, is never the thing being\npunished. Read the verses carefully. The body is\ndescribed as a blessing. The body has a purpose. The\nbody has a reason. The body was given to you so that\nthe mind — your conscious, your throne — could","experience love in this world, through this skin, through\nthese hands, through this breath. The body is the\ninstrument. The body is not the criminal.\n* * *\nSo what is being punished, if anything? Not the\nbody. Not the children's bodies. The mind that refused\nto wake up. The mind that lived inside the body but\nnever took the throne. The mind that let the\nsubconscious run everything, that let the body fight the\nwrong fights, that forgot the purpose of arriving here in\nthe first place.\nThat mind is what carries consequence. Not because\nGod is cruel. Because the unawake mind is its own\npunishment. Hellfire is the subconscious burning the\nbody now, in this life — already written in another\nchapter of this book. The fire is not waiting for after\ndeath. The fire is here. And the unawake man passes\nthat fire down — through his behaviour, through his\nanger, through his unforgiveness, through the small\ncruelties of his daily life — to the people around him,\nincluding, sometimes, his own children.","Not because God is punishing the children. Because\nthe unawake father is the punishment. The mind that did\nnot wake up becomes the inheritance the next\ngeneration has to deal with.\n* * *\nThe body cannot escape entropy. The body cannot\nleave the wheel of being born, growing, decaying,\ndying. That is the physical law God built into this\nworld. Everybody's body will go through this. The\nsaint's body. The thief's body. The prophet's body. The\nmurderer's body. None of them escape entropy. The\nbody never escapes. That is the design. That is part of\nwhy we are here.\nSo when people argue, fight, kill, defend — they are\nfighting at the level of bodies, at the level of entropy,\nwhere nothing is actually being decided. Two sticks of\nentropy fighting each other. Two bodies arguing about\nwhich body is right, while both bodies are dying at the\nsame rate, both losing time at the same rate, both\nsubject to the same physical law. The fight is\nmeaningless at the body level.","The meaning is one level higher — at the level of the\nmind. The conscious mind is what God placed inside\nthe body to lift the body above entropy. The mind is the\none that can wake up. The mind is the one that can\nexperience love. The mind is the one that can choose\nthe awakened life. And the mind is the only thing that\nwill be asked the questions, when the questions are\nasked.\nThe body cannot answer for itself. The body just did\nwhat the mind told it to do. The mind is the one that\nwill be questioned.\n* * *\nThey will call you many things in this life. They call\nyou by your job. They call you by your money. They\ncall you by your wife or your husband. They call you by\nyour country. They call you by your religion. They call\nyou by your nationality. They call you by your salary.\nThey call you by your social media name.\nOne day, they will call you human.\nThat is the whole teaching in one line. The day the\nworld calls you human is the day you have finally\narrived at the only name that matters. All the other","names are temporary. All the other names are\nbody-names. The name human is the name of the\nawakened mind that finally took its seat on the throne.\nMost people die without ever being called by that\nname. Most people die as the engineer, the father, the\nMuslim, the Sri Lankan, the immigrant, the prisoner,\nthe husband, the worker. They never reach the name\nunderneath all the other names. The Qur'an, the Bible,\nevery real tradition — all of them are trying to bring the\nreader to the moment where the deeper name finally\narrives. Human. Not Akmal. Not Jabir. Not Sri Lankan.\nNot Muslim. Human.\nThat is the final blank, from the Light Is Dark\nchapter, called by a different name. The final blank is\nalso the final name. The same destination. Different\ndoors.","SPIRITUAL CORE · PILLAR CHAPTER\n44\nThe Sound of Kun\nFirst, let me say something to the reader about what\nkind of book this is.\nThis is not a book about Lamborghinis. It is not a\nguide to buying twenty houses. It is not about how to be\nrich. Those books already exist. The world is full of\nthem. I am not writing one of them.\nWhat I am writing about is something much smaller,\nand much harder. The very thin, very fine fight between\nyour own body and your own mind. The one that\nnobody else can see. The one that has nothing to do\nwith cars, houses, salaries, or status. If you came to this\nbook looking for the other kind, put it down. Save your\ntime.\n* * *\nWhen gratitude is real, hate is gone. Love is gone.\nBoth are gone. They are no longer needed. Because hate\nand love are reactions to whether life is giving you what","you want. Gratitude is the state where you have stopped\nmeasuring what life is giving and started simply\nreceiving it. In gratitude, there is nothing to react to.\nThere is only what is — and the awake\nacknowledgement that what is, is enough.\nFifteen hundred years ago, a man needed a horse.\nNothing more. He did not cry about cars, because cars\ndid not exist. He did not imagine missing a sports car,\nbecause there was no such thing to miss. His desires\nwere shaped by his time. His suffering was shaped by\nhis time. He suffered only over what he could conceive\nof.\nTen years from now, you will not cry about the bike\nyou cry about today. Whatever frustration is consuming\nyou right now — about a phone, a car, a watch, a brand,\na salary, a comparison with someone else's life — ten\nyears from now, you will not even remember it clearly.\nThe thing you wanted will be obsolete. The brand will\nhave changed. The model will be discontinued. You\nwill laugh, ten years from now, at the small bike you\nalmost cried over today.","Time exposes the entropy of every desire. The horse\nwas enough then. The car is not enough now.\nTomorrow's car will not be enough either. Because\ndesire is not about the object. Desire is about the\nunawake mind always reaching for the next thing. The\nobject is interchangeable. Only the reaching is constant.\nAnd the reaching is what gratitude finally cures.\n* * *\nYour body is part of entropy. It is born, grows,\ndecays, dies. It is on the wheel. It cannot escape. But —\nand this is the warning — sometimes you have to shape\nthe body, not just feed it. Most people only feed the\nbody. They cover the body in clothes they think are\nflattering. They feed the body in ways that make them\ncomfortable, not in ways that make them awake. They\nuse the body as a costume to win small social fights —\nlook at my watch, look at my shape, look at my outfit.\nThat is feeding entropy. That is letting the body drag\nthe mind into wars the mind never agreed to fight.\nTake care. Take care. The body is part of entropy,\nand entropy does not lead anywhere good if it is left to\nrun by itself. The body must be shaped by the mind —","toward stillness, toward yoga, toward gratitude, toward\nthe fast, toward the discipline of one meal a day when\nthe mind judges that one meal is enough.\nAnd here is the deep relief — the body itself can\nnever harm God or the creator. The body is doing what\nbodies do. It has done its purpose simply by existing.\nThe body is innocent. The body is a vehicle. The mind\nis the driver. The mind is the one being asked.\n* * *\nIf you believe in science, follow the science down.\nLook closer.\nThe body is not one thing. It is many. The body is\nmade of organs. Organs are made of tissues. Tissues are\nmade of cells. Cells are made of molecules. Molecules\nare made of atoms. Atoms are made of neutrons,\nprotons, electrons. And around 2022, science went one\nlayer deeper, into what they call string theory.\nWhat is a string?\nYou ask yourself the question carefully. String. The\nword means something. When a guitar string is\nplucked, it makes a sound. When any object vibrates, it","makes a sound. Sound is what a string produces when it\nmoves.\nAt the deepest layer that science has so far been able\nto measure, the universe is not made of solid things. It is\nmade of vibrating strings. And every vibrating string is\nproducing a sound.\nThat sound is the sound of Kun.\n* * *\nKun fayakun. Be — and it is. The Qur'an's two-word\ndescription of creation. The thing I have been writing\nabout across this whole book. God spoke, and the\nuniverse came into being. For fourteen hundred years,\npeople read this as a story. A poetic image. A line in\nscripture.\nNow science is quietly catching up. The deepest\nlayer of physical reality is vibration. Vibration is sound.\nSound is the audible form of a word. The universe is, at\nits deepest measurable level, a word being continuously\nspoken. Every atom in your body is, right now, a tiny\nstring vibrating with the residue of that original Be. You\nare not a static object. You are a sustained sentence in\nthe mouth of God. Every breath is a continuation of the","Kun. Every cell is a held note.\n* * *\nAnd so the fight. You — the unawake man —\nbecame this miraculous, vibrating, Kun-spoken being,\nand what did you do with it? You started fighting for\nsmall love. You started crying over bikes. You started\nchasing women instead of recognising the love that\nwomen were sent to embody. You started arguing over\nreligion instead of understanding the verses that were\ntrying to teach you what you actually are.\nYou were built from the sound of God's word, and\nyou spent your life fighting like a small body in entropy\nover things that ten years from now you will not even\nremember.\nThe whole purpose of being here was to understand\nlove and accept gratitude. That is it. That is the only\nassignment. Every other achievement — the\nLamborghini, the twenty houses, the salary, the\nfollowers, the wife, the praise — is decoration. The\nassignment is to wake up enough to understand that you\nare a vibrating sentence of God, that the people around\nyou are also vibrating sentences of God, and that love is","the only correct response to recognising this. Gratitude\nis the only correct posture for receiving this. Everything\nelse is wasted breath.\n* * *\nI sometimes wonder how the Prophet knew this. He\nhad no laboratory. He had no equipment. He had a cave,\na stillness, and a channel that opened. And what came\nthrough that channel matches what science is still\nuncovering today.\nPeople play with the Qur'an like a counting game.\nThe word good appears this many times. The word bad\nappears this many times. The number this matches the\nnumber that. That kind of reading has its place. But it is\nshallow. The deeper knowledge in the Qur'an is not in\nthe counting. It is in the meaning. And the meaning,\nwhen you finally read it slowly, in a language you can\nunderstand, with the conscious mind awake — the\nmeaning is staggering. You begin to suspect that what is\nwritten in those pages came from somewhere that\nfourteen-hundred-years-ago Arabia could not, by itself,\nhave produced.","That is when the book stops being a religious\nobligation and becomes a living document.\nI read the Qur'an differently now. I just read, and my\neye goes on doing the action, and I am just looking at\nmyself. I am damn serious. Not reading the Qur'an.\nBeing read by it. The eyes move across the page, the\nbody does the action, and the conscious mind sits\nbehind and watches itself being shaped by what it is\nreading. That is the deepest form of reading. The book\nhas stopped being information. The book has become a\nmirror.","LATE SPIRITUAL CORE · TEACHING CHAPTER\n45\nMan and Woman — The Form Love\nTakes\nThis thought came to me one minute into listening to\na Nietzsche lecture on a podcast, and I want to share it\ncarefully, because it is one of the deepest things I have\nunderstood since I came out of the prison.\nWhatever you believe — religion, system, atheism,\nno system at all — there is one thing every human being\nmust accept. You were created by a mother and a father.\nYou came from an act of love. Even if you do not\nbelieve in any God, even if you reject every scripture,\nyou cannot reject this simple fact: at the deepest\nbeginning of you, there was an act of love between two\nhuman beings. That is where every single one of us\ncame from. No exceptions.\nSo at the deepest point, every human being is the\nproduct of love wanting to experience itself in human\nform.\n* * *","Let me take this further. Imagine love itself, asking\nthe question: how do I experience myself in this\nphysical world? How do I feel my own nature, when I\nhave no body of my own?\nAnd love answers: I will create a being just like me.\nA being that carries my nature in human form. That\nbeing will be woman. The human form of love.\nThen love says again: but for love to be felt, love\nmust also be received. Love must also be sought, asked\nfor, searched for. So I will also create a seeker —\nsomeone who reaches toward me, who chases me, who\nexperiences me through wanting me. That seeker will\nbe man. And to the seeker I will give consciousness —\nthe conscious mind, the throne, the pineal seat — so\nthat he can search and eventually find me.\nAnd love adds, carefully: do not forget. The seeker\nand the sought are both my creation. The man and the\nwoman are both me. Neither is above the other. Both\nare forms of love wanting to feel itself through human\nskin.\n* * *","Man came down into the body. The body needed\nfood — food was provided. The body needed water —\nwater was provided. The body needed shelter — shelter\nwas provided. And the body needed to experience love\n— and love arrived in the form of woman.\nThis is the part I want you to read slowly. Love\ncomes with everything, because we live in entropy. It is\nnot easy to keep love. Love does not stay automatically.\nLove must be honoured, recognised, received. The God\nwho gave you love in the form of a woman did not\nplace an obligation on her to remain. He placed an\nopportunity in front of you to recognise her. The\nrecognition is your part.\nYou still have a kind of control — the way you have\ncontrol of your garden, of your cow, of your land. You\npour water, the plant grows. You do not own the\ngrowing. You only participate in it. The same with the\nwoman in your life. The same with every relationship.\nYou are not the master. You are the gardener. You\nparticipate. You do not command.\n* * *","And here is the line I want every reader to carry\naway.\nDo not forget that woman is the form love took, so\nthat you in your human body could experience love at\nall. That is the only reason you are here. She was\nalready blessed by God when she arrived in your life —\nthat is why she is here. She is here to support, to help, to\nguide, sometimes to distract — and even the distraction\nis part of the experience love asked for when love\nagreed to come down into a body.\nSo do not call her your opponent. Do not call her\nyour commander. Do not call her nothing. Do not call\nher a slave. Do not reduce her to one purpose. You\nchose to accept the love by being born into this world.\nHow then can you ignore the form love has taken to\nfind you?\n* * *\nThis is the test. The test does not happen in the heart,\nexactly. The test happens in the pineal gland — in the\nconscious mind, on the throne, where the awake\nawareness lives. The man who passes the test is the man\nwho recognises that the woman in front of him is the","form love has taken to test whether he can recognise\nlove at all.\nThe unawake man sees only her body. He uses her,\njudges her, ranks her, possesses her, compares her. He\nmisses the entire reason she was sent into his life.\nThe awake man sees what brought her to him. He\nsees the love behind the form. He honours the woman\nbecause honouring her is honouring the love that placed\nher in his life. And in honouring her, he is also finally\nhonouring his own arrival on this earth.\nIf you understand this through the book — through\nthese few paragraphs — I am amazed. Most people read\ntheir entire lives without ever reaching this thought. If it\nhas reached you, something rare has happened between\nus in this conversation.","LATE SPIRITUAL CORE · TEACHING CHAPTER\n46\nDestiny Starts with Nothing\nPeople hear the phrase destiny starts with nothing\nand they immediately misunderstand it. They say —\nwhat do you mean, nothing? I have no house, no car, no\ncow, no savings. What destiny can come from nothing?\nYou are misunderstanding the word. Nothing, in this\nteaching, is not the absence of objects. Nothing is the\nnothingness inside the mind — the silent commanding\ncentre, the place that tells the lungs to breathe, the legs\nto walk, the mind to think. That nothingness is not\nempty. That nothingness is the source. Destiny does not\nbegin from poverty of objects. Destiny begins from the\nstillness inside the head — the throne, the pineal, the\nconscious seat — the part that has been issuing your\nbreath and your heartbeat since the day you were born.\n* * *\nMost people only meet that nothingness when they\nare in trouble.","The subconscious handles the easy days on its own.\nYou go to work, you eat, you scroll, you sleep — the\nsubconscious runs all of it. The conscious mind is not\ncalled for any of this. The conscious sits unused, like a\ntool in a drawer that has been forgotten.\nThe conscious gets called only when the\nsubconscious cannot manage. When fear arrives. When\npanic arrives. When the body realises it has run out of\nits usual answers. That moment of panic is not a\npunishment. That moment is actually the subconscious\nknocking on the door of the conscious — saying,\nplease, I do not know what to do, come and help me.\nAnd in that knocking, the conscious wakes. The\nbreath comes back. The breath goes inward. And the\ninward breath begins to speak — not commanding, not\ndemanding, just speaking what is truly needed. That is\nprayer.\nPrayer is not commanding God to give you things.\nPrayer is not demanding from God. Prayer is the\nconscious mind, finally awake, speaking honestly inside\nthe body about what the body actually needs.","The way most people pray — commanding,\ndemanding, give me this, fix that, take this away — is\nhurting themselves first. The Qur'an warns about this in\nmany places. When the heart hardens, when the pineal\nis no longer working properly, the subconscious runs\nthe whole body and the body grows tired, restless,\nexhausted. That is not how a human being is supposed\nto live.\n* * *\nLet me give you an image from my own life that\nmay help you understand how the subconscious works.\nIn 2003, when my father returned from the Gulf and\nbrought home our first Pentium III computer, one of the\nfirst programs I worked with was a small antivirus. I\nwill not name it here exactly because I want to keep the\nexample general. I learned the program. I used it. It\nprotected the computer in those years.\nDo I still use that antivirus today? No. The version is\nobsolete. The program no longer suits the modern\noperating systems. I learned from it, I grew, and I let it\ngo. Today I only use what I learned — how to scan,\nhow to protect, how to keep the system clean. The","specific 2003 program is gone. The skill it gave me\nremains.\nThat is exactly how the subconscious works.\nYour subconscious is your old antivirus. It was built\nin your childhood. It was useful then. It learned a\nthousand small protections that helped you, as a small\nchild, survive your family, your school, your village.\nBut you are no longer that small child. You are a grown\nhuman being with a conscious mind. You should not\nstill be running 2003 software in 2026.\nWhat you need now is the lesson from those years,\nnot the program itself. Eat what you learned to eat.\nWalk how you learned to walk. Run how you learned to\nrun. Read how you learned to read. But do not let the\nold protective software keep running in the background,\nscanning every new moment of your adult life as if it\nwere still the threat of 1999.\n* * *\nAnd here is the deeper picture. What most people\ncall the real world — the world in front of their eyes —\nis actually a moving image generated by their own\nsubconscious. The subconscious of the subconscious is","the third world you see in front of you. It keeps\nchanging every moment.\nThe father gets rich — the picture changes.\nThe mother gets sick — the picture changes.\nThe mayor becomes powerful — the city changes.\nThe country improves — the country changes.\nA hundred years ago, completely different. Today,\ncompletely different.\nWhen everything is constantly changing like this,\nthat motion is what we call entropy. And entropy by\nitself does not move toward love, toward God, toward\nanything good. Entropy moves toward whatever you\nkeep choosing. Toward whatever Kun you keep\nspeaking.\nBe — and it is. Be — and it is. Be — and it is. A\nthousand small be's every day, mostly unconscious,\nbuilding the world the body then walks through.\nSo take care. Take care. The word you speak builds\nthe world you live in. The subconscious you do not\nupdate will keep choosing for you. And one day you\nwill look around and ask — how did I end up here? You","ended up here because the old antivirus was still\nrunning while you were not looking.","LATE SPIRITUAL CORE · TEACHING CHAPTER\n47\nOne Side Is Enough\nThere is a saying I heard somewhere, and it landed in\nme the right way. Hate cannot be erased by hate. Only\nlove can erase hate.\nThat is the law. It is not a feeling. It is mechanics. If\ntwo people are caught in hate — the one who hurt you,\nand you in return — the situation cannot move forward\nas long as both of you keep feeding the same fire. Hate\nadded to hate produces more hate. Always. Without\nexception. That is not philosophy. That is what hate is.\nThe only thing that can break the cycle is love —\nand love only needs to enter from one side to break it.\nYou, or your opponent — only one of you has to choose\nlove. Not both. One is enough. Because love is not a\ntransaction. Love is a release. The moment one person\nstops feeding the fire, the fire begins to die, even if the\nother person is still throwing wood at it.\n* * *","This is the test the universe places in front of every\ndifficult relationship. God wants to reveal love to this\nworld. Every conflict between two human beings is,\nunderneath, an invitation from love asking: which of\nyou will be brave enough to release the hate first? I\nwant to come through. One of you has to open the door.\nMost people wait for the other person to open it. Let\nhim apologise first. Let her admit she was wrong first.\nLet him come to me first. That is the universal posture.\nAnd so the door stays closed for years, sometimes for\ndecades, sometimes until one of them dies and the door\nis closed forever.\nThe awake man does not wait. He opens the door\nfrom his side.\nNot because the other person deserves it. Not even\nsimply because love deserves it. Because love itself\nchose to enter this world through human bodies, and\nyou are one of the bodies love chose. You still hold\nlove, even when the other side refuses.\nSometimes that person will never understand love\nuntil the day they die. That is allowed. The door does\nnot have to open from both sides for love to remain real","on one side.\n* * *\nAnd when their door is sealed — bless them. Do not\ncurse them.\nNever curse the people who hurt you. Never curse\nyour ex. Never. The unawake man, when he cannot\nbreak through to someone who hurt him, curses them.\nHe calls them names. He wishes them harm. He hopes\ntheir life goes badly. He keeps the wound open by\nspeaking poorly of them — and every curse he speaks\ncarves the curse deeper into his own soul, not theirs.\nThe curse does not land on them. The curse cooks in\nyour own belly, in your own fire, in your own\nsubconscious, for years — long after they have\nforgotten you. You become the prisoner of the curse\nyou spoke. The unawake man spends his whole life\ncursing people who are no longer even thinking about\nhim. The awake man blesses them once, lets them go,\nand is free that same evening.\nBless them. Say quietly, even alone in your own\nroom: I wish you peace. I wish you healing. I wish you\nthe awakening you have not yet reached. The blessing is","not for them. The blessing is the medicine that heals\nyou of having to carry them anymore. When you bless\nthe person who hurt you, you finally release them from\nthe cage you have been keeping them in inside your\nown chest. They walk out. And so do you.\n* * *\nIf neither chooses love, what comes, comes. What\npasses, passes. What does not come, does not come.\nThat is honest. The teaching is not a guarantee. It is\nan invitation. If both sides refuse to choose love, the\nsituation simply runs its course. Whatever was going to\narrive will arrive. Whatever was going to pass will pass.\nThe universe does not force the door open. The universe\nonly waits for one of the two to open it.\nThat is the patience of God. That is also the patience\nof the awakened man. You cannot force another person\nto choose love. You can only choose it yourself, and\nthen accept whatever happens after that — because\nwhat comes after the choosing is no longer in your\nhands.\nYour work is to choose. The rest is the universe's\nwork.","LATE SPIRITUAL CORE · TEACHING CHAPTER\n48\nEarn the Gratitude\nRecently, modern science and the manifestation\nmovement have been saying — pure gratitude attracts\nwealth. Be grateful, and abundance will come. People\nhear this and they jump to the wrong end. They start\nperforming gratitude in order to get the wealth. They\nmake a list. They say thank you to the universe in the\nmorning. They wait for the abundance. And when it\ndoes not come fast enough, they say the manifestation\ndoes not work.\nThey got the order backwards.\nThe peak of gratitude is hidden inside wealth and\ninside woman — not because wealth and woman are\ngratitude, but because they are forms of love. Wealth is\none form love takes in this world. Woman is another.\nBoth are arrivals of love into the human experience.\nThe unawake man chases the money and never finds the\nlove hidden inside it. The awake man honours the love,\nand the money becomes part of how that love is allowed","to flow.\n* * *\nSo here is what people get wrong. They think\ngratitude is the trick that gets them the wealth. It is not a\ntrick. Gratitude is not a tool. Gratitude is a practice — a\ndiscipline that, when done long enough, changes the\nman, and the changed man then naturally receives more\nof what life is offering him.\nYou do not perform gratitude. You earn it. Every\nday. Through practice.\nBegin small. Begin inside your own body. Be\ngrateful for your breath — the breath you have not been\nmanaging, the breath that has continued without your\npermission since the day you were born. Be grateful for\nyour skin closing every small cut. Be grateful for your\nheart, which has beat without your supervision for\nevery minute of your life. Be grateful for the small\nmiracles your body is performing right now, without\nthanks, without payment, without applause.\nThen move outward. Be grateful for the people\naround you. The kindness of strangers. The food on the\ntable. The water in the tap. The animals that walk past","you in the street. The trees that breathe out what you\nbreathe in. The insects that pollinate the food you eat.\nThe plants on the rooftop. The auntie who fed you when\nyou had nothing.\nThat is the practice. Not a list. A daily, slow, real\nrecognition of what is already arriving in your life\nwithout your effort.\n* * *\nWhen you practise gratitude this way — slowly,\ndaily, without trying to use it as a tool — something\nhappens that the surface manifestation people do not\ntalk about.\nThe practice itself changes you. It moves you from\nthe third level — the body, the unconscious — to the\nsecond level, the calculating subconscious, to the first\nlevel, the awake conscious mind. Gratitude practised\ndaily becomes the ladder up the three minds.\nAnd once you reach the conscious level — the\nthrone, the pineal, the seat where the light lives — you\nbegin to receive more naturally. Not because the\nuniverse is a vending machine that pays grateful people.\nBecause the awakened man is the one who can hold","what arrives without breaking it. Most people cannot\nhold wealth even when it comes. It corrupts them. It\naddicts them. It makes them anxious. The awakened\nman holds it lightly, uses it well, returns it to flow. So\nlife keeps sending more, because the channel is open.\n* * *\nBut even an awake man drifts. The subconscious\nsometimes breaks contact with the conscious. A bad\nday. A loss. A betrayal. A panic. The light goes off the\nthrone for a moment, and the body falls back into\nreaction. The man who had been awake for a year\nsuddenly speaks angrily, acts foolishly, loses what he\nhad been building.\nThat is the collapse. The collapse is normal. The\ncollapse is part of being human. Even the prophets had\ncollapse moments. Even the Sufis had nights where the\nlight dimmed.\nThe honest response is — this is just what happened.\nI do not fully understand it. Let me return to the breath.\nLet me return to the practice. Let me open the drawer of\nmy own rules again and begin once more.","* * *\nAnd then the daily warning. Always be aware of\nwhat you think and what you speak. Every word is Kun\nfayakun. Every thought is Kun fayakun. Choose them\ncarefully.\nI love to speak. I love to speak.\nThat admission stays. The teacher who pretends to\nbe perfect cannot teach. The teacher who admits he\nloves to talk, and still tries to choose his words\ncarefully, can teach anyone.","LATE SPIRITUAL CORE · TEACHING CHAPTER\n49\nThe Stone on the Road\nA small real-life example. I learned this thing, but I\nrarely practised it. When I say I rarely practised it, I am\nnot saying I never did it. I am saying I did not do it\noften enough. I am not God. I do not claim perfection. I\nam a man who has learned things and has tried, and\nsometimes failed, to live them.\nThe thing I learned was simple. When you walk and\nyou see something that could hurt someone else — a\nstone in the path, a sharp object, something that could\ntrip an old man or cut an animal — pick it up. Move it.\nBe the small kindness the road needs. Not because\nanyone is watching. Not because it earns reward in\nheaven. Because that is what a human being is for.\n* * *\nIn my first week of trying to practise this, I walked\nthrough the streets and saw stones everywhere. The\nsubconscious immediately began calculating. Which","one should I pick? This one? That one? The big one?\nThe small one? There are too many. I cannot pick all of\nthem. What is the point of picking just one?\nThat is the first stage of the failure. The mind, when\nit begins to calculate, has already started talking itself\nout of the act. The first clean impulse — pick that stone\n— gets buried under arithmetic.\nThen, as you get older, a second voice arrives. The\nvoice of shyness. What will people think if they see me\nbending down to pick up a stone? Someone will see me.\nSomeone will think I am strange. Let someone else\nhandle it. That is the second stage. The body that was\nonce free as a child to bend down and move a stone has\nnow been trained by social fear to leave the stone where\nit is.\nThen comes the third stage, and this is the worst one.\nYou begin to tell yourself — there is a system.\nSomeone has been assigned to clean the streets. The\nmunicipality. The workers. The people whose job it is.\nThat is why they were hired. That is why my taxes go\nwhere they go. That is why I am working here, paying\nfor the system to handle these things.","And in that third stage, the whole purpose of being a\nbody in the world is lost.\n* * *\nYou forgot what the body is for.\nYou forgot that you are not only a worker who pays\ninto a system. You are also a body assigned, by God, to\nbe useful to the road you are walking on. The stone in\nfront of you is not the municipality's problem. The\nstone in front of you is your small share of the work of\nbeing alive. The system is a parallel arrangement. It\ndoes not erase your responsibility. It only handles the\nstones you cannot reach. The one in front of you was\nplaced there for you.\nWhen the man stops doing his small share —\nbecause he calculates, because he is shy, because he\ntrusts the system — the system begins to give him\nthings he did not ask for. Things he does not even\nrecognise as gifts or as punishments. Just things. And\nhe becomes a passive receiver, drifting through his\ndays, collapsing slowly toward his own death, having\nnever understood that the body was the tool the soul had\nbeen given precisely so it could do the small acts the","system cannot do.\n* * *\nThis is the simplest way mankind has been\nmisguided. Not by some great conspiracy. Not by some\nhidden ruler. By the small daily forgetting of the stone\non the road. By a thousand tiny refusals to bend down.\nBy the slow erosion of the man who once was a boy\ngenerous enough to remove a thorn from a leg, careful\nenough not to throw a stone behind a cow.\nHe grows up. He becomes the man who walks past\nthe stone. He pays his taxes. He trusts the system. He\ngoes home. He sleeps. He dies.\nAnd he never understood that mankind is not the\ngoats and the cattle, who live properly inside their own\nentropy. A cow does not need to pick up the stone —\nthe cow lives correctly inside its own natural design. A\ngoat lives correctly inside its design. Even a tree lives\ncorrectly inside its design. But mankind was given the\nconscious mind precisely so it could rise above pure\nentropy and act with intention. The stone in the road is\nthe test. The unawake man walks past. The awake man\nbends down.","* * *\nI did not finish this teaching the first time I gave it.\nLet me finish it now.\nI lived inside my subconscious for most of my life.\nThrough Qatar. Through Dubai. Through Malta.\nWalking past stones every single day without seeing\nthem. The body was on autopilot. The subconscious\nwas running the show. The stones were everywhere,\nand I was nowhere.\nThen prison. Then release. Then two years of slow\nrebuilding — reading, sitting still, studying the verses,\nwatching the orange light arrive behind my eyelids.\nTwo years of practice.\nAnd then something quiet happened — so quiet I\nalmost did not notice it.\nI started picking up the stones. Without asking\nmyself first.\nI would be walking on a Maltese street. I would see a\nstone, or a piece of broken glass, or something sharp on\nthe road, and my body would simply bend down and\nmove it. No calculation. No shyness. No internal","debate. The hand would just do the work. The legs\nwould carry the body forward. And only later —\nsometimes when I reached home, sometimes hours after\n— would the subconscious wake up and ask, why did\nyou do that? Nobody saw you. Nobody thanked you. It\nwas not your problem.\nAnd the awake part of me, the new resident of my\nown body, would answer quietly — yes. I know. But it\nwas good. That is enough.\n* * *\nThat is the conversation. That is exactly what is\nhappening inside an awakened man. The subconscious\nis still alive. It still talks. It does not die. It just no\nlonger runs the body. It has been demoted from driver\nto passenger. It still asks its questions from the back\nseat. Why did you do that? Why did you spend that\nmoney? Why did you say yes? Why did you not protect\nyourself?\nBut the conscious mind is now driving. And the\nconscious mind answers gently, every time — yes. I\nheard you. It is fine. We did the right thing. Keep\nwatching.","That is the whole goal of the practice. Not to silence\nthe subconscious. To outvote it. Not to kill the old\nprogramme. To make the new programme stronger.\n* * *\nPeople think awakening happens with the eyes\nclosed. Sitting still. Meditating. Praying. That is\nhalf-true. The closing of the eyes is the training ground.\nThat is where the conscious mind is built.\nBut the proof of the awakening is when you open\nyour eyes.\nBecause the body still has to walk through the world\nwith the eyes open. You cannot live with your eyes\nclosed. So the conscious mind that was built in the\nstillness of closed eyes must then come with you when\nthe eyes open. It must guide the body through the\nopen-eyed world. It must decide which stone to pick up.\nWhich conversation to enter. Which food to eat. Which\ncall to answer. Which silence to keep.\nLet the eyes decide where to walk, how to walk,\nwhat to do. But let the eyes be the eyes of the awakened\nconscious, not the eyes of the sleeping subconscious.\nSame eyes. Different driver.","Interesting, right? Every kind of message soon\nbecomes interesting.","LATE SPIRITUAL CORE · TEACHING CHAPTER\n50\nBefore the Bloom\nI was driving. Not in a crazy way — driving the\nnormal way, on a normal Maltese highway. And in that\nordinary movement, my awakened eyes began to notice\nthings that the sleeping driver inside me would never\nhave seen.\nI saw a small piece of debris on the road. Something\nthat should not have been there. And the feeling that\narrived in me was not the old sleeping feeling. It was\nsomething new. Sorrow. A small, genuine sorrow that\nsomeone had not picked it up. Please — whoever you\nare — please collect those things. The awakened mind\nspeaking, gently, to whoever the system was, on behalf\nof the road.\nThis is the same teaching as the stone — but now\nfrom a moving car instead of a walking body. The\nbending-down is no longer possible because you are at\nspeed. But the seeing is still possible. And the seeing\nitself is the practice. You cannot pick up every stone.","But you can stop being the man who walks past without\neven noticing. Noticing is the first half of awakening.\nThe body bends down when it can. The eyes notice\nalways.\n* * *\nThen — still driving — my eyes found something\nelse.\nApril is coming. Most people, when they think of\nApril in Malta, think of the flowers. The blooms. The\ncolour. The petals. The Instagram moment of a yellow\nfield or a red poppy. That is what the sleeping eye sees\nin April. It waits for the bloom.\nBut the awakened eye sees what comes before the\nbloom. Before the flowers arrive, the green deepens.\nThe brightness inside the leaf becomes more vivid. The\nwhole landscape, in the weeks before the colour arrives,\nis already preparing itself — quietly, in a green so\nbright that only the awakened eye notices it. The land is\ngetting ready. The flowers have not arrived yet. But the\nland is rehearsing them.\nMost people miss this. They wait for the bloom.\nThey photograph the bloom. They celebrate the bloom.","And then, when summer comes, they watch the green\ndry, the colour fade, the land go brown — and they look\naway. They forget the same plot of land they once\nadmired. They wait for next April, and they forget the\nin-between.\n* * *\nBut I — the watcher — do not forget the same land\nin its dry season. I keep looking at it.\nBecause I understand that the\ngreen-before-the-bloom and the dry-after-the-bloom are\nnot two different lands. They are the same land in two\ndifferent breaths. The land that bloomed will bloom\nagain. The land that has gone dry is not dying. It is\nresting. It is waiting for the next season. The cycle is\nunbroken. Only the watcher who keeps watching across\nall four seasons sees the whole life of that piece of\nground.\nAnd this is exactly how an awakened human sees\nother human beings.\nThe sleeping man only loves people in their bloom.\nHe praises the friend who is succeeding. He spends time\nwith the woman who is at her most beautiful. He","admires the brother who is at his strongest. And when\nthe friend falters, when the woman ages, when the\nbrother weakens — he looks away. He waits for them to\nbloom again. He forgets them in their dry season.\nThe awakened man does not look away. He keeps\nwatching. He understands that the dry season is part of\nthe life. He stays. He waits. He honours the same plot of\nland, the same friend, the same family member, the\nsame self — across all four seasons. That is real love.\nNot love of the bloom. Love of the whole cycle.\nI still see, and I wait for the next season, like a\nhuman.","LATE SPIRITUAL CORE · TEACHING CHAPTER\n51\nThe Teacher in the Small Body\nI have learned this many more times since the cell,\nbut one moment in particular taught it to me at a depth I\nhad not reached before.\nI found a small animal on the street. Almost dead. I\ntook it in. I will not name what kind of animal — that\ndetail is not the point. The animal needed feeding. The\nanimal needed cleaning. The animal needed care.\nAnd here is the honest part — the part most people\nwould hide. I did not love it perfectly. I had mixed\nfeelings. Part of me wanted to keep it. Part of me was\ntired of feeding it. Part of me wanted to care for it, and\npart of me did not want to do the daily work of cleaning\nafter it. I hated some of the parts of caring. I loved the\nanimal. Both were true at the same time.\nThat is the most honest description of love I can\ngive. Love is not the absence of hating the work. Love\nis choosing to do the work anyway, even on the days\nthe work is heavy. The unawake man waits to feel pure","love before he acts. The awake man acts, even when the\nfeeling is mixed, and lets the love grow underneath the\naction.\n* * *\nAnd then it died. The small animal died.\nAnd in that moment, something happened that I did\nnot expect.\nFor three days, I could not stop thinking about it. Not\nin the small way I think about most things. In the deep\nway. The animal that I had complained about feeding\n— that I had hated cleaning after — was suddenly the\nonly thing in my mind. I missed it. Even without saying\na word about it. Even unknown to anyone around me. I\nmissed it for three days.\nThis is the Prophet's three-day teaching showing up\nagain. The hadith about not speaking to your brother for\nmore than three days when there is anger between you.\nThe same three days appear in the story of Jonah —\nthree days inside the fish before the change. The same\nthree days appear in the story of Jesus — three days in\nthe heart of the earth. Three days is the natural cycle of\ngrief and reprogramming inside the human body. Three","days is how long the mind needs to digest a loss before\nit can begin to move forward differently.\nI spent those three days in quiet, internal\nconversation with the animal. Please — wherever you\nare — accept my love. Accept the love that I did not\nalways show clearly when you were alive. And forgive\nme for the small hate I carried on the days I did not\nwant to clean after you. Take my love. Leave behind\nonly the love.\n* * *\nAnd here is the deepest part. When the animal left, it\ndid not take anything with it. It left something behind.\nIt left me love and kindness — taught patiently, over\nthe weeks I had it, without my even realising I was\nbeing taught.\nThe small animal was a teacher in disguise. It came\nto me to teach me how to care for something that could\nnot give back in words. To teach me how to feed\nsomething when I did not feel like feeding. To teach me\nhow to clean after something when I did not want to\nclean. To teach me how to love even when the love was\nmixed with reluctance. And to teach me how to grieve,","quietly, for three days, when the small life finished.\nSome time later, I was walking on the street, and I\nsaw a pigeon — hurt, struggling, about to be returned to\nentropy. And my hands moved before my mind asked. I\nbent down. I picked the pigeon up. I helped it back to\nsafety.\nI thought, in that moment, that I had picked up the\npigeon because I am an awake man. That is partly true.\nBut the deeper truth is — I picked up the pigeon\nbecause the small animal that had died in my home,\nweeks earlier, had already taught me how. The pigeon\nwas the test the world set for me, after the animal had\nfinished training me. The animal had quietly built the\nmuscle of small care inside me. The pigeon found me\nready, because the animal had prepared the soil first.\n* * *\nThat is how the world actually trains us. Not through\ngrand teachers in robes. Through small lives that arrive\nin our homes, ask for food, ask for cleaning, ask for\npatience — and then leave, having quietly given us the\nonly education that matters. The animal was my\nteacher. The pigeon was my exam. I passed because the","teacher had prepared me.\nEvery small life that enters your home is teaching\nyou something. The plant on the rooftop. The cat at the\ndoor. The bird at the window. The fish in a small bowl.\nEven the children of your friends and family who you\nbabysit for a few hours. All of them are small teachers\nwearing the costume of dependents. They arrive\nlooking like they need care from you. They leave\nhaving quietly given more care to you than you ever\ngave to them.\nThe unawake man sees the small life as an\ninconvenience. More feeding. More cleaning. More\ndisruption. The awake man begins to suspect that the\nsmall life is the curriculum, and the inconvenience is\nthe syllabus.\nWhen the small life ends — when the animal dies,\nwhen the plant withers, when the child grows up and\nmoves away — the awake man does not say good,\nfinally, less work for me. The awake man sits, for three\ndays, in the quiet conversation that grief produces. And\nin those three days, he discovers what the small life had\nbeen quietly teaching him all along.","LATE SPIRITUAL CORE · TEACHING CHAPTER\n52\nHow to Grow a Child\nIf you take only one practical thing from this book,\ntake this. Re-read the Qur'an first. Most people last\ntouched it in around seventh grade — recited it without\nunderstanding, then put it down. The first instruction to\nany reader of this book — go back. Re-read it. This\ntime with the meaning. You missed it the first time.\nThat is fine. The book has been waiting.\nAnd then, when the time comes for you to raise a\nchild, here is what I have learned. I do not give this as\ncommandment. I give this as a manual one man worked\nout for himself, after years of paying for the absence of\nany manual at all.\n* * *\nThe decision begins before the child arrives.\nWhen mother and father agree to bring a child into\nthe world, that agreement is the first foundation. From\nthat moment — through the months of carrying, the","early years of caring — they must decide together that\nthey will hold kindness, love, patience, mercy. They\nmust decide that anger will not rule the house. Because\nwhat most parents do is the opposite — they bring the\nchild into entropy without preparing the soil. The child\ngrows into the mess that was already there.\nEntropy means the natural disorder all systems drift\ninto when no one is paying attention. The world drifts.\nHouses drift. Marriages drift. Bodies drift. A child\nentering a house full of unconscious drift becomes a\nsmall unconscious drifter, by absorption, before any\nwords have been spoken.\n* * *\nYears one to five — let the child grow physically,\nbut with the soft hand of a guide.\nThe early years are for awakening the body, not for\ncramming the mind. You will guide your child the way\nyou guided yourself — through your own watcher, even\nwhen no formal teacher was present. And where there\nare good teachers — quiet ones, real ones, not the\nfamous ones — you will bring those teachers in. Like\nthe principal who taught me, years ago, that strictness","and warmth can live in the same man. Those quiet city\nteachers, the ones who do not seek popularity, are the\nreal teachers. You will find them and place them near\nyour child.\n* * *\nThe child's first formal language for learning will be\nEnglish, not the mother tongue.\nNot because the mother tongue is lesser. Tamil, for\nme, is the language of my blood, the language of my\nlove, the language of my dreams. But the mother tongue\nis autopilot. The subconscious absorbs the mother\ntongue without effort — it goes in like a song, it lives in\nthe body without ever being examined. Songs become\nmanuals in the subconscious mind without a single\nconscious reading. That is dangerous for sacred text.\nIf you read the Qur'an in your mother tongue too\nearly, it enters the same autopilot channel — practised,\nnot understood.\nTherefore the Qur'an, the sacred manual of being\nhuman, must be read in a language the child has to work\nto understand. A learned language — English, in this\ncase. Because the work of understanding the second","language forces the conscious mind to engage. The\nverses cannot slip past into autopilot. They must be\nparsed, weighed, considered.\nThat is why even classical Islam teaches learning\nArabic — not because Arabic is magical, but because a\nlearned language carries the awareness that the mother\nlanguage cannot carry.\nI will not force my child to learn Arabic grammar. I\nwill teach them enough English to read the Qur'an with\nfull understanding. That is sufficient. The deeper\nArabic, if they choose it later, will come later.\n* * *\nFrom around fifth grade — basic English,\nmathematics, and basic sciences. That is all.\nNo mountain of subjects. No exam pressure. The\nmind needs space.\n* * *\nBefore the child even opens the Qur'an, the child\nmust learn stillness.\nStillness before scripture. I will teach my child to\nclose the eyes, to breathe slowly, to forget the body for","short stretches at first, then longer. I will teach the child\nwhat I did not learn until thirty-four years of age.\nI use the word yoga, not prayer, on purpose. The\nword prayer has been so heavily shaped by the\nsubconscious of every tradition that it no longer means\nwhat it once meant. For some Muslims, prayer is five\ntimes a day. For some, it is four. For some Sufis, eight\nor more. The moment you say prayer, the listener's\nsubconscious jumps straight to which kind, which\nposture, which count — and the deeper meaning of\nwhat prayer actually is gets lost under the argument\nabout its form.\nSo I deliberately use yoga. Not because yoga is\nHindu, and not because I am moving away from Islam.\nI use the word yoga because it points more clearly at\nwhat prayer was actually meant to be — the slow,\nconscious return of the body into stillness, the breath\nbecoming the bridge to the throne, the mind opening to\nreceive what the body alone cannot receive.\nLook at the Prophet himself, peace be upon him. In\nhis deepest prayer, he went so completely into stillness\nthat the people around him sometimes thought he had","died. He was not moving. He was sitting in one frame\n— the deepest yoga any tradition has ever described. He\nwas so still that, at one moment, he moved only his\nindex finger — just enough to show that he was still\nalive. That single small motion was the proof of life.\nAnd what does the modern subconscious of religion\ndo with that example? It argues. Did he break the fourth\nraka'ah or the fifth? Was that the correct moment to\nmove? Is moving the finger sunnah or innovation?\nPeople who never sat still for ten minutes in their lives\nare arguing about whether a man who sat still for hours\nmoved his finger at the right moment. They lost the\nlesson. The lesson was not the finger. The lesson was\nthe hours of stillness before the finger.\n* * *\nSo with my child, I will not say go pray your five\ntimes. I will say — sit. Close your eyes. Breathe slowly.\nForget your body. Find the stillness.\nOnly after the child has experienced what the\nProphet was actually doing — only after the child\nknows what stillness feels like in the body — will I\nintroduce the Qur'an, and explain that this stillness is","what salah was always pointing at.\nThen the child reads. With headphones. With the\nEnglish translation. While doing yoga, while sitting,\nwhile breathing slowly. The Qur'an is taught not as\nritual but as a manual for living. I will not teach the\nchild surface details — not how the Prophet went to the\ntoilet fourteen hundred years ago, not the small rules\nthat the village wraps around the religion. I will teach\nthe child the verses that teach how to live. Kindness.\nMercy. Forgiveness. Patience. The throne. The light.\nThe fasting. The breath. The witness. The recipe for\ncompletion.\n* * *\nAround tenth to twelfth grade — let the child go. Let\nthe child suffer a little. Let the child find fear. Let the\nchild overcome difficulty without parental support.\nThis is how the human body and mind actually grow.\nA child raised inside total protection becomes the man\nwho walks into an airport at thirty-four years of age\ncompletely unprepared. I know this because that man\nwas me. My mind knew nothing. I had not been\nprepared. Do not let your child be unprepared.","The mind opens its gates when it must. But the gates\nopen more easily, and earlier, in the child who has been\nallowed to face small fears young.\n* * *\nReligion is not the priority. One God is.\nI will not raise my child inside the loud noise of\nwhich sect, which madhab, which posture, which\nfinger. I will raise my child to know one God — and to\nknow that the path to God is stillness. If my child\nknows how to be still for five hours a day, that is more\nthan enough. The prayer is the door. The stillness is the\nroom. Most people stand at the door for fourteen\nhundred years and never enter the room.\nFor fourteen hundred years, people have died\npractising without understanding. They handed down\nthe ritual but lost the meaning. The book asks you to do\nthe opposite. When you understand, you have the power\nto live the life beautifully. That is the inheritance you\nwill give your child. Not the ritual. The understanding.\nI do not know how to explain this any more clearly\nthan that.","SECOND-TO-LAST CHAPTER · THE PRACTICE\n53\nThe Practice\nBefore you read this chapter, ask yourself\nsomething. Be honest with yourself, because no one\nelse can answer this for you.\nWhat part of your mind brought you to this page?\nDid you arrive here page by page, having read\neverything that came before? The childhood, the\nbloodline, the cell, the throne, the eleven rules, the\nawakening songs, Kun fayakun, the fire that cooks you\nnow, the light that lives in the darkness, the man and the\nwoman, the stone on the road, the bloom that comes\nbefore the colour? Did you walk slowly through all of\nit, letting the earlier chapters soften you, prepare you,\nreshape you?\nOr did your ego pull you to the back of the book to\ntake the technique without doing the work?\nBe honest. Only one of these two readers will benefit\nfrom what comes next. The other will read it and walk","away with nothing, because the words on this page only\nmean what they mean to the mind that has earned them.\nIf you arrived here as the second reader — the one\nwho skipped — close the book. Go back to the\nbeginning. Or put it down for now and come back later.\nThere is no rush. The practice will wait for you. It has\nbeen waiting for fourteen hundred years. It can wait a\nfew more weeks while you walk the path properly.\nIf you arrived here as the first reader — the one who\nread everything — welcome. The practice is yours.\n* * *\nHere is what I do, every day, and what I am inviting\nyou to begin doing. I am not God. I am not a guru. I am\na man who walked into a prison asleep and walked out a\nlittle awake, and who has spent the years since trying\nnot to fall back asleep. This is the discipline I use to\nstay awake.\nStart with fasting. Begin with what your religion\nalready gives you, if you have one. For me, Ramadan is\nthe obligatory minimum — the yearly corrective\nmaintenance of the body. You must do that, even if\nyour heart has not yet accepted the deeper purpose.","Even if you fast only because you were told to fast,\neven if you do not understand why. Just do it. The body\nstill receives the repair. The mind catches up later.\nThen, when the conscious mind accepts it, add a\nweekly fast. The Prophet himself fasted on Mondays\nand Thursdays — not because anyone told him to, but\nbecause his awakened body knew it was useful. You\nmay choose a different day. The day does not matter.\nThe practice matters.\nA longer fast — around seventy-two hours of water\n— triggers a deep cellular repair in the body. Modern\nscience is beginning to measure what the tradition has\nalways known. You do not need a temple. You do not\nneed a guru. You do not need a clinic. You need only\nwater, time, and the willingness to sit with your own\nhunger long enough that the body's own repair systems\nwake up. The body has been waiting your whole life for\nyou to give it this gift. Most people never do.\n* * *\nBreathe slowly every day. Not the shallow breathing\nof the chest, where most modern people live. Real deep\nbreathing, where the belly expands, the diaphragm","drops, the lungs fill from the bottom up. Do this for\nsome minutes every day. Do this during the fast.\nStretch the tissue. The skin is elastic. The fascia, the\nconnective tissue under the skin, is elastic. The muscles\nare elastic. But elasticity only stays alive if you use it. If\nyou sit at a desk all day, if you never move, if you never\nstretch, if you never flex — the elastic dries. The tissue\ntightens. And then one ordinary morning, you bend\ndown to pick up something small, and your back\ncatches. The pain seems to come from nowhere. It does\nnot come from nowhere. It comes from years of not\nstretching the tissue that was designed to be stretched.\nThis is why athletes live in their bodies differently.\nWhy yoga practitioners do too. Why the people who do\nreal meditation — not just sitting, but the kind that\nincludes breath and movement — also fall into this\ngroup. They stretch the tissue. They flex the system.\nThey keep the elastic alive. And so their bodies stay\nsupple deeper into life. They do not collapse the way\nunstretched bodies collapse.\nYou do not have to be an athlete. You do not have to\ndo extreme practices. You only have to stretch every","day. Reach the arms up. Bend forward gently. Twist the\nspine. Open the chest. Breathe into the stretch. Hold for\na few breaths. Move on. Ten minutes a day. Less, if that\nis all you have. Just something. The tissue responds to\neven a small daily greeting.\n* * *\nWhy does any of this matter? Why does the awake\nman bother with fasting and breath and tissue?\nBecause a strong, repaired, flexible body extends the\nlife of the mind that lives inside it.\nA body that is poorly maintained dies sooner. The\nmind inside it gets fewer years to mature, to awaken, to\nread, to write, to give back what it has learned. The\nbody collapses, and the mind inside it goes with the\nbody, having never finished its real work.\nA body that is well maintained, fasted, stretched,\nbreathed-into, fed simply — that body can live longer in\nusefulness. And the mind inside that body has more\nyears to perceive more. More years to sit in stillness.\nMore years to read the verses with deeper\nunderstanding. More years to bless the people who hurt\nyou. More years to teach the next generation. More","years to write the book the bloodline never had.\nThat is the whole reason for the practice. Not vanity.\nNot athletic competition. Not fear of death. More time\nfor the mind to perceive. That is it. The whole\nengineering of the practice is in service of one thing —\ngiving the conscious mind a longer runway in which to\nwake up further, again, and again, every year, into\ndeeper and deeper layers of the same simple truths.\nThe body is the vehicle. Maintain the vehicle. The\nmind is the driver. Give the driver more road.\n* * *\nAnd what do I actually do, in a typical day, on this\npractice?\nI wake before the world wakes. I drink water first. I\nmake my coffee slowly. I do not touch my phone for the\nfirst thirty minutes. I sit by the window. I read forty\nminutes of real books. I move my body — yoga,\nstretching, sometimes the gym. I eat one meal a day,\nsince Ramadan in March 2026, with rare exceptions\nwhen my body or my work needs a second. I work\nhonestly. I make outgoing calls to the people I love. I do\nnot wait for them to remember me. I go to the rooftop in","the evening to water plants. I sit by the water some\nafternoons with my eyes closed, and the orange light\narrives, or it does not. The point is the sitting. I pray. I\nread again at night. I sleep early. I begin again the next\nday.\nThat is the whole practice, in one day. Nothing\ndramatic. No incense. No mountain retreat. The same\neleven rules I wrote in a cell with a borrowed pen, lived\nout in an apartment in Malta, one quiet day at a time.\nIf you can build a good day from these elements, you\ncan build a good life from these elements. If you cannot\nmake a good day out of these, you will not make a good\nday out of any others. The cell taught me this. The\nrooftop continues to teach me this. The book is my\nattempt to give you the same lesson without making\nyou go to a cell to learn it.","The practice is one quiet day repeated.","ACT THREE · THE ROOFTOP\nTHE TEACHINGS","54\nThe Witnesses\nI want to tell you something about how I came to\nknow what I know, because it matters more than the\nknowing itself. I did not learn it. I received it. I sat in\nstillness, I closed my eyes, and the understanding\narrived — not as words on a page I had studied, but as\nsomething remembered, something that was already\nmine. And then, afterward, I began to notice a strange\nand beautiful thing. Everywhere I looked — in science,\nin the old Sufi path, in the words of men who had spent\ntheir whole lives searching — I found people saying the\nsame thing I had received. Not because I copied them.\nBecause the truth is not owned by anyone. It is one\nthing, wearing many coats, speaking in many tongues.\n* * *\nA cell biologist named Bruce Lipton will tell you\nthat in the first seven years of your life, before you can\nchoose, your mind runs at a low frequency, like a kind\nof hypnosis, and in those years you absorb everything\n— the way your mother moves, the way your father","speaks, the fears and the habits of your whole family\nand your culture. He says that most of what runs your\nlife was written into you before you were old enough to\nagree to it. I did not need his laboratory to know this. I\ntold you already: your mother is the walker of your\nthoughts, and more than half of what moves through\nyour mind came from her. He measured it with\ninstruments. I received it in the memory of a cup of\nmorning tea. The same truth, arriving by two different\nroads.\n* * *\nA physicist named Brian Greene asks you to imagine\nan orange. Magnify it, he says, and you find molecules.\nMagnify those, and you find atoms. Go deeper —\nelectrons, a nucleus, protons — and inside them, tiny\nquarks. And then, at the very bottom, where the old\nscience believed nothing was left, he says you find a\nsingle filament of energy, like the string of a violin, and\nit is vibrating. Everything that exists, at its deepest\nfloor, is a string trembling into a note.\nNow, this is a theory, not yet proven, so I hold it\nwith an open hand. But hear what it is saying, because I","have already said it to you in my own language. In the\nbeginning was the sound. Kun — be. The universe is\nnot built of stone; it is built of a hum. The physicist\ncalls it a string. I call it the utterance. We are pointing at\nthe same floor, from two different stairwells.\n* * *\nThe old Sufis, long before any microscope, said the\nQur'an was never meant to be read the way you read a\nlaw book — counted, finished, ticked off like a chore.\nThey said it was meant to be felt: a recitation, a rhythm,\na vibration that moves through the body and opens\nsomething the eyes alone cannot reach. Some go further\nand try to turn it into numbers and hidden codes, and\nthat I leave to one side, because counting is the shallow\nwater, and I have warned you about the numbers more\nthan once. But the heart of it is true, and I keep it: the\nBook is a sound before it is a sentence. You do not only\nread it. You let it move through you, the way the\nmorning call moves through a sleeping street.\n* * *\nThere is a teacher who speaks to the people chasing\nthe modern religion of self-improvement — the","five-o'clock alarms, the cold showers, the endless hustle\nand discipline — and he tells them, gently, that the road\nwas never more doing. It is gratitude, and it is istighfar,\nthe asking of forgiveness, the quiet cleaning of the\nheart. Be thankful, he says, and you are increased;\nwhatever lack you carry, meet it first with thanks. I\nhave told you the very same: love first, then gratitude,\nand the universe answers. He found it in the mosque. I\nfound it in a prison cell. The same medicine, held in two\ndifferent hands.\n* * *\nEven the men studying the eyes arrive at my\ndoorstep. The neuroscientists say the modern world is\nquietly breaking your vision — that you blink too little\nwhen you stare at a screen, that the flat straight lines of\nhouses and streets and glass starve the brain, and that\nyou must go back to nature, to the fractals, the\nbranching of the trees, the moving of the water, because\nthe brain was built to rest inside those patterns. I have\nalready walked you to that water in Valletta. I have\nalready told you to watch the tree and the bird before\nyou ever try to watch yourself. They proved with","instruments what the stillness had been teaching me for\nfree.\n* * *\nAnd here is the deepest agreement of all, the one that\nholds my whole book together. There were thinkers —\nWilliam James, Aldous Huxley — who dared to say\nthat the brain may not be the maker of consciousness at\nall, but only its receiver. A radio does not compose the\nmusic; it catches a signal that is already in the air.\nPerhaps, they said, the brain is a radio, and you are not\nthe machine but the music coming through it. I cannot\nprove this, and neither could they.\nBut hold that beside the man who is building the\nmost advanced brain-machine on earth. He says, plainly\nand honestly, that he does not know what consciousness\nis — that nobody does. He can move information in and\nout of the brain a million times faster, bits becoming\nmegabits becoming gigabits, and still he cannot tell you\nwho is receiving it. He is building a faster road to a\nhouse without knowing who lives inside. That is the\nquestion the machine cannot answer. And it is the\nquestion my whole life has been answering: you are not","the brain. You are the one the brain is listening for.\n* * *\nThere is even a film — Avatar — that tells it as a\nstory: a whole living world joined through one great\ntree that holds the memory of everyone who came\nbefore, reachable by anyone quiet enough to listen. It is\nonly a story. But the best stories are doors. And\nsometimes I feel that door myself — as if the thoughts\nof those who came before me do not simply vanish, as\nif something of them lingers in the breath and the air,\nand as if in deep stillness I am receiving from much\nfurther back than my own short life. I cannot prove that,\nand I will not pretend to. I offer it as a man's wonder,\nnot as a fact.\nAnd I keep the one rule I will not break, because it is\nthe rule that keeps a seeker honest. This stillness carries\nyou to the door of the Unseen — but the door is a door,\nnot a deity. You approach it. You receive through it.\nYou do not stand up and call yourself the Creator. Stay\nat the door. The door is more than enough for one life.\n* * *","So this is why I have gathered these witnesses for\nyou — the biologist, the physicist, the Sufi, the\nneuroscientist, the man with the machine. Not because I\nlearned my truth from them. Because I received it first,\nin the dark, behind my own closed eyes, and only\nafterward found them, each travelling his own road,\narriving at the same quiet place. If you are a reader who\ntrusts a laboratory, take the physicist's string and the\nbiologist's seven years. If you trust the old path, take the\nSufi's sound and the asking of forgiveness. If you trust\nnothing at all yet, then take the machine's own\nconfession that it does not know who you are. Any one\nof them is a door. You only have to walk through one.\nBecause the truth was never mine to hand you. It was\nonly ever mine to point at — and to say to you, quietly,\nthe way someone once needed to say it to me: you\nalready carry it too. You only have to go still enough to\nreceive it.","ACT THREE · THE ROOFTOP\nTHE TEACHINGS","55\nThe Two Books\nThere is an old and quiet idea that I want to give you\nthis morning, because it came to me this morning, in the\nstillness — the way the truest things arrive, not when\nyou chase them, but when you sit and let them come. I\nhad been trying to push the thoughts away to reach a\ndeeper quiet, and then I understood that the watching of\nthe thoughts was itself the quiet, and into that watching\nthis came.\nThe old scholars used to say that God wrote two\nbooks. The first is the scripture, the words on the page.\nThe second is the world itself — the sky, the sea, the\ntree, the body, the slow turning of the stars. Two books,\nthey said, written by the same hand. And your own\nBook tells you this, if you listen closely: the things of\nnature are called ayat — signs. The very word used for\na verse of the Qur'an is the word used for a mountain, a\nriver, the birth of a child. The verse and the world are\nwritten in the same ink.","So let me say a thing that frightens some people,\nthough it should not. Science is not the enemy of faith.\nThe scientist and the seeker are not at war. They are two\nreaders, bent over the same single book — the book of\nnature — reading it in two different scripts.\n* * *\nLet me show you how they are not enemies, with a\nsimple picture. Imagine a house. The scientist walks all\nthe way around the outside of it. He measures the walls,\nhe counts the windows, he weighs the bricks, he maps\nevery crack with his instruments — and everything he\nwrites down is true. He is an honest man doing honest\nwork. But there is one thing he can never reach from\nout there: he cannot know what it is like to live inside\nthe house. He cannot feel the warmth of the room, or\nhear the silence that fills it at night, or know the one\nwho sits quietly by the window.\nThe seeker does the opposite. He sits inside. He\ncannot tell you the weight of the bricks or the exact\nwidth of the walls — but he knows, from within, what it\nis to be the one the whole house was built around. He\nknows the living, not the measuring. Now, which of the","two is right? Both. Neither is the enemy of the other.\nThey are two windows onto one house, and no single\nwindow ever shows the whole of it. The scientist sees\nfrom without; the seeker knows from within. Lay the\ntwo together, and only then do you begin — only begin\n— to see the whole.\n* * *\nThis is why I reach for the physicist, the biologist,\nand the machine in these pages. Not to quarrel with\nthem. To stand beside them at the window. When the\nphysicist says that everything, at its deepest floor, is a\nvibrating string, and I say the universe is the utterance,\nKun — we are reading the same line of the same book,\nhe in the script of numbers and I in the script of the\nsoul. When the man building the brain-machine admits,\nhonestly, that he cannot find the one who receives the\nsignal, he has read the book all the way to the edge of\nhis own script — and it is exactly at that edge that my\nscript begins. We are not fighting over the book. We are\nfinishing each other's sentence.\n* * *","And I think now of my great-grandfather, the one\nthey called the fire saint. He had the understanding of\nhis time and the language of his time, and so his sign\nwas fire. That was the script his century could read.\nMine is different. I hold the gathered knowledge of my\nown age — the science, the words, the witnesses — and\nso my sign is not fire but the word: this book, this\nbridge I am trying to lay between the man with the\ninstrument and the man with the prayer. The truth has\nnot changed from his time to mine. Only the language\nhas. Each of us is handed the one flame, and each must\npass it on in the tongue that the next people will be able\nto hear.\n* * *\nSo let me lift a weight off you — the same weight I\nhad to set down myself this morning. You may feel,\nonce you see what I see, that now you must prove it:\nthat you must defeat the scientists, or make the whole\nworld believe, or single-handedly answer the one\nquestion no one has yet answered. Put that down. It was\nnever yours to carry. You do not have to win an\nargument against science, because science was never","your opponent. You do not have to prove the\nunprovable. Your task is smaller and far truer: to live\ninside the house, and to tell, honestly, what it is like in\nthere — to add the witness from within to all the\nmeasuring from without. That is enough. The seeking\nwas always the gift. The proving was never asked of\nyou.\nTwo books. One hand. Read them both, in whatever\nscript you trust — and know that the one at the other\nwindow, reading in a language you do not speak, is not\nyour enemy. He is your brother, leaning toward the\nsame light.","ACT THREE · THE ROOFTOP\nTHE TEACHINGS","56\nThe Speed of Stillness\nOne morning I sat in the driver's seat at the edge of\nthe water, before the day could take me, and I gave\nmyself two or three minutes that belonged to no one —\nnot to the company, not to the clock, only to me and the\nsea in front of me. I watched the boats out on the water\nand the waves close to the shore, and I let the noise\nslow down inside me until I could almost hear the boats\nmoving without any sound at all, the way you sense a\nthing rather than hear it. And in that slowness, the water\ntaught me something I want to give to you.\nLook at the boat far out on the horizon. It barely\nseems to move. Now look at the wave breaking near\nyour feet — it rushes, it hurries, it is gone in a second.\nThe far thing crawls; the near thing races. And here is\nthe strange truth underneath it: the far boat may in fact\nbe moving faster than the near wave, and still your eye\nsees it as almost still. Distance turns speed into stillness.\nYou have seen this all your life without naming it. A\njet at the top of the sky looks like it is hardly moving,","though it is flying faster than any sound — while a bird\nat arm's length flashes past. The farther a thing is from\nyou, the slower it appears, even when it is the quicker of\nthe two. This is what your eye knows. And it is the\nsimple, everyday cousin of what Einstein proved in the\nhard language of mathematics: there is no single speed\nthat is true for everyone, no single moment that is now\nfor all. What is fast or slow, near or far, depends\nentirely on where the watcher is standing. The water\ntaught me in the language of boats what he taught the\nworld in the language of numbers.\n* * *\nThen the water turned the lesson around in my\nhands. If distance makes a thing slow to the eye, then\nhow does a man reach the far — the edge of the sky, the\nthing outside the world, the Unseen? Watch how people\ntry. They want to cover ground, so they take a bicycle to\ngo faster, then a car, then a plane. Always more speed.\nAlways more rushing. But the body is a slow and local\nthing. It can only truly touch what is near it — what is\nwithin a few kilometres of where it stands, not what is\nfive thousand kilometres away. The body cannot rush","its way to the far. It was never built for that.\nSo the fastest way to the far is not speed at all. It is\nstillness. Because thought arrives before the body has\neven risen from its seat. Sit perfectly still, close your\neyes, and you can be standing in Dubai, or among the\nstars, in less than a single breath. The body crawls; the\ninner eye is already there. God did not hand you a faster\nbicycle to reach Him. He placed the inner eye inside\nyour own head — the swiftest vehicle there is — and it\nonly works when the body goes still. The one who\nrushes takes the car to reach what is near. The one who\nis still closes his eyes to reach what is far.\n* * *\nAnd see how plainly this lands on the ground, in the\nmost ordinary decision. I used to tell my brother that a\nman cannot simply drive off to some place at random\nand expect to figure his whole life out once he arrives.\nIt does not work that way. You move toward the picture\nthat is already inside you — the one built from your\nmemory, your hardship, everything you have learned\nand stored. The picture comes first; the journey only\nfollows it. I did not conjure Dubai out of the air and find","myself magically there. I held the picture of it, day after\nday, and then I walked toward it with the slow body —\nhere, then here, then here — using only what was\nwithin my reach at each step. The picture was the\ncompass. The walking was local, and patient, and real.\nThat is why a man with no picture, who just goes\nsomewhere to 'figure it out,' finds nothing: there is no\ndestination drawn inside him yet, so there is nothing to\nwalk toward.\n* * *\nSo do not let anyone, including the voice inside your\nown head, tell you that slowness is laziness. They are\nopposites. The lazy man is asleep — he is absent, he is\ngone, nothing is happening in him. But the slow man is\nthe most awake man alive: steady, deliberate, present,\nholding. Think of the still water in front of me that\nmorning. It was not doing nothing. It was holding the\nentire sky in its surface, perfectly, without spilling a\ndrop. Think of a held breath — it is not weakness; it is\nthe gathered pause where all the power waits before it\nmoves.","Slowness. Steadiness. Stillness. That is not a man\nfalling behind the world. That is a man who has\nmatched his pace to the universe itself. When you hold\nyour breath in the deep quiet, you are doing exactly\nwhat the whole universe does when it holds everything\nin its place — precisely, patiently, and without hurry.\nThe rushing was never the strength. The stillness was\nthe strength all along.","CLOSING CHAPTER · BEFORE THE MAN ON THE\nROCK\n57\nWhy I Have Told You All of This\nWhy have I told you all of this?\nBecause today, sitting at the desk where I am writing\nthis, I am beginning to feel something I had not felt\nbefore. A small reaching toward completeness. A small\ntouch of the nothingness that is not empty. A small\narrival, in pieces, at what every prophet has been\npointing at for thousands of years.\nAnd the strange thing — the thing I want you to\nunderstand — is that this arrival is not coming through\nsome giant act. It is not coming through a forty-day\nretreat or a pilgrimage to a famous mountain or a long\nstretch of prayer in a famous mosque. It is coming\nthrough small things.\n* * *\nA few days ago, walking on the street, I saw a\npigeon. Hurt, struggling, about to be returned to entropy","— about to disappear back into the wheel of birth and\ndecay. I bent down. I picked it up. I helped it back into\nsafety. Nobody saw. Nobody clapped. No one is going\nto give me an award for picking up a pigeon. There was\nno Instagram photograph. There was no like. There was\nno count.\nBut the gratitude I received from that small act was\nlarger than any praise the public world has ever given\nme. Larger than any salary I have earned. Larger than\nany compliment from a stranger. Larger than any\napproval from a female friend or a parent. The pigeon\ncould not even say thank you. The pigeon flew off. And\ninside me, something was given that I cannot describe\nin any other word than gratitude.\nThat is the gratitude the Prophet was actually\npointing at. Not the gratitude of paying two-and-a-half\npercent of your wealth in zakah, although that is the\nform he gave when his people asked him for something\nmeasurable. They asked — tell us something we can\npractise, something we can count. He gave them the\nfigure. They have been doing it for fourteen hundred\nyears. And many of them have been doing it without\never experiencing the deeper gratitude that the form","was always pointing at.\nThe form was the door. The deeper gratitude — the\nkind you receive from a pigeon on the street — is the\nroom.\n* * *\nThis is universal. Every real tradition pointed at the\nsame thing. The Christian teaching says — let not your\nleft hand know what your right hand is doing. Why?\nBecause the conscious mind, when it gives quietly,\ngives cleanly. The moment the left hand knows — the\nmoment the subconscious notices that a good act is\nbeing performed — the subconscious takes credit for it,\nposts it on social media, tells the friend, counts it in the\npersonal ledger. And the gift is corrupted.\nReal giving, real kindness, real love — happens\nwhen the left hand does not know. When the body acts\nbefore the calculating mind can intervene. When the\nawake conscious moves through the body and does the\nkind thing, and the subconscious only finds out later, at\nhome, asking why did you do that? — and the\nconscious answers gently, because it was good.","* * *\nPeople say — we live in illusion. And they think the\nillusion is some kind of fake hologram, some\nMatrix-like deception, some philosophical game. No.\nThat is not what the illusion is.\nThe illusion is what we have built with our own\nentropy — without love, without kindness, without\nmercy from the source. The illusion is the world the\nunawake man constructs daily by his unawake choices.\nThe car he bought to impress people he does not even\nlike. The salary he chases at the cost of his sleep. The\nclothes he wears to be seen. The relationships he\nmaintains for appearance. The arguments he wins to\nfeel powerful. All of it is real to the body. All of it is\nillusion to the conscious mind. Because none of it\nbrings him closer to the pigeon-moment. None of it\ngives him the gratitude the small bird gave for free.\n* * *\nIf you choose to name God Jesus, then Jesus lives in\nthe heart. If you choose to name God Muhammad, then\nMuhammad lives in the pineal gland.","I am not saying Jesus is God. I have already said this\nclearly. I am not saying Muhammad is God either. If\nyour subconscious flinched when you read that sentence\n— notice it. That flinch is the antivirus from 2003 still\nrunning on your modern mind. It arrived before you\nfinished reading.\nWhat I am saying is this. Jesus and Muhammad are\nteachers. They are messengers. They are doors. Each of\nthem opened a particular channel in the human body so\nthat the divine signal could be received there.\nMuhammad's whole life and teaching — the stillness,\nthe long sittings, the third of the night, the channel of\nrevelation — points to the work that happens at the seat\nof the conscious mind, the throne, what science calls the\npineal gland. Jesus's whole life and teaching — love\nyour enemies, the kingdom of God is within you,\nblessed are the pure in heart — points to the work that\nhappens at the seat of the heart.\nDifferent organs. Different doors. Same house. Same\nGod behind both.\nThe Qur'an itself says it. We make no distinction\nbetween any of the prophets. Our God and your God is","one. These are not my inventions. These are the verses,\ntelling you exactly what I am telling you, in language\nfourteen hundred years older than mine.\nWho is going to fight about this? I am not fighting\nabout this. I want you to understand and live the life —\nbefore you die as a human being.\n* * *\nYou — the reader — do not have to live and die that\nway. You can ask. You can sit still long enough that the\nasking actually happens. You can shape your own mind\nthrough the asking itself, instead of waiting for the\nworld to shape you while you sleep.\nAsk the deep questions before you ask how to make\na deep question. Other people — prophets, saints,\nscientists, mystics across cultures — have already asked\nthem. They have left their answers. The answers are not\nhidden. The answers are sitting in books, in scriptures,\nin laboratories, in the patient eyes of old grandmothers\nand the silent watching of pigeons on a Maltese street.\nYou only have to look. It is not hard. It can be learned\neasily.","Why am I here? Why was I born? Why was I raised?\nWhere am I going when I die — and is death even\nsomething to fear?\nAlmost nobody asks. Almost everybody lives and\ndies without ever asking. They are too busy chasing the\nsmall bike. The Lamborghini. The twenty houses. The\nsalary. The status. The argument about which posture is\nthe correct prayer. They die having never asked the only\nfour questions that matter.\n* * *\nSo ask. Ask the deep questions. Shape your own\nmind through your own asking. Do not let someone else\nshape you before you have even met yourself.\nThat stays. Word for word. That is the whole book's\nfinal instruction.\nMost people are shaped before they meet\nthemselves. Their parents shape them. Their school\nshapes them. Their religion shapes them. Their country\nshapes them. Their employer shapes them. Their partner\nshapes them. By the time they are forty, they have been\nshaped by a thousand hands and have never once asked\ntheir own mind — who am I, underneath all this","shaping?\nYou do not have to live and die that way. You can\nask. You can sit. You can wait for the orange light. You\ncan pick up the stone. You can pick up the pigeon. You\ncan write your own book, or read someone else's, or\nsimply sit by the window of your apartment in the third\nof the night, and let the channel finally open.\nThat is what this whole book has been for. Not to\nimpress you with my story. Not to compete with other\nbooks. Not to sell you a Lamborghini-shaped\nenlightenment. To put the four questions in your hands,\nwhile you are still alive, so you can ask them yourself,\nin your own voice, with your own conscious mind,\nbefore the body finishes its run of entropy.\nThat is the whole reason I wrote.","ACT THREE — THE ROOFTOP\nEPILOGUE","58\nA Witness to My Whole Time on\nEarth\nI have lived through the 2004 tsunami. I have lived\nthrough wars in my own country and wars in other\npeople's countries that I watched from far away. I have\nlived through COVID, and watched a friend through the\nwindow of his isolation room because I could not enter.\nI have lived through migration and visa offices and\ncustoms lines and the small humiliations of being a\nbrown man in white airports. I have lived through\nprison. I have lived through loneliness. I have lived\nthrough love that was beautiful and love that was a\nmistake and love that turned out to be my own waking\nmind in disguise.\nI have been, in my small way, a witness to my whole\ntime on earth so far.\nThat phrase came to me one evening in the cell, and\nI wrote it on a folded piece of paper, and now I am\nwriting it again here at the end of the book. A witness.\nNot a hero. Not a teacher. A witness. Someone who was","there, and who tried to look at what was happening, and\nwho tried to remember what he saw, and who now,\nwhile the body is still alive, is writing some of it down\nbefore the body returns to the soil.\n* * *\nI do not know how many years I have left. Nobody\ndoes. The cell was a good teacher in that respect too. It\nremoved any sense I had that I could plan a long,\nsmooth life and assume it would arrive. A life can be\ninterrupted in a single afternoon, by an accusation, by\nan accident, by an illness, by a war, by a wave, by a\npiece of paper handed to you at an airport. The cell did\nnot invent that truth. The tsunami had already taught me\nthat truth when I was fifteen and the sea came up to the\nmain street and stopped. But the cell made the truth\npersonal in a way the tsunami had not. The tsunami had\nspared me. The cell did not.\nSo I write now with the awareness that I do not know\nhow long the writing will go on. The book you are\nholding is what I have managed to set down in the years\nsince I walked out. There is more. There may be other\nbooks, if the body keeps going. There may not be.","Either way, this one is finished. This one is done. This\none is yours now.\n* * *\nIf you are reading this and you have lived through\nsome version of your own cage — a literal one, or a\nfigurative one, an illness, a divorce, an addiction, a\ngrief, a war, a heartbreak, a financial collapse, anything\nthat has taken the legs out from under your old life — I\nwant you to know that the cage is not the end. The cage\nmay be the beginning of the only real life you were ever\ngoing to live. The cage is where the body is finally still\nlong enough for the mind to wake up. The cage is the\nloudest invitation the universe knows how to send you.\nMost people refuse the invitation. Most people, even\ninside the cage, find ways to keep the body busy\nenough that the mind never has to wake. They watch\nscreens. They play games. They argue. They drink.\nThey sleep through the hours that were given to them as\ngifts in disguise. I almost did the same. The Filipino\nbrother with the book, the Norwegian with the broken\npen, Mike with his physics, Ray with his quiet trust, the\ncall bell ringing every day with Meme's voice on the","other end — these were the things that kept me from\nsleeping through my own cell.\nI want this book to be one of those things for you, if\nyou are in your cage right now. I want it to be the\nborrowed book that a Filipino brother might hand you\nin your own dark hour. Read it. Mark the parts you want\nto come back to. Argue with the parts you disagree\nwith. Disregard the parts that do not fit you. But do not\nsleep through your cage. Whatever your cage is. The\ncage is the time the mind has finally been given. Use it.\n* * *\nThere is one last thing I want to write here, and then\nI will let you go.\nMy great-grandfather Shehu Muhammad did not\nleave a book. My grandfather Muhammad Ibrahim did\nnot leave a book. My father Mohamed Jabir has not left\na book, though he is alive, and there is still time. I,\nMathihul Akmal Mohamed Jabir, am the first in this\nline to leave a book. That is not a boast. It is a\nresponsibility delivered to me by the bloodline, finally\ncashed in after four generations.","The fire that Shehu Muhammad held in his breath —\nthe fire that the village of Kankeyanodai watched him\nbreathe onto dry leaves — that fire has been travelling.\nIt has been travelling through Muhammad Ibrahim's\nprayer-mark on his forehead, even though Muhammad\nIbrahim could not bring himself to forgive his own son.\nIt has been travelling through my father's quiet, lonely\ntwenty-six years in Saudi and Qatar, working away\nfrom his family so we could grow. It has been travelling\nthrough me — through Sri Lanka, Qatar, Dubai,\nCroatia, Malta, the prison cell, the rock by the sea. And\nnow, finally, the fire has reached a page.\nThe fire is not as bright in me as it was in him. I do\nnot breathe on dry leaves and set them aflame. But the\nfire is the same fire. And in the form a book takes —\nquiet, slow, available to anyone who opens it — the fire\nwill go further than my breath ever could. A book is not\na smaller fire. A book is a fire that travels.\nIf you have read this far, the fire has reached you.\nCarry it. Do whatever you do with it. Light your own\npage. Light your own cage. Light your own small\npurpose. The Kun fayakun is in your hands now.","And one last sentence, because I promised myself in\nthe cell that the book would end with these words, and I\nwant to keep my promise:\nWherever you go, your mind goes with you. The\nquestion is whether you are feeding it.","ACT THREE — THE ROOFTOP\nAUTHOR'S NOTE","59\nOn the Making of This Book\nThis book began on a folded piece of white paper\ninside Cell 13 of Paola Prison in Malta, in the summer\nof 2024. The paper had originally been a Western\nUnion money-request form. The pen was borrowed\nfrom a Norwegian man with a torn sleeve. The first ink\nthat hit the first folded page was a kind of helpless love\nletter to a woman in Dubai that the man writing it did\nnot yet understand was his own waking conscious mind\nin disguise.\nBetween that first folded page and the book you are\nholding now, there have been many drafts, many\nreadings, many conversations with editors and friends,\nmany corrections, many additions, many trimmings. I\nhave written some chapters and rewritten them three or\nfour times. I have left out things I could not yet write\nabout because they touched on an ongoing legal matter,\nand the law has its own time. I have left out things that\ninvolved other people who deserve their privacy. What\nis in the book is what I felt I could honourably set","down.\nI have changed some names. Where I describe\npeople I met in the prison — Rud, Ray, Mike, Ham,\nMario, Tony, and others — I have either changed their\nnames or written them carefully enough that they would\nnot be identifiable to anyone who did not already know\nthem. I have done this out of respect. They did not sign\nup to be in a book. What I learned from them is in here.\nTheir privacy, where possible, is also in here.\nSome names I have kept, because they are mine to\nkeep. The names of my bloodline — Shehu\nMuhammad, Muhammad Ibrahim, Mohamed Jabir,\nMathihul Akmal — are written as they are. The name of\nmy mother's father, Muhammad Ismail, known to the\nvillage as Farooq Bedi, is written as he was. My\nbrother, my sisters, Saraswati, Meme, the Filipino\nbrother who gave me the book that changed everything\n— these names appear because, in different ways, they\neach gave me permission, or because the story would\nnot be true without them.\n* * *","I want to acknowledge the people who helped this\nbook come into being.\nFirst, my parents. My mother, who carried our\nfamily for twenty-six years while my father worked\nabroad, and who never once let me feel I was not loved,\neven on the days I deserved her anger more than her\ntenderness. My father, who handed me ninety rupees at\na bus depot in 1999 and changed the shape of my life in\na single gesture. I am their son. Whatever is good in this\nbook comes from them. Whatever is missing is my own\nfault, not theirs.\nMy brother, who built a rooftop garden while I was\naway, and who, when I was in the cell, said through a\nfriend the one sentence that broke open everything I had\nbeen hiding: he just wants to hear your voice.\nSaraswati, who carried my forty-six-kilo bag across\nMalta, who hustled deposits and lawyers and landlords\non my behalf, who arrived at the prison gate in a plain\noutfit on the day of my release and brought me home.\nShe knows what she did. I cannot repay it. The book is\none of my many small attempts.","Meme, who called me every day from Dubai for 114\ndays, who listened without correcting, who held the\nchannel open while the deeper truth came through. The\nchapter on the awakening songs is mine to her, even\nthough she now knows, as I do, that the songs were\nreally my own conscious mind speaking through her\nname.\nRud and Ray, who inside the prison gave me\nkindness without religion, love without colour. Mike,\nwho taught me physics and English and wrote Kun\nfayakun in a birthday letter without knowing he was\nwriting it, and who did not walk out of those walls. I am\nsorry. I am still grateful.\nThe Filipino brother who handed me Conversations\nwith God, the man whose name I will not write here out\nof respect. He changed the direction of my mind by\nhanding me a book. That is what readers can do for\neach other, if they are willing. He was.\nMy friends in Sri Lanka, in Qatar, in Dubai, in\nMalta, who held space for me before and during and\nafter. Muhammad Inshaf, Sharon, Keith, A, Thomas,\nand others who appear in these pages and many more","who do not. You know who you are. Thank you.\nMy lawyer, Nicholas, who carried the legal weight\nwhile I carried the inner weight. The work each of us\ndid was different. Without yours, I might still be inside,\nand there would be no book.\nAnd finally, the people in places I have only briefly\nmentioned — Archie in Colombo, who taught me\nthrough food and fed me from her own plate, Rosie and\nFreddie in Beragala, Haron sir at the school, the old\nSinghalese woman at the gate, the Italian man in Valle\ndi Torino, the off-duty officer who hugged me at the\nbus stop on the day of release and said do good, be nice\nto people. You did not know you were teachers. You\nwere teachers anyway. The book belongs to you too.\n* * *\nA word on the legal side, because some readers will\nwonder.\nThis book does not discuss the specific accusation\nagainst me, the complainant, the dates, the courtroom\nstatements, the medical records, or any other details of\nthe ongoing legal matter. I have kept all of that private,\non the advice of my lawyer and on the instinct of my","own conscience. What I have shared is my inner\nexperience — the fear, the loneliness, the awakening,\nthe love that taught me about body and mind, the people\nwho kept me human. The case itself is in another room,\nwith the people whose job it is to handle it. This book is\nin a different room.\nI ask any reader who is curious about the legal facts\nto respect their absence. The absence is intentional. The\nbook is more useful without them. The book is about\nwhat happens inside a man in a cage, not what happens\nin a courtroom. Those are two different stories. This is\none of them.\n* * *\nOn the spelling and grammar of these pages: I am a\nTamil speaker first, then a Sinhala speaker, then an\nEnglish speaker. English is my third language. I have\nwritten this book in English because English is the door\nthrough which the most readers can walk. I have done\nmy best with the language. Where the English is\nimperfect, I hope the meaning still arrives. I am\nworking on a Tamil edition for the readers in my home\ncountry, which will come later, in its own time.","If you are a reader for whom English is also a third\nlanguage, and you found the book reachable, that is part\nof why I wrote it the way I wrote it. I wanted the door to\nbe wide. I wanted the man who finds this book in a\nsmall town somewhere, in a hard moment of his life, to\nbe able to read it without needing a dictionary on every\npage. Simplicity is a kind of mercy. I hope the book has\nbeen merciful to you.\n* * *\nAnd now I will close.\nIf you have read this book all the way through, thank\nyou. I do not take it lightly. There are many books in\nthe world, and you chose to spend some of your time\nwith this one. I hope it has been useful. I hope at least\none sentence in it found its way into the part of you that\nneeded it. I hope the rooftop on which I sit now, in\nsome quiet evening of writing, has reached the room in\nwhich you are reading, in some quiet evening of your\nown.\nIf you ever sit on a rock by the sea, or in a chair by a\nwindow, or on the floor of a room in a hard time of your\nlife, and the orange light arrives behind your closed","eyes — I would like you to know that you are not alone.\nThe light has been waiting for you, the way it waited for\nme, and the way it waited for Shehu Muhammad before\nme, and the way it has waited for every quiet seeker\nsince the human being first sat still long enough to\nreceive it. Welcome to the line. You belong here. There\nis more than enough fire to go around.\nWherever you go, your mind goes with you. The\nquestion is whether you are feeding it.\nMathihul Akmal Mohamed Jabir\nMalta, 2026","ACT THREE - THE ROOFTOP\n60\nExplained in Detail\nA continuation of the page I left open\n***\nI kept one page open on purpose.\nAt first, even I did not fully know why.\nI only knew that the book was not\ncompletely closed. I had already told the\nstory of the boy, the father, the mother,\nthe village, the Gulf, Dubai, Malta,\nprison, the first folded paper, the rooftop,\nthe teachings, the witnesses, the two\nbooks, and the speed of stillness. I had\nalready followed the body through its\nhunger, fear, work, love, shame,\nexhaustion, and survival. I had already\nfollowed the mind into the room where it\nfinally stopped running.\nBut still, something inside me said:\nleave one page.\nNot because I wanted the book to\nbecome longer. Some books grow fat","without becoming full. I wanted this page\nbecause there was one final kind of\nunderstanding that had not yet taken its\nplace. It was not another event. It was not\nanother prison memory. It was the\nmeaning behind the meaning, the place\nwhere the scattered signs began to stand\nbeside each other.\nI need to say this carefully before I go\nfurther.\nI am not giving tafsir. I am not\nclaiming to explain the Qur'an with\nauthority. I did not sit in a school for\nyears and become a scholar. I do not stand\nabove the Book. I stand below it, as one\nhuman being who was broken, stopped,\naccused, counted, watched, and forced\ninto stillness until certain verses began to\nenter me differently.\nSo what I am giving here is testimony.\nThis is how some meanings touched my\nlife. The old readings of the scholars may\nstand where they stand. I am not trying to\nremove them. I am only placing beside","them the wound through which I came to\nunderstand.\nThere is a difference between\nexplaining a holy text to others and telling\nthe truth about how that text explained\nyou to yourself. This chapter is the second\none.\nSurah Fussilat - Explained in Detail\nThe word that kept coming back to me\nwas Fussilat - explained in detail.\nWhen I first heard that meaning, I did\nnot hear only the title of a surah. I heard\nthe method of my own life. Nothing\nimportant in my life had been explained\nto me in one sentence. It had been\nexplained through years. It had been\nexplained through repeated signs. It had\nbeen explained through what I lost, what I\nmisunderstood, what I chased, what I\nfeared, and what I finally sat still enough\nto see.\nA translation can give you information.\nA lecture can give you structure. But life","explains with pressure. Prison explained\nfreedom. Waiting explained time.\nHumiliation explained the ego.\nAccusation explained the name. A locked\nroom explained the difference between\nthe body and the one inside the body. A\nmother carrying eight children explained\nwhat work is. A father living twenty-six\nyears away explained what sacrifice is. A\nbroken pen explained how a book can\nbegin when the world thinks the man is\nfinished.\nThis is why Surah Fussilat touched me\nso strongly. It speaks of a message made\nclear, but human beings do not always\nreceive clarity when it first arrives.\nSometimes the explanation is in front of\nus and we still turn away. Sometimes the\nverse is in the mouth before it is in the\nheart. Sometimes the sign is standing\nbeside us for years before we recognise it\nas a sign.\nIn that surah, the heavens and earth are\nnot dead objects. They respond to the","command of their Lord. The nearest\nheaven is adorned with lamps and\nguarded. When I thought about that, I\nremembered the lamp and the oil, the fire\nin the family story, the sky above the\nprison window, and the small human\nbeing beneath it trying to understand why\nhis life had become so detailed in pain.\nI do not write this as a scholar. I write\nit as a man who began to see that the outer\nsky and the inner sky were both full of\nsigns. The stars above the world are\nlamps. But sometimes the memories\ninside a person are also lamps. A father\nleaving. A mother cooking. A grandfather\ncoughing at three in the morning. A boy\nunder a mango tree. A prisoner looking at\na narrow window. Each one gives a little\nlight, but only when the body stops\nrunning enough for the mind to see.\nSurah Fussilat also speaks of the\nhuman being's own body becoming a\nwitness. Hearing, sight, and skin are not\nonly things we use; they are things that","will speak. This entered me in a different\nway after prison. I had already felt my\nbody watched by others: officers,\ncameras, papers, dates, files, fingerprints,\nphotographs, and courtrooms. But deeper\nthan all that, I began to understand that\nthe body itself had been recording. The\nskin remembers fear. The ear remembers\nkeys. The eye remembers a small piece of\nsky. The chest remembers shame. The\nbody is not only the place where life\nhappens. It is also a witness to what the\nsoul refused to understand.\nThat frightened me at first. Then it\nsoftened me. Because if the body is a\nwitness, then I cannot keep lying to\nmyself. I cannot say I was peaceful when\nmy chest knew revenge was burning. I\ncannot say I was awake when my habits\nshowed sleep. I cannot say I loved truth\nwhile feeding the body more than the\nmind. The body testifies before the tongue\nbecomes honest.","The surah also returns the knowledge\nof the Hour to God. No soul owns the\nfinal timing. No human being controls the\nlast opening or the last closing. This\nhumbled me. In my case, I wanted every\nanswer immediately. I wanted every\nmisunderstanding corrected in one day. I\nwanted the world to see the full truth\nbecause I could feel the truth inside me.\nBut not every truth opens when the\ninjured person demands it. Some truth has\nan appointed time. Some explanation is\nstill travelling. Some doors open only\nafter the heart has stopped trying to break\nthem with its fists.\nThis does not mean I abandon justice.\nIt does not mean I accept lies. It means I\ndo not hand my soul to timing I cannot\ncontrol. I can speak clearly. I can defend\nmy name. I can prepare. I can wait. And\nwhile I wait, I can decide what kind of\nhuman being the waiting will produce.\nSurah Fussilat says, in meaning, that\ngood and evil are not equal, and that harm","should be met with what is better. This is\neasy to admire when the wound is small.\nIt becomes heavy when the wound enters\nyour name, your freedom, your family,\nyour sleep, your body, and your future.\nRepel with what is better does not\nmean become weak. It does not mean\nsurrender to falsehood. It does not mean\nlet people harm you and call it spirituality.\nIt means do not let the harm that came to\nyou become the shape of your soul. It\nmeans defend truth without becoming\nwhat wounded you. It means refuse to let\nanother person's darkness become your\npermanent language.\nThis is where I am still learning. Some\ndays I fail. Some days the chest becomes\nhot. Some days the thoughts move fast.\nThen I ask myself: what am I feeding\nnow? Am I feeding the wound, or am I\nfeeding the witness?\nThe surah also points to signs in the\nhorizons and within ourselves. That line\nstayed with me because this whole book","is built from those two directions. The\nhorizon: sea, sky, road, boat, prison wall,\nairport, village, Malta, Qatar, Dubai. The\nwithin: shame, hunger, desire, fear,\npatience, ego, memory, breath, the\nwatcher. The Qur'an did not ask me to\nchoose between outer signs and inner\nsigns. It asked me to read both.\nScience is also reading the horizon.\nMedicine reads the body. Psychology\nreads behaviour. Physics reads\nmovement. The seeker reads stillness. If\nthey are honest, these are not enemies.\nThey are different readers in the same\nlibrary. The mistake is not reading\nscience. The mistake is reading science\nwithout wonder. The mistake is not loving\nscripture. The mistake is loving scripture\nwhile refusing to read the signs God\nplaced in the body, the sky, the wound,\nand the world.\nIn Surah Fussilat, the sun and moon are\nnot objects of worship. They are signs. Do\nnot bow to the things that shine; bow to","the One who made them shine. That\nbecame very personal to me. I had bowed,\nin my own way, to things that were not\nGod: relationship, body, attention,\napproval, movement, being understood,\nbeing desired, being seen. None of them\nwere evil by themselves, but when the\nsoul bows to them, the order breaks. The\nsign becomes an idol when we forget the\nOne it points to.\nSo the surah became a mirror. It did not\nonly tell me about people long ago. It told\nme about me. The one who asks for good,\nthen despairs when harm touches him.\nThe one who receives mercy and says,\nperhaps this is mine. The one who hears\nbut does not listen. The one whose body\nknows more truth than his mouth is ready\nto say. The one who must be explained to\nhimself in detail.\nSurah Ar-Rahman - The Mercy That Repeats\nAnd then there was Surah Ar-Rahman.\nI have called it my soul surah because\nit does something to the ego that ordinary","advice cannot do. It does not argue like a\nhuman being argues. It repeats. It brings\nthe human being again and again to the\nsame question: which of the favours of\nyour Lord will you deny?\nSome people hear repetition and\nbecome bored. But the ego needs\nrepetition because the ego forgets quickly.\nA person can be fed in the morning and\ncomplain by noon. A person can breathe\nwithout paying, see without paying, think\nwithout paying, be loved without paying,\nbe forgiven without paying, and still\nbehave like life has given him nothing. So\nmercy repeats. It knocks again. It asks\nagain. It does not ask because God does\nnot know the answer. It asks because the\nhuman being does not know himself.\nSurah Ar-Rahman begins with mercy\nbefore anything else. The Most Merciful.\nThen the teaching of the Qur'an. Then the\ncreation of the human being. Then\nexpression. This order touched me deeply.\nBefore my body, there was mercy. Before","my speech, there was teaching. Before my\nexplanation, there was a gift I did not\ncreate.\nA human being thinks he speaks\nbecause he owns language. But\nexpression is a mercy. The ability to say I\nam hurt, I am sorry, I remember, I\nforgive, I do not understand, I am trying -\nall of that is not small. Prison showed me\nhow precious expression is. A pen\nbecomes sacred when your mouth cannot\nreach the people you love. A page\nbecomes a second breathing. A sentence\nbecomes proof that there is still someone\ninside.\nThen the surah moves to order. The sun\nand the moon move by measure. The sky\nis raised. The balance is set. Do not\nviolate the balance. Establish weight with\njustice. Do not make the scale deficient.\nThis is not only about markets, money,\nand measurement. It is also about the\ninner scale.","I had lived with imbalance. Body more\nthan mind. Desire more than wisdom.\nMovement more than stillness. Reaction\nmore than witness. Giving sometimes\nmixed with expectation. Love sometimes\nmixed with hunger. Faith sometimes\nmixed with habit. The scale inside me had\nnot always been straight. Surah\nAr-Rahman did not accuse me with\ncruelty. It showed me the order of\ncreation and then let me see my disorder\nagainst it.\nWhen the sun and moon keep their\nmeasure, when the sea keeps its boundary,\nwhen the seed becomes fruit in its time,\nwhen the body knows how to heal a cut\nwithout my instruction, then why does the\nhuman being insist on living without\nbalance? Why do we disturb our own\nscale and then blame the world when the\nheart becomes heavy?\nThe earth in that surah is prepared for\nliving beings. Fruit, grain, fragrance,\nprovision. The surah keeps placing mercy","in front of the reader until denial becomes\nembarrassing. It is as if the verse is\nsaying: look at how much was prepared\nbefore you arrived. You came late to a\ntable already set. And still you complain\nas if nothing was given.\nThis changed how I saw my mother.\nHer kitchen was also a small earth\nprepared for us. Rice, tea, curry, bread,\nworry, washing, shouting, softness,\ndiscipline, prayer. Every day she set a\ntable in one form or another. We did not\ncall it mercy because it looked ordinary.\nBut most mercy looks ordinary until you\nlose it.\nThe surah speaks of the human created\nfrom clay, and the jinn from smokeless\nfire. Clay and fire. Body and force.\nWeight and movement. Form and energy.\nI thought of my own title: mind vs body.\nThe body is clay-like: heavy, hungry,\ntired, wanting comfort, wanting touch,\nwanting food, wanting sleep. The mind\ncan be fire-like: fast, invisible, restless,","burning, travelling farther than the body\ncan go. But neither clay nor fire is safe\nwithout mercy. Clay without spirit\nbecomes only weight. Fire without\nbalance becomes destruction.\nThen there are the two seas meeting,\nwith a barrier between them. They touch,\nbut they do not overrun each other. That\nimage followed me into many parts of my\nlife. Man and woman. Body and mind.\nLove and boundary. Faith and science.\nSpeech and silence. Sri Lanka and Malta.\nPast and future. They can meet without\ndestroying each other if there is a\nboundary placed by wisdom.\nMuch of my pain came when\nboundaries were confused. What I called\nlove sometimes crossed into need. What I\ncalled care sometimes carried expectation.\nWhat I called trust sometimes ignored\nwarning. What I called silence sometimes\nbecame fear. Surah Ar-Rahman reminded\nme that even the seas need a barrier. Not\nbecause they hate each other, but because","mercy sometimes protects by separating.\nFrom the seas come pearl and coral.\nHidden beauty. Pressure and depth\nproducing something small and precious.\nThat too became part of my\nunderstanding. A pearl does not form in\ncomfort. Coral does not appear in a dry\nplace. Some beauty comes from pressure,\nsalt, depth, and time. I do not say pain is\nbeautiful. I say God can bring beauty\nfrom places pain tried to own.\nThe repeated question of Ar-Rahman\nbecame a discipline for my mind. Which\nfavour will you deny? The breath? The\nmother? The father? The teacher? The\nstranger? The pen? The court date that\ngave more time? The prison wall that\nstopped the body? The friend who\nlistened? The food that arrived? The\nmistake that taught you? The collapse that\nmade you ask who is inside?\nIf I am honest, I denied many favours\nwhile standing inside them. I denied the\nfavour of time by wasting it. I denied the","favour of the body by feeding it badly. I\ndenied the favour of love by mixing it\nwith fear. I denied the favour of faith by\ncarrying sound without meaning. I denied\nthe favour of stillness until stillness had to\nbe forced on me.\nBut mercy did not stop asking. That is\nwhy I love the repetition. It is not only\npoetry. It is treatment. It returns to the\nsame wound until the ego becomes tired\nof pretending. It says: look again. Count\nagain. Admit again. You are living inside\ngifts you did not create.\nThe Two Surahs Inside One Life\nWhen I place Surah Fussilat and Surah\nAr-Rahman beside each other, I see two\nmovements that complete the ending of\nthis book.\nFussilat says: the signs will be\nexplained in detail. Look at the horizons.\nLook within yourselves. Let the body\ntestify. Do not worship the shining thing.\nStand firm after saying your Lord is\nAllah. Meet evil without letting evil","become you.\nAr-Rahman says: all of this\nexplanation is wrapped in mercy. The\nQur'an is taught by mercy. The human\nbeing is created by mercy. Speech is\nmercy. Balance is mercy. Boundaries are\nmercy. Provision is mercy. Repetition is\nmercy. Even the question that breaks the\nego is mercy.\nOne surah explained the detail. The\nother explained the mercy inside the\ndetail.\nThat is what happened to my life. The\ndetails were not random. The boy who\nburned the boat. The boy who made\nparatha before the tsunami. The\nmessenger of ninety rupees. The\ngrandfather on the bicycle. The unknown\nUstad. Musammil Alim and the mango\nleaves. The father in the Gulf. The mother\nholding eight children. Dubai feeding the\nbody. Malta stopping the body. Cell 13.\nThomas and the broken pen. The pigeon.\nThe rooftop. The book. The photographs.","The chapter I left open. All of them\nbecame explanation only when mercy\nallowed me to read them together.\nThis does not mean I understand\neverything. I do not. A man who says he\nunderstands everything has stopped being\nteachable. The final lesson is not that I\nhave become complete. The final lesson is\nthat I have become aware of what is\nincomplete.\nThat is a different kind of mercy.\nA man who thinks he is finished cannot\ngrow. A man who knows he is unfinished\ncan still be taught by a pigeon, a cup of\ncoffee, a verse, a boat, a child, a mother, a\nfather, a stranger, a prison wall, a judge, a\ncleaner, a plant on a rooftop, a page that\nrefuses to close.\nMaybe this is what the page left open\nwas trying to tell me. The book is not\nfinished because the human being is not\nfinished. The chapter arrives, but life\ncontinues after the chapter. The photo\nalbum comes, but the faces continue","inside the blood. The last page closes, but\nthe witness keeps walking.\nSo I do not want to end this book by\npretending I have all answers. I want to\nend it by giving the reader the question\nthat saved me again and again:\nWho is the one inside, still watching?\nWhen you find that one, protect him.\nFeed him. Let him read both books. Let\nhim listen to the sound before the\nexplanation. Let him learn from nature\nwithout losing God. Let him seek God\nwithout fearing truth. Let him honour the\nmother who made the mind, the father\nwho carried the distance, the ancestors\nwho carried the fire, and the pain that\nforced the body to stop long enough for\nthe soul to speak.\nDo not wait for prison to become still.\nDo not wait for collapse to ask who you\nare. Do not wait until the body is locked\nbefore you begin to feed the mind.\nSit. Breathe. Watch. Read the world.\nRead the Book. Read yourself.","The explanation may already be\nhappening.\n***\nO God,\nIf this chapter carries truth, let it reach\nthe heart that needs it.\nIf I have misunderstood, correct me\nwith mercy.\nIf I have spoken too much, teach me\nsilence.\nIf I have hidden too much, teach me\ncourage.\nIf my pain can become light for\nsomeone else, let it become light without\nbecoming pride.\nProtect my mother, who carried more\ndays than anyone counted.\nProtect my father, who gave his years\nacross the sea.\nHave mercy on the ancestors whose\nfire reached me through stories, blood,\ndiscipline, and silence.","Do not let this book increase hatred.\nLet it soften one heart. Let it awaken one\nmind.\nLet it remind one human being that the\nbody is not the whole story, and that even\nin the darkest room, there is still someone\ninside, watching.\nAmeen.","","Great-grandfather from my mother's side - another root that carried the\nstory.","Grandfather Muhammad Ismail - my mother's father, in his younger days.","Grandfather Muhammad Ismail (Farooq) - my mother's father, later in\nlife.","","My father in Qatar, carrying the distance for all of us.","My father at home - the face behind many years of work.","Mother and father together, with a little soul between them.","Umma across years - the same arms, carrying life in different seasons.","Umma in the corridor, where a narrow light carried the house.","Baby Akmal - before memory had words.","The family that made the first world.","The mango tree where the watcher first learned to climb.","The schoolboy before the world became heavy.","A boy in a room, already learning silence.","The classroom where curiosity first touched the bones of life.","The boy who carried the verses before he understood them.","A quiet page, a small desk, and the beginning of questions.","The village road and the name I came from.","The bus to Jailani - the alone trip inside the journey.","The mountain path where the young traveller began listening inward.","The ground of home, where the body learned to stand.","A young man dressing for a future he could not yet see.","Dubai fed the body before the mind knew its hunger.","Qatar days - water, distance, and the body learning the world.","Dubai trained the body through uniform, routine, and service.","The camera taught me how to look before I knew how to speak.","The last day in Dubai, before another door opened.","Learning the traveller inside the journey.","Between leaving and arriving, the face still tried to smile.","First days in Malta - a new cold, a new silence.","Sharjah - the last smile before Malta, before the arrest, before the collapse\nI could not yet see.","Switzerland - one of the countries I crossed before the silence.","Another country, another room, another version of the self.","Snow on the coat, silence on the face.","A northern city, and the traveller still searching for himself.","Rome - old stones watching a changing man.","Cell 13 - the face that learned to smile after darkness.","The witness behind the pages.","The present still carries the faces of the beginning."];